\id JER EN_ULT en_English_ltr Thu May 25 2023 15:33:11 GMT-0400 (Eastern Daylight Time) tc
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Jeremiah
\toc1 The Book of Jeremiah
\toc2 Jeremiah
\toc3 Jer
\mt1 Jeremiah
\c 1
\p
\v 1 The words of Jeremiah, the son of Hilkiah, from the priests that {are} in Anathoth in the land of Benjamin,
\v 2 which was the word of Yahweh to him in the days of Josiah, the son of Amon, king of Judah, in the thirteenth year of his reign.
\v 3 And it happened in the days of Jehoiakim, the son of Josiah, king of Judah, until the end of the eleventh year of Zedekiah, the son of Josiah, king of Judah, until the exile of Jerusalem in the fifth month.
\p
\v 4 And the word of Yahweh was to me, saying,
\q1 \v 5 “Before I formed you in the belly, I knew you,
\q2 and before you came out from the womb I set you apart;
\q2 I appointed you a prophet to the nations.”
\m
\v 6 Then {I} said, “Alas, Lord Yahweh! Behold, I do not know {how} to speak, for I {am} a youth.”
\q1 \v 7 But Yahweh said to me, “Do not say, ‘I {am} a youth,’ for to all to whom I shall send you, you shall go, and all that I command you, you shall speak.
\v 8 Do not be afraid of their faces, for I {am} with you to rescue you”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\p
\v 9 Then Yahweh stretched out his hand and touched on my mouth. And Yahweh said to me, “Behold, I have placed my words in your mouth.
\v 10 Look, I am appointing you this day over the nations and over the kingdoms, to pluck up and to pull down and to destroy and to throw down, to build and to plant.”
\p
\v 11 And the word of Yahweh was to me, saying, “What do you see, Jeremiah?” And {I} said, “I see an almond branch.”
\v 12 And Yahweh said to me, “You have seen well, for I am watching over my word to do it.”
\p
\v 13 And the word of Yahweh was to me a second {time}, saying, “What do you see?” And {I} said, “I see a blown-on pot and its face {is away} from the face of the north.”
\v 14 And Yahweh said to me, “From the north, disaster will be released on all the inhabitants of the land.
\v 15 For behold me calling to all the clans of the kingdoms of the north”—the declaration of Yahweh. “And they will come, and {each} man will set his throne {at} the entrance of the gates of Jerusalem and against all its surrounding walls and against all the cities of Judah.
\v 16 And I shall speak my judgment {against} them concerning all their evil in which they abandoned me and burned incense to other gods, and worshiped what they made with their own hands.
\v 17 So you yourself shall gird your loins and stand up and say to them all that I myself shall command you! Do not be dismayed from before their faces lest I dismay you before their faces!
\v 18 And I—behold!—I have made you this day a city of fortification and an iron pillar and bronze walls against all the land—against the kings of Judah, against its officials, against its priests, and against the people of the land.
\v 19 And they will fight against you, but they will not prevail against you, for I {will be} with you to rescue you—the declaration of Yahweh.”
\c 2
\p
\v 1 And the word of Yahweh was to me, saying,
\v 2 “Go and proclaim in the ears of Jerusalem, saying, ‘Thus says Yahweh:
\q1 “I have remembered for you the covenant faithfulness of your youth,
\q2 the love of your betrothal {time},
\q1 your going after me in the wilderness,
\q2 in the land not sown.
\q1 \v 3 Israel {was} holy to Yahweh,
\q2 the first of his harvest!
\q1 All those who ate it became guilty;
\q2 disaster came upon them”
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh.’”
\b
\q1 \v 4 Hear the word of Yahweh, house of Jacob,
\q2 and all clans of the house of Israel.
\m
\v 5 Thus says Yahweh,
\q1 “What did your fathers find wrong in me,
\q2 that they went far away from upon me,
\q1 and they went after the empty,
\q2 and became empty?
\q1 \v 6 And they did not say, ‘Where {is} Yahweh,
\q2 who brought us up from the land of Egypt?
\q1 The one who led us in the wilderness,
\q2 in a land of a desert and a pit,
\q1 in a land of dryness and thick darkness,
\q2 in a land—no human passes through it and no man lives there?’
\q1 \v 7 And I brought you into a fruitful land,
\q2 to eat its fruit and its good things.
\q1 But you came and defiled my land,
\q2 and you made my inheritance into an abomination!
\q1 \v 8 The priests did not say,
\q2 ‘Where {is} Yahweh?’
\q1 and the handlers of the law did not know me,
\q2 and the shepherds transgressed against me,
\q1 and the prophets prophesied by Baal
\q2 and walked after {things} not profitable.
\b
\q1 \v 9 Therefore I shall still contend a {legal} case with you“
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 “and with the sons of your sons, I shall contend a {legal} case.
\q1 \v 10 For cross over {to} the coasts of Kittim and look,
\q2 and {to} Kedar send out and pay very close attention,
\q2 and see if there has been {a thing} as this!
\q1 \v 11 Has a nation exchanged {its} gods,
\q2 though they {were} not gods?
\q1 But my people have exchanged their glory
\q2 for {what} does not profit.
\q1 \v 12 Be appalled, heavens, at this,
\q2 and shudder, be very desolate!”
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 13 “For my people have done two evils {to} me:
\q2 They have abandoned the spring of living waters
\q1 to dig out cisterns for themselves,
\q2 broken cisterns that do not hold the water.
\q1 \v 14 {Is} Israel a servant? Is he homeborn {slave}?
\q2 {So} why has he become for plunder?
\q1 \v 15 Young lions roared against him;
\q2 they gave their voice.
\q1 And they made his land into a desolation;
\q2 his cities became ruins without an inhabitant.
\q1 \v 16 Also, the sons of Memphis and Tahpanhes
\q2 have shaved the crown of your head.
\q1 \v 17 Have you not brought this to yourself
\q2 {when} you abandoned Yahweh your God,
\q2 when he led you in the way?
\q1 \v 18 And now, why—to you, to the road of Egypt
\q2 to drink the waters of the Nile?
\q1 And why—to you, to the road of Assyria
\q2 to drink the waters of {the} River?
\q1 \v 19 Your evil will rebuke you,
\q2 and your apostasy will punish you.
\q1 So know and see
\q2 that {it is} evil and bitter
\q1 for you to forsake Yahweh your God,
\q2 and the fear of me {is} not in you.”
\q2 —the declaration of Lord Yahweh of Armies.
\b
\q1 \v 20 “For from long ago I broke your yoke;
\q2 I tore to pieces your fetters.
\q2 But you said, ‘I will not serve!’
\q1 For on every high hill
\q2 and beneath every leafy tree,
\q2 you yourself lie down, prostitute.
\q1 \v 21 And I myself planted you {as} a choice vine,
\q2 all of it of genuine seed.
\q1 And how have you changed toward me
\q2 {into} degenerates of the strange vine?
\q1 \v 22 For even if you clean yourself with natron
\q2 or multiply for yourself lye,
\q1 your iniquity is a stain before my face”
\q2 —the declaration of Lord Yahweh.
\q1 \v 23 “How can you say, ‘I am not defiled;
\q2 I have not walked after the Baals’?
\q1 See your path in the valleys!
\q2 Know what you have done
\q1 —a swift female camel
\q2 entangling her ways,
\q1 \v 24 a wild donkey, taught ones of the wilderness,
\q2 in the desire of its spirit, sniffing at the air!
\q2 {In} her heat, who can turn her away?
\q1 All of the ones seeking her will not tire;
\q2 in her month they will find her.
\q1 \v 25 Restrain your foot from {becoming} bare
\q2 and your throat from thirst!
\q1 But you have said, ‘{It is} hopeless!
\q2 No, for I love strangers
\q2 and after them I shall go!’
\b
\q1 \v 26 Like the shame of a thief when he is found,
\q2 so the house of Israel has been put to shame—
\q1 they, their kings, their princes,
\q2 and their priests and prophets,
\q1 \v 27 the ones who say to the tree, ‘You {are} my father,’
\q2 and to the stone, ‘You gave birth to me.’
\q1 For they have turned {their} back to me
\q2 and not {their} faces.
\q1 But in the time of their trouble they say,
\q2 ‘Arise and save us!’
\q1 \v 28 But where {are} the gods that you made for yourself?
\q2 Let them arise if they {can} save you
\q2 in the time of your trouble,
\q1 for the number of your cities,
\q2 they are {equal to} your gods, Judah!
\b
\q1 \v 29 Why do you complain to me?
\q2 All of you have rebelled against me,”
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 30 “For futility I have struck your sons.
\q2 They would not accept discipline.
\q1 Your sword has devoured your prophets
\q2 like a destructive lion!
\q1 \v 31 You {are} the generation!
\q2 See the word of Yahweh!
\q1 Have I been a wilderness to Israel?
\q2 Or a land of thick darkness?
\q1 Why do my people say, ‘We shall roam free,
\q2 we shall not go to you anymore’?
\q1 \v 32 Will a virgin forget her jewelry,
\q2 a bride, her sashes?
\q1 Yet my people have forgotten me
\q2 days without number!
\q1 \v 33 How well you make your way to seek love,
\q2 so even {to} the wicked women you taught\f + \ft Instead of \fqa you taught\fqa* the Hebrew text has \fqa I taught.\fqa* Most read this as \fqa I taught.\fqa*\f* your ways.
\q1 \v 34 Also on your skirts has been found
\q2 the blood of the lives of the poor innocent ones.
\q2 They were not discovered in burglary.
\q1 But despite all these,
\q2 \v 35 you also say, ‘But I am innocent;
\q2 surely his nose has turned away from me.’
\q1 Behold me judging you,
\q2 because you say, ‘I have not sinned.’
\q1 \v 36 Why do you treat very lightly
\q2 to change your ways?
\q1 You will also be shamed from Egypt,
\q2 just as you were shamed from Assyria.
\q1 \v 37 You will also go out from this {place},
\q2 and your hands {will be} on your head,
\q1 for Yahweh has rejected the ones you trusted,
\q2 and you will not be successful by them.”
\c 3
\q1 \v 1 Saying, “If a man sends away his wife
\q2 and she goes from him and becomes another man’s,
\q1 will he return to her again?
\q2 Would that land not be greatly polluted?
\q1 And you have been a prostitute {with} many lovers,
\q2 and would {you} return to me?”
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 2 “Lift up your eyes to the barren heights and see!
\q2 Where have you not been ravished?
\q1 By the roads you sat for them,
\q2 as an Arab in the wilderness.
\q1 And you have polluted the land
\q2 with your prostitution and with your evil.
\q1 \v 3 So the showers have been withheld
\q2 and the late rains have not been;
\q1 yet you have the forehead of a prostitute woman;
\q2 you refuse to be embarrassed.
\q1 \v 4 Have you not just now called to me:\f + \ft The MT has \fqa I have spoken\fqa* but other manuscripts and modern versions have \fqa you called.\fqa*\f*
\q2 ‘My father! You {are} a close friend of my youth!
\q1 \v 5 Will he keep forever?
\q2 Or will he keep watch to eternity?’
\q1 Behold, you have spoken, \f + \ft The MT has \fqa I have spoken\fqa* but ancient manuscripts and modern versions have \fqa you have spoken.\fqa*\f*
\q2 but you have done the evils as you can!”
\m
\v 6 Then Yahweh said to me in the days of Josiah the king, “Do you see what apostate Israel has done? She herself has walked up every high hill and to underneath every leafy tree, and she has been a prostitute there.
\v 7 And {I} said, ‘After her doing all these, she will return to me,’ but she did not return. Then her treacherous sister Judah saw.
\v 8 So I saw that for the very reason that apostate Israel had committed adultery, I had sent her away and had given a scroll of her divorce to her. But her sister Judah, acting treacherously, did not fear, and she went out, and she herself also prostituted.
\v 9 And her prostitution was from thoughtlessness. And she defiled the land and committed adultery {with} the stone and the tree.
\v 10 And even in all of this, her treacherous sister Judah has not returned to me with all her heart, but only in deceit”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh said to me, “Apostate Israel has shown her spirit to be more righteous than treacherously acting Judah!
\v 12 Go and proclaim these words toward the north and say,
\q1 ‘Return, apostate Israel!’—the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 ‘I shall not cause my face to fall against you.
\q1 Since I {am} faithful’—the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 ‘I shall not keep forever.
\q1 \v 13 Only, know your iniquity,
\q2 that you have transgressed against Yahweh your God,
\q1 and you have spread your ways to strangers
\q2 under every leafy tree,
\q1 and to my voice you did not listen’”
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh.
\m
\v 14 “Turn back, apostatizing sons!”—the declaration of Yahweh—“for I myself am your husband, and I shall take you, one from a city and two from a clan, and I shall bring you {to} Zion!
\v 15 Then I shall give you shepherds according to my heart, and they will shepherd you {with} knowledge and insight.
\v 16 And it will happen that you will increase and be fruitful in the land in those days”—the declaration of Yahweh—“they will no longer say, ‘The Box of the Covenant of Yahweh!’ And it will not come up in heart, and they will not remember it. And they will not miss {it}, and another will not be made.
\v 17 In that time they will call Jerusalem ‘the throne of Yahweh,’ and all the nations will gather to her—to the name of Yahweh, to Jerusalem. And they will no longer walk after the stubbornness of their evil heart.
\v 18 In those days, the house of Judah will walk with the house of Israel. And they will come together from the land of the north to the land that I caused your fathers to inherit.
\p
\v 19 And I myself said,
\q1 ‘How I would set you among sons
\q2 and give to you a pleasant land,
\q2 the most beautiful heritage among the nations!’
\q1 And {I} said, ‘You shall call me “my father”’
\q2 and from after me, you shall not turn away.
\q1 \v 20 Surely, {like} a woman dealing treacherously with her companion,
\q2 so you have dealt treacherously with me, house of Israel”
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh.
\b
\q1 \v 21 A voice is heard on the bare heights,
\q2 weeping pleas of the sons of Israel!
\q1 For they have made their ways crooked;
\q2 they have forgotten Yahweh their God.
\b
\q1 \v 22 “Turn back, apostatizing sons!
\q2 I will heal your apostasies!”
\q1 “Behold! We come to you,
\q2 for you {are} Yahweh our God!
\b
\q1 \v 23 Truly in vain from the hills,
\q2 the commotion {on} the mountains;
\q1 truly in Yahweh our God
\q2 {is} the salvation of Israel.
\q1 \v 24 Yet from our youth the shame has consumed
\q2 the toil of our fathers—
\q1 their flock and their cattle,
\q2 their sons and their daughters!
\q1 \v 25 Let us lie down in our shame,
\q2 and let our humiliation cover us,
\q1 for against Yahweh our God we have sinned!
\q2 Ourselves and our fathers,
\q1 from our youth and to this day,
\q2 and we have not listened to the voice of Yahweh our God!”
\c 4
\q1 \v 1 “If you return, Israel”—the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 “to me you should return.
\q1 And if you remove your detestable things from before my face
\q2 and do not wander,
\q1 \v 2 and you swear, ‘{As} Yahweh {is} alive’
\q2 in truth, in justice, and in righteousness,
\q1 then the nations will bless themselves in him,
\q2 and in him they will boast.”
\m
\v 3 For thus says Yahweh to the man of Judah and to Jerusalem:
\q1 “Break up your unplowed ground,
\q2 and do not sow into thorns.
\q1 \v 4 Be circumcised to Yahweh,
\q2 and remove the foreskins of your heart,
\q2 man of Judah and inhabitants of Jerusalem,
\q1 lest my heat go forth like fire,
\q2 and burn, and there is no one to extinguish
\q2 from the face of the evil of your deeds.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Declare in Judah, and in Jerusalem announce, and say,
\q2 ‘Blow the horn in the land!’
\q1 Call out fully and say,
\q2 ‘Assemble, and let us go
\q2 into the cities of fortification.’
\q1 \v 6 Lift up a banner toward Zion,
\q2 and bring yourself to safety; do not stand still!
\q1 For I am bringing disaster from the north
\q2 and a great collapse.
\b
\q1 \v 7 A lion has come up from his thicket,
\q2 and a destroyer of nations has set out.
\q1 He has gone out from his place
\q2 to make your land into a desolation;
\q1 your cities will become ruins,
\q2 without an inhabitant.
\q1 \v 8 Because of this, gird yourselves {with} sackcloth,
\q2 lament and wail!
\q1 For the force of the nose of Yahweh
\q2 has not turned away from us.
\q1 \v 9 And it will happen in that day”—the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 “the heart of the king and the heart of the officials will perish,
\q1 And the priests will be appalled,
\q2 and the prophets will be horrified.”
\m
\v 10 And I said, “Ah, Lord Yahweh, surely deceiving, you have deceived this people and Jerusalem, saying, ‘It will be peace for you.’ Yet the sword will reach as far as the life.”
\p
\v 11 At that time it will be said to this people and to Jerusalem, “A searing wind of the bare hills in the wilderness, the way of the daughter of my people, not to winnow and not to cleanse,
\v 12 a wind more full than these will come for me. Now I myself will also speak judgments {against} them.”
\q1 \v 13 Behold, like clouds, he comes up,
\q2 and his chariots {are} like the storm.
\q1 His horses are faster than eagles.
\q2 Woe to us, for we are devastated!
\v 14 Cleanse your heart from evil, Jerusalem, so that you might be saved.
\q2 Until when will lodge within you your wicked thoughts?
\v 15 For a voice declares from Dan,
\q2 and announces coming disaster from the mount of Ephraim.
\v 16 Report to the nations: “Behold!”
\q2 Announce to Jerusalem,
\q1 “Guards are coming from the distant land,
\q2 and they give their voice against the cities of Judah.
\q1 \v 17 Like the watchmen of a field, they will be all around against her,
\q2 since she has been rebellious {against} me”
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh—
\q1 \v 18 “your way and your deeds
\q2 have done these to you.
\q1 This {is} your evil.
\q2 Indeed {it is} bitter!
\q2 Indeed it reaches as far as your heart!”
\b
\q1 \v 19 My inward parts! My inward parts!
\q2 I writhe in pain {in} the walls of my heart!
\q1 My heart is turbulent for me.
\q2 I shall not be silent,
\q1 for I hear the sound of the horn, my soul,
\q2 an alarm for battle.
\v 20 Collapse upon collapse is called out,
\q2 for all the land is devastated.
\q1 Suddenly my tents are devastated,
\q2 in a moment, my curtains!
\q1 \v 21 Until when will I see the banner,
\q2 will I hear the sound of the horn?
\b
\q1 \v 22 “For my people {are} foolish;
\q2 they do not know me.
\q1 They {are} foolish sons
\q2 and they have no understanding.
\q1 They {are} wise to do evil,
\q2 but do not know to do good.”
\b
\q1 \v 23 I saw the land,
\q2 and behold, {it was} formless and empty,
\q1 and to the heavens,
\q2 and there was not their light.
\q1 \v 24 I saw the mountains.
\q2 And behold, they were quaking,
\q2 and all the hills were shaking themselves.
\q1 \v 25 I saw, and behold, there was not the man,
\q2 and all the birds of the heavens had fled.
\q1 \v 26 I saw, and behold, the orchard {was} the wilderness,
\q2 and all the cities had been pulled down
\q2 before the face of Yahweh, before the face of the fury of his nose.
\m
\v 27 For thus says Yahweh,
\q1 “All the land will become a devastation,
\q2 but complete destruction I shall not make.
\q1 \v 28 Because of this the land will mourn,
\q2 and the heavens will darken from above.
\q1 Because I have spoken my plans and will not regret,
\q2 and I shall not turn away from it.”
\b
\q1 \v 29 From the noise of the horseman and the shooter of the bow
\q2 every city will flee.
\q1 They will go into the thickets
\q2 and ascend among the rocks.
\q1 Every city will be abandoned,
\q2 and there will be no man dwelling in them.
\b
\q1 \v 30 And you, \f + \ft The text has a female \fqa you\fqa* pronoun, which does not match the male form of \fqa devastated.\fqa* Many modern versions have \fqa What will you do, devastated one?\fqa* despite the mismatch in gender.\f* devastated one, what will you do?
\q2 Although you dress {in} scarlet,
\q2 although you adorn yourself {with} jewelry of gold,
\q1 although you enlarge your eyes with eye paint,
\q2 you beautify yourself for futility.
\q1 The ones lusting reject you;
\q2 they seek your life.
\b
\q1 \v 31 For I hear a sound like a woman in labor,
\q2 distress like a woman birthing her firstborn,
\q1 the sound of the daughter of Zion;
\q2 she gasps for breath.
\q1 She spreads out her hands,
\q2 “Woe now to me,
\q2 for my soul faints {because of} murderers!”
\c 5
\q1 \v 1 “Roam about in the streets of Jerusalem,
\q2 and see now and know,
\q2 and seek in her plazas,
\q1 if you can find a man,
\q2 if there is one who does justice, who seeks truth,
\q2 then I will forgive her.
\q1 \v 2 And if they say, ‘{As} Yahweh {is} alive,’
\q2 surely they swear falsely.”
\b
\q1 \v 3 “Yahweh, {are} not your eyes for honesty?
\q2 You struck them, but they do not tremble.
\q2 You have completely defeated them; they refuse to receive discipline.
\q1 They harden their faces more than rock;
\q2 they refuse to return.”
\q1 \v 4 So I myself said, “They are only ones who are poor.
\q2 They are foolish,
\q1 for they do not know the way of Yahweh,
\q2 the ordinance of their God.
\q1 \v 5 I shall go for myself to the great ones
\q2 and speak {to} them,
\q1 for they themselves know the way of Yahweh,
\q2 the ordinance of their God.”
\q1 But they together broke the yoke;
\q2 they tore to pieces the fetters.
\q1 \v 6 Therefore, a lion from the forest will strike them down;
\q2 a wolf of the deserts will devastate them;
\q1 a leopard watches over their cities;
\q2 all who go out from there will be torn apart,
\q1 for their transgressions increase;
\q2 their apostasies are numerous.
\b
\q1 \v 7 “How, for this, could I forgive you?
\q2 Your sons have abandoned me
\q2 and have sworn by not-gods.
\q1 When I satisfied them,
\q2 they committed adultery
\q2 and assembled themselves {to} the house of the prostitute.
\q1 \v 8 They were well-fed, lusty stallions. \f + \ft The meaning of the Hebrew for \fqa well-fed, lusty\fqa* is uncertain.\f*
\q2 {Each} man neighing to the wife of his neighbor.
\q1 \v 9 Should I not punish because of these?”
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 “Or even against a nation that {is} like this,
\q2 should I not avenge myself?
\b
\q1 \v 10 Go up onto her terraces \f + \ft The meaning of this word is uncertain\f* and destroy!
\q2 But do not bring complete destruction.
\q1 Remove her vines,
\q2 for they {are} not of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 11 For betraying me, they have betrayed me,
\q2 the house of Israel and the house of Judah,”
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh.
\b
\q1 \v 12 They have deceived about Yahweh,
\q2 and they said, “He {is} not,
\q1 and no evil will come upon us,
\q2 and we will not see sword or famine.
\q1 \v 13 And the prophets are for wind,
\q2 and the word is not in them;
\q2 thus it will be done to them.”
\m
\v 14 Therefore, thus says Yahweh the God of Armies:
\q1 “Because you have spoken this word,
\q2 behold me placing my word in your mouth for a fire,
\q2 and this people {will be} wood, and it will consume them.
\q1 \v 15 Behold! I shall bring a nation against you from far away, house of Israel”
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh—
\q1 “it {is} a lasting nation; it {is} a nation from ancient times!
\q2 A nation whose tongue you do not know,
\q2 and you will not understand what they speak.
\q1 \v 16 Its quiver {is} like an open grave.
\q2 All of them {are} warriors.
\q1 \v 17 And it will consume your harvest and your bread.
\q2 They will consume your sons and your daughters.
\q1 It will consume your flock and your cattle;
\q2 It will consume your vine and your fig tree.
\q1 It will beat down with a sword
\q2 your cities of fortification that you trusted in.
\m
\v 18 But even in those days”—the declaration of Yahweh—“I shall not make of you a complete end.
\v 19 And it will happen when you say, ‘Why has Yahweh our God done all these things to us?’ then you shall say to them, ‘Just as you abandoned me and served gods of a foreigner in your land, so you will serve strangers in a land not yours.’
\q1 \v 20 Declare this to the house of Jacob
\q2 and announce it in Judah, saying:
\q1 \v 21 ‘Hear this, please, foolish people and without heart;
\q2 they have eyes but do not see;
\q2 they have ears but do not hear.
\q1 \v 22 Should you not fear me?”—the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 “Or should you not tremble from my face?
\q1 For I have placed sand {as} a border for the sea,
\q2 an eternal decree, and it does not cross over it,
\q1 and {if} it rises and falls, still it would not prevail,
\q2 and {if} its waves roar, still they do not cross over it.
\q1 \v 23 But this people has a stubborn and rebellious heart.
\q2 They have turned aside and walked away.
\q1 \v 24 And they do not say in their hearts,
\q2 ‘Let us now fear Yahweh our God,
\q1 the one who gives the rain—both the early rain and the late rain—in its season,
\q2 he keeps the appointed weeks of the harvest for us.’
\q1 \v 25 Your iniquities turned have away these {things},
\q2 and your sins have kept back from you the good.
\b
\q1 \v 26 For wicked ones are found among my people;
\q2 he watches like fowlers lying in wait; \f + \ft The meaning of the word for \fqa lying in wait\fqa* is uncertain.\f*
\q2 they set a snare; they capture people.
\q1 \v 27 Like a cage full of birds,
\q2 thus their houses {are} full of deceit,
\q1 thus they grow large and become rich.
\q2 \v 28 They are fat; they are sleek.\f + \ft The meaning of the word for\fqa they are sleek\fqa* is uncertain. The Hebrew word only occurs once in Scripture.\f*
\q2 They also trespass {with} acts of evil.
\q1 They do not plead the legal cause—
\q2 the legal cause of the fatherless one,
\q1 yet they prosper, but the justice of the needy ones—
\q2 they have not given justice.
\q1 \v 29 Why, on account of these things should I not punish?”
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh—
\q1 “against a nation that {is} like this
\q2 should my soul not avenge itself?
\b
\q1 \v 30 Desolation and horror
\q2 has happened in the land.
\q1 \v 31 The prophets prophesy with deceit,
\q2 and the priests rule with their hands,
\q1 and my people love it so,
\q2 but what will you do in the end?
\c 6
\p
\v 1 “Bring yourselves to safety, sons of Benjamin,
\q2 from the midst of Jerusalem!
\q1 And in Tekoa blast a horn!
\q2 And over Beth Haccherem lift up an uplifting!
\q1 For evil looks down from the north,
\q2 and a great collapse.
\q1 \v 2 I liken\f + \ft The Hebrew word for \fqa I liken\fqa* could also be read as \fqa I silence\fqa* or \fqa I destroy.\fqa* The meaning of the word is uncertain.\f* the daughter of Zion
\q2 {to} the beautiful and delicate woman.
\q1 \v 3 To her shepherds and their flocks will go;
\q2 they will pitch tents against her all around;
\q2 they shall pasture each {in} his place.”
\b
\q1 \v 4 “Consecrate {yourselves for} war against her!
\q2 Arise and let us go up at noon!
\q1 Woe to us, for the day faces away,
\q2 for the shadows of evening are lengthening.
\q1 \v 5 Arise and let us attack at night
\q2 and destroy her palaces.”
\m
\v 6 For thus says Yahweh of Armies:
\q1 “Cut a tree,
\q2 and heap up siegeworks against Jerusalem!
\q1 She {is} the city he left behind;
\q2 \f + \ft The meaning of the Hebrew word for \fqa left behind\fqa* in this context is unclear.\f* all of her {is} oppression in her midst.
\q1 \v 7 Like a well keeps its waters fresh,
\q2 thus she keeps her evil fresh.
\q1 Violence and oppression are heard in her;
\q2 always before my face {are} sickness and plague.
\q1 \v 8 Let yourself be instructed, Jerusalem,
\q2 lest my soul turns away in disgust from you,
\q1 lest I make you a wasteland,
\q2 a land not inhabited.”
\m
\v 9 Thus says Yahweh of Armies,
\q1 “Gleaning, they will glean as the vine,
\q2 the remnant of Israel.
\q1 Cause your hand to return
\q2 as a grape gatherer over the branches.”
\q1 \v 10 “To whom shall I speak and give warning
\q2 that they may listen?
\q1 Behold! Their ear {is} uncircumcised,
\q2 and they are not able to pay attention!
\q1 Behold! The word of Yahweh has come to them for disgrace;
\q2 they would not delight in it.
\b
\q1 \v 11 Therefore, I am filled with the heat of Yahweh.
\q2 I am tired of holding {it in}.”
\q1 “Pour it out on the children outside
\q2 and on the groups of young men {who are} together.
\q1 For also a husband will be captured with a wife,
\q2 an old person with fullness of days.
\q1 \v 12 And their houses will be turned over to others,
\q2 fields and wives together.
\q1 For I shall stretch out my hand
\q2 against the inhabitants of the land”
\q2 —the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 13 “For from the smallest and to the greatest,
\q2 all of them are greedy {for} dishonest gain.
\q1 And from prophet and to priest,
\q2 each deals deceitfully.
\q1 \v 14 And they have healed the wound of my people lightly,
\q1 saying, ‘Peace, Peace,’ but there is no peace.
\q1 \v 15 Were they ashamed when they did an abomination?
\q2 Indeed to be ashamed, they were not ashamed;
\q2 they did not even know to blush!
\q1 Therefore, they will fall among the fallen ones;
\q2 At the time I punish them, they will stumble,”
\q2 says Yahweh.
\m
\v 16 Thus says Yahweh,
\q1 “Stand by the roads and look,
\q2 and ask for the ancient paths,
\q1 where the good way is;
\q1 and walk in it,
\q2 and find rest for your souls.
\q2 But they said, ‘We shall not walk {in it}.’
\q1 \v 17 And I stationed watchmen over you.
\q2 ‘Pay attention to the sound of the horn!’
\q2 But they said, ‘We shall not pay attention.’
\q1 \v 18 Therefore, hear, you nations!
\q2 And know, assembly,
\q2 what {will be} among them.
\q1 \v 19 Hear, O earth:
\q2 Behold, I am bringing disaster to this people,
\q2 the fruit of their plans.
\q1 For to my words they did not pay attention,
\q2 and my law— they rejected it.
\q1 \v 20 What {is} this to me, frankincense coming from Sheba?
\q2 Or reed perfume from a distant land?
\q1 Your burnt offerings {are} not acceptable,
\q2 and your sacrifices are not pleasing to me.”
\m
\v 21 Therefore, thus says Yahweh,
\q1 “Behold me giving stumbling blocks to this people,
\q2 and they will stumble on them—fathers and sons together.
\q2 A resident and his neighbor will perish.”
\m
\v 22 Thus says Yahweh,
\q1 “Behold, a people is coming
\q2 from the land of the north,
\q1 and a great nation is being woken up
\q2 from the farthest parts of the earth.
\q1 \v 23 They hold bows and spears.
\q2 They {are} cruel and they are not compassionate.
\q1 Their sound roars like the sea,
\q2 and they ride on horses,
\q1 arranged like men for battle
\q2 against you, daughter of Zion.”
\b
\q1 \v 24 “We have heard the reports about them;
\q2 our hands fall limp.
\q1 Distress seizes us,
\q2 anguish as of a woman giving birth.
\q1 \v 25 Do not go out {to} the field,
\q2 and do not walk {on} the road,
\q1 for the sword of the enemy,
\q2 terror from all around.”
\q1 \v 26 Daughter of my people, gird yourself {with} sackcloth
\q2 and roll about in ash;
\q1 make mourning for yourself {as for} an only son
\q2 a lamentation of bitterness,
\q1 for suddenly the destroyer
\q2 will come upon us.
\b
\q1 \v 27 “I have given you {as} a tester among my people, an appraiser,
\q2 so you can know and test their way.
\q1 \v 28 All of them {are} stubborn ones\f + \ft Instead of \fqa stubborn ones\fqa* some manuscripts have \fqa princes.\fqa*\f* who rebel,
\q2 going about {in} gossip
\q1 All of them {are} bronze and iron;
\q2 they act corruptly.
\q1 \v 29 The bellows fiercely blow
\q2 the lead from their fire.\f + \ft Instead of \fqa from their fire\fqa* the Hebrew Qere has \fqa consumed from the fire.\fqa*\f*
\q1 In vain he refines to refine,
\q2 but the evil things are not torn away.
\q1 \v 30 They will be called, ‘rejected silver,’
\q2 for Yahweh has rejected them.”
\c 7
\p
\v 1 The word that was to Jeremiah from Yahweh, saying,
\v 2 “Stand at the gate of the house of Yahweh and proclaim there this word! And you shall say, ‘Hear the word of Yahweh, all of Judah, who enter these gates to bow down to Yahweh.’”
\v 3 Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel, “Make your ways and your deeds good, and I will cause you to dwell in this place.
\v 4 Do not trust for yourself in words of deceit, saying, ‘The temple of Yahweh! The temple of Yahweh! The temple of Yahweh {is} this!’
\v 5 For if making good, you make good your ways and your deeds, if doing, you do justice between a man and between his neighbor,
\v 6 if you do not oppress the stranger, the fatherless one, or the widow, and you do not shed innocent blood in this place and do not walk after other gods to your harm,
\v 7 then I will cause you to dwell in this place, in the land that I gave to your fathers from ancient times and to eternity.
\v 8 Behold! You trust for yourself in words of deceit to no benefit.
\pm
\v 9 Will {you} steal, murder, and commit adultery and swear deceitfully and burn incense to Baal and walk after other gods whom you have not known,
\v 10 and then come and stand before my face in this house which is called by my name and say, ‘We are delivered, in order to do all of these abominations’?
\v 11 Has this house, which is called by my name, become a cave of robbers in your eyes? Behold, I myself have also seen”—the declaration of Yahweh. pm
\v 12 “But go now to my place that {was} in Shiloh, where I made my name dwell at in the beginning, and see what I did to it because of the evil of my people Israel.
\v 13 So now, on account of your doing all of these deeds’—the declaration of Yahweh—‘and {because} I spoke to you rising early and speaking, but you did not listen, and I called you, but you did not answer,
\v 14 therefore, I shall do to the house that is called by my name, which you are trusting in, and to the place that I gave to you and your fathers, as I did to Shiloh.
\v 15 And I shall cast you out from my face just as I have cast out all your brothers, all the seed of Ephraim.’
\pm
\v 16 As for you, do not pray for this people, and do not lift up a cry or a prayer for them, and do not plead with me, for I shall not hear you.
\v 17 Do you not see what they are doing in the cities of Judah and in the streets of Jerusalem?
\v 18 The sons are collecting wood, and the fathers, kindling the fire, and the women are kneading dough to make cakes for the Queen of the Heavens, and they pour out drink offerings to other gods in order to provoke me to anger.
\v 19 Are they provoking me to anger?”—the declaration of Yahweh—“{Is it} not themselves, to the shame of their {own} faces?
\pm
\v 20 Therefore, thus says the Lord Yahweh, ‘Behold, my nose and my wrath will be poured out on this place, on man and on beast, and on the tree of the field and on the fruit of the ground, and it will burn and will not be quenched.’
\pm
\v 21 Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel, ‘Add your burnt offerings to your sacrifices, and eat meat.
\v 22 For I did not speak to your fathers nor did I command them on the day I brought them out from the land of Egypt concerning matters of burnt offering and sacrifice.
\v 23 But I commanded them this word, saying, “Listen to my voice, and I will be your God and you will be my people; and you shall walk in all the ways that I command you so that it may go well for you.”
\v 24 But they did not listen, and they did not incline their ear. And they walked by schemes in the stubbornness of their evil heart, and they went to the back and not to the face.
\v 25 From the day that your fathers went out from the land of Egypt until this day, I have sent all of my servants, the prophets, to you; {each} day rising early and sending.
\v 26 And yet they did not listen to me, and they did not incline their ear, but they stiffened their neck. They did evil more than their fathers.’
\pm
\v 27 So you shall speak all these words to them, but they will not listen to you. And you shall call to them, but they will not answer you.
\v 28 And you shall say to them: ‘This {is} the nation that does not listen to the voice of Yahweh its God and does not receive discipline. Truth has perished and has been cut off from their mouth.
\v 29 Shear your consecration {hair} and throw {it} away, and lift up on the bare heights a lamentation. For Yahweh has rejected and abandoned the generation of his fury.
\v 30 For the sons of Judah have done evil in my eyes”—the declaration of Yahweh—“they have set their detestable things in the house over which my name is called, to defile it.
\v 31 And they have built the high places of Topheth, which {is} in the Valley of the Son of Hinnom, to burn their sons and their daughters in the fire—which I did not command, and it did not come into my heart.
\v 32 Therefore, behold, days are coming”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and it will no longer be called Topheth or the Valley of the Son of Hinnom, but rather, the Valley of Slaughter. And they will bury in Topheth because there is no room.
\v 33 And the corpses of this people will be for food for the bird of the skies and for the beast of the earth, and there will be none to frighten {them}.
\v 34 And I shall cause to cease from the cities of Judah and from the streets of Jerusalem: the sound of joy and the sound of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride. For the land will be desolate.
\pmc
\c 8
\p
\v 1 At that time”—the declaration of Yahweh—“they will bring out the bones of the kings of Judah and the bones of its rulers and the bones of the priests and the bones of the prophets and the bones of the dwellers of Jerusalem from their graves.
\v 2 And they will spread them out to the sun and to the moon and to all of the host of the heavens, which they loved and which they served, and that they walked after, and that they sought, and that they bowed down to. They will not be gathered, and they will not be buried. They will be for dung on the face of the land.
\v 3 And they will choose death from life for all of the remnant who are remaining from this evil family, in all of the places of those who {are} remaining, where I drove them”—the declaration of Yahweh of Armies.
\p
\v 4 “And I will say to them, ‘Thus says Yahweh, “Will they fall and not rise? If one turns away, then does he not {then} return?
\q1 \v 5 Why {then has} this people, Jerusalem, turned away, a continuous apostasy?
\q1 They grasp upon deceit and refuse to turn.
\q1 \v 6 I paid attention and listened; they did not speak rightly. There was not a man regretting over his wickedness, saying,
\q1 ‘What have I done?’ All of them turn in their courses,
\q1 like a stallion plunging into the battle.
\q1 \v 7 Even the stork in the heavens knows its appointed times,
\q1 and a turtledove, and a swift one, and a crane keep the time of their coming, but my people do not know the rule of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 8 How can you say, ‘We are wise, and the law of Yahweh {is} with us’?
\q1 Indeed, behold! The stylus of the deceitful scribes has made {it} a deceitful thing.
\q1 \v 9 The wise ones will be put to shame; they will be dismayed and will be captured.
\q1 Behold! They have rejected the word of Yahweh, so what wisdom do they have?
\q1 \v 10 Therefore, I will give their wives to others, their fields, to those possessing them,
\q1 because from the least to the greatest, everyone is greedy for dishonest gain. From the prophet to the priest, everyone deals deceitfully.
\q1 \v 11 For they have healed the brokenness of the daughter of my people lightly,
\q1 saying, ‘Peace, Peace,’ and there is no peace.
\q1 \v 12 Were they ashamed when they did an abomination? No, being ashamed, they were not ashamed; they did not know {how} to be humiliated! Therefore, they will fall among the fallen, they will stumble in the time of their punishment,” says Yahweh.
\q1 \v 13 “‘Gathering, I will gather them”—the declaration of Yahweh—“there are not grapes on the vine, and there are not figs on the fig tree, and the leaf {is} withered, and {the things} I gave to them, they have passed away {from} them.”
\q1 \v 14 “Why {are} we sitting? Be gathered, and let us go to the cities of fortification, and let us perish there,
\q1 for Yahweh our God will cause us to perish and has given us water of poison to drink because we have sinned against Yahweh.
\q1 \v 15 {We were} hoping for peace, but no good {came},
\q1 for a time of healing, and behold, terror.
\v 16 The snorting of his horses was heard from Dan. At the sound of the neighing of his mighty ones, the whole land quakes,
\q1 for they have come and consumed the land and its fullness, the city and those dwelling in it.”
\q1 \v 17 “For behold me sending snakes among you, vipers that there is not for them a charm.
\q1 And they will bite you”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 18 Joy of me in sorrow; my heart {is} faint within me.
\q1 \v 19 Behold! The sound of the cry of the daughter of my people from a faraway land!
\q1 “Is Yahweh not in Zion? Is her king no longer in her?”
\q1 “Why have they provoked me to anger with their idols, with vain foreign things?”
\v 20 “The harvest has passed, summer has ended, and we have not been delivered.”
\q1 \v 21 For the brokenness of the daughter of my people, I am broken; I mourn; horror has seized me.
\q1 \v 22 Is there no balm medicine in Gilead? Is there not a healer there?
\q1 Then why does the healing of the daughter of my people not go up?
\c 9
\q1 \v 1 I wish that my head {were} waters, and my eyes {were} a fountain of tears,
\q1 that I might weep day and night for the slain ones of the daughter of my people.
\q1 \v 2 Who will give me a lodging place of travelers in the desert, that I might leave my people and walk away from them?
\q1 For all of them are adulterers, an assembly of ones dealing treacherously!
\q1 \v 3 “And they tread on their tongues, their bow, {for} lies,
\q1 and not for truth they are strong in the land.
\q1 For from evil thing to evil thing they go, and they do not know me,”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 4 Let each one take heed to his neighbor, and do not trust any brother,
\q1 for every brother, deceiving, he deceives, and every neighbor walks {in} slander.
\q1 \v 5 And each one deceives his neighbor, and they do not speak the truth;
\q1 they have taught their tongue to speak deceitful things; they weary themselves committing iniquity.
\q1 \v 6 Your dwelling {is} in the midst of deceit; through deceit they refuse to know me,”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 7 Therefore, thus says Yahweh of Armies, “Behold me refining them and will test them,
\q1 for how shall I act from before the face of the daughter of my people?
\q1 \v 8 Their tongue is a murderous arrow; it speaks a deceitful thing.
\q1 With his mouth he speaks peace with his neighbor, but in his midst he sets ambush of him.
\q1 \v 9 Shall I not punish upon them because of these things”—the declaration of Yahweh—
\q1 “shall I not avenge myself on a nation such as this?”
\q1 \v 10 For the mountains I will take up weeping and wailing, and for the pastures of the wilderness, a lamentation,
\q1 because they are burned up so that {there is} not a man passing through, and they do not hear the sound of cattle,
\q1 from the bird of the heavens and to the beast they have fled; they are gone.
\q1 \v 11 “And I will make Jerusalem to piles {of ruins}, a dwelling-place of jackals,
\q1 and I will make the cities of Judah a desolation without dwellers.”
\q1 \v 12 Who is the man that is wise that he may understand this? And to whom has the mouth of Yahweh spoken, that he may declare it?
\q1 Why does the land perish, burn up like the wilderness so that {there is} not {a man} passing through?
\p
\v 13 And Yahweh said, “Because of their forsaking my law that I set before the face of them, and they do not listen to my voice, and they do not walk in it.
\v 14 But they have walked after the stubbornness of their hearts and after the Baals, which their fathers taught them.
\v 15 Therefore, thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel, ‘Behold me feeding them, this people, wormwood and making them drink water of poison.
\v 16 And I will scatter them among the nations whom they have not known, they nor their fathers, and I will send out the sword after them until I consume them.”
\q1 \v 17 Thus says Yahweh of Armies, “Think about this
\q1 and call for the ones who chant laments, that they may come, and send for those who are skilled, that they may come.
\q1 \v 18 Let them hurry and take up a wailing for us,
\q1 that our eyes may go down {with} tears, and our eyelids flow {with} water.
\q1 \v 19 For the sound of wailing is heard from Zion, ‘How we are devastated.
\q1 We are greatly ashamed, because we have left the land because they have cast down our dwellings.’”
\q1 \v 20 Now hear women, the word of Yahweh, and let your ear receive the word of his mouth,
\q1 and teach your daughters wailing, and each woman, her neighbor, a lament.
\q1 \v 21 For death has come up into our windows, it has entered into our palaces
\q1 to cut off children from outside, young men from the open plazas.
\q1 \v 22 Speak “Thus {is} the declaration of Yahweh, ‘and the corpse of man will fall like dung upon the face of the field,
\q1 and like sheaves after the harvester, and {there is} no one to gather {them}.’”
\q1 \v 23 Thus says Yahweh, “Do not let the wise one boast in his wisdom,
\q1 and do not let the mighty one boast in his strength. Do not let the rich one boast in his riches.
\q1 \v 24 But let him who boasts, boast in this, understanding and knowing me,
\q1 that I {am} Yahweh, the one doing covenant faithfulness, justice and righteousness in the earth, for in these I take pleasure—the declaration of Yahweh.”
\p
\v 25 “Behold, days {are} coming”—the declaration of Yahweh—“when I will punish upon all of the ones being circumcised in foreskin,
\v 26 upon Egypt and upon Judah, and upon Edom and upon the sons of Ammon, and upon Moab and upon all of those being cut off of corner, those dwelling in the desert, for all of the nations {are} uncircumcised, and all of the house of Israel {are} uncircumcised of heart.”
\c 10
\q1 \v 1 Hear the word that Yahweh speaks to you, house of Israel.
\q1 \v 2 Thus says Yahweh, “Do not learn to the way of the nations,
\q1 and do not be dismayed at the signs of the heavens, for the nations are dismayed from them.
\q1 \v 3 For the customs of the peoples are breath,
\q1 for a tree from the forest, he cuts it down, the work of the hands of a craftsman with the tool.
\q1 \v 4 With silver and gold he decorates it; with nails and hammers they fasten it so that it will not totter.
\q1 \v 5 They {are} like scarecrows of a cucumber field, and they do not speak, carrying, they are carried because they do not walk.
\q1 Do not be afraid of them, for they will not do harm, and also there is not good with them.”
\q1 \v 6 There is no one like you, Yahweh. You {are} great, and your name {is} great in power.
\q1 \v 7 Who would not fear you, king of the nations? For it is fitting to you,
\q1 for among all the wise of the nations and among all of their kingdoms there is no one like you.
\q1 \v 8 And in one they are stupid and foolish; the instruction of vain things {is} wood.
\q1 \v 9 Beaten silver is brought from Tarshish, and gold, from Uphaz, the work of a craftsman,
\q1 and the hands of one who refines. Their clothing {is} violet and purple; the work of skilled ones {are} all of them.
\q1 \v 10 But Yahweh {is} the true God, he {is} the living God and eternal king.
\q1 The earth quakes from his anger, and the nations cannot endure his indignation.
\q1 \v 11 You shall say like this to them, “The gods that did not make the heavens and earth
\q1 shall perish from the earth and from under these heavens.”
\q1 \v 12 The maker of the earth by his strength, the establisher of the world by his wisdom, and by his understanding he stretched out the heavens.
\q1 \v 13 At the sound of his uttering, {there is} a tumult of waters in the heavens, and he causes mists to ascend from the end of the earth,
\q1 he makes lightning for the rain, and he brings out wind from his storehouses.
\q1 \v 14 Every man is stupid; without knowledge. Every metalworker is put to shame from his idols,
\q1 for his molten images are falsehood, and {there is} no breath in them.
\q1 \v 15 They are vain, the work of mockery; they will perish at the time of their visitation.
\q1 \v 16 Not like these {is} the portion of Jacob, for he {is} the one who formed all things,
\q1 and Israel is the tribe of his inheritance. Yahweh of Armies {is} his name.
\q1 \v 17 Gather from the land your bundle, you one who dwells in the siege.
\q1 \v 18 For thus says Yahweh, “Behold me slinging the ones who inhabit the land at this time.
\q1 I will cause them distress in order that they will find.”
\q1 \v 19 Woe to me because of my breaking! My wound is severe,
\q1 But I said, “Surely this {is} affliction, and I will bear it.”
\q1 \v 20 My tent is devastated, and all of my cords are ripped apart.
\q1 My sons have gone {from} me, and they are not. There is not one spreading out my tent again, raising up my tent curtains.
\q1 \v 21 For the shepherds are stupid, and they do not seek Yahweh,
\q1 therefore, they have not prospered, and all their flock is scattered.
\q1 \v 22 The sound of a report! Behold, it is coming, and a great commotion from the land of the north
\q1 to make the cities of Judah a desolation, a dwelling place of jackals.
\q1 \v 23 I know, Yahweh, that of a man {is} not his way, and not to a man {is} walking and make firm his steps.
\q1 \v 24 Discipline me, Yahweh, but in justice, not in your anger, lest you diminish me.
\q1 \v 25 Pour out your fury on the nations that do not know you and on the clans that do not call on your name,
\q1 for they have consumed Jacob, and they have consumed him, and they have destroyed him and desolated his habitation.
\c 11
\p
\v 1 The word that was to Jeremiah from Yahweh, saying,
\v 2 “Hear the words of this covenant, and speak them to the men of Judah and to the ones who dwell in Jerusalem,
\v 3 and say to them, ‘Thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel, “Cursed is the man who does not hear the words of this covenant
\v 4 that I commanded your fathers on the day of my bringing them out from the land of Egypt, from the furnace of iron, saying, ‘Listen to my voice and you are to do according to all that I command you, so you will be to me for a people and I will be to you for God,’
\v 5 so that {I may} confirm the oath that I swore to your fathers, to give to them a land flowing of milk and honey, as this day.”’” Then I answered and said, “So be it, Yahweh!”
\p
\v 6 And Yahweh said to me, “Proclaim all these words in the cities of Judah and in the streets of Jerusalem. Say, ‘Listen to the words of this covenant and do them.
\v 7 For warning, I warned your ancestors in the day I brought them up from the land of Egypt and until this day, rising early and admonishing, saying, “Listen to my voice.”
\v 8 Yet they did not listen and they did not incline their ear, but everyone walked in the stubbornness of their evil heart. Therefore I brought upon them all of the words of this covenant that I commanded {them} to do, but they did not do {it}.’”
\p
\v 9 And Yahweh said to me, “A conspiracy has been found among the men of Judah and in the inhabitants of Jerusalem.
\v 10 They have returned to the iniquities of their first fathers, who refused to listen to my words, and they walked after other gods to serve them. The house of Israel and the house of Judah broke my covenant that I cut with their fathers.
\v 11 Therefore, thus says Yahweh, ‘Behold me bringing calamity to them, which they will not be able to go out from it, and though they cry out to me, yet I will not listen to them.
\v 12 Then the cities of Judah and the inhabitants of Jerusalem will go and cry out to the gods {to} whom they {are} offering to them, and saving, they will not save them in the time of their calamity.
\v 13 For {as} the number of your cities are your gods, Judah, and {as} the number of the streets of Jerusalem, you have set up altars to {that} shameful thing, altars to offer to Baal.
\p
\v 14 Therefore, you do not intercede for this people and do not lift up a cry or a prayer on their behalf, for there is not me listening in the time of their calling to me because of their calamity.
\q1 \v 15 What to my beloved in my house, to do her wickedness, the many,
\q1 and the flesh of the holy thing they pass over from you? When evil of you, then you rejoice.’
\q1 \v 16 Yahweh called your name ‘Leafy Olive Tree, Beautiful {and} of Good Fruit.’
\q1 With the sound of a great storm, he will kindle a fire upon her, and its branches will be broken.
\v 17 For Yahweh of Armies, the one who planted you, has spoken calamity against you because of the evil of the house of Israel and the house of Judah which they have done to anger me, offering to Baal.
\p
\v 18 And Yahweh made me know {it}, and I knew {it}; then you showed me their deeds.
\v 19 And I was like a gentle lamb brought to slaughter, and I did not know that they had devised plans against me, “Let us destroy the tree with its fruit! And let us cut him off from the land of the living, that his name will not be remembered any longer.”
\q1 \v 20 But Yahweh of Armies {is} one judging of righteousness, examining kidneys and the heart.
\q1 Let me see your vengeance upon them, for to you I have revealed my cause.
\p
\v 21 Therefore, thus says Yahweh concerning the men of Anathoth who {are} seeking your life, saying, “Do not prophesy in the name of Yahweh, and you will not die by our hand.”
\v 22 Therefore thus says Yahweh of Armies, “Behold me punishing upon them. The young men will die by the sword. Their sons and their daughters will die by famine,
\v 23 and there will not be a remnant to them, for I will bring calamity on the men of Anathoth, {the} year of their visitation.”
\c 12
\q1 \v 1 You {are} righteous, Yahweh, when I plead to you.
\q1 Yet I would speak claims with you. Why does the way of the wicked prosper, {why} are they at ease, all of the ones being treacherous of treachery?
\q1 \v 2 You have planted them; they have also taken root. They grow; they have also produced fruit.
\q1 Near you {are} in their mouth but far away from their kidneys.
\v 3 But you, Yahweh, know me; you see me, and you test my heart toward you.
\q1 Drag them away like sheep for slaughter and set them apart for the day of slaughter!
\q1 \v 4 Until when will the land dry up and the herbage of every field wither from the wickedness of those who dwell in it?
\q1 The beasts and the birds dwindle away because they said, “He will not see our end.”
\q1 \v 5 “If you have run with footmen and they have wearied you, how will you compete with the horses?
\q1 And {if} you {are} trusting in a land of peace, then how will you do in the height of the Jordan?
\q1 \v 6 For even your brothers and your father’s house, even they have betrayed you, even they have called after you fully.
\q1 Do not trust in them, though they speak good things to you.
\q1 \v 7 I have abandoned my house, I have left my inheritance.
\q1 I have given the beloved of my soul into the hand of her enemies.
\q1 \v 8 My inheritance has become to me like a lion in the forest,
\q1 she uttered against me with her voice; therefore, I hate her.
\q1 \v 9 Is my inheritance a speckled bird of prey to me? Are {they} a bird of prey all around against her?
\q1 Go, assemble all of the beast of the field; bring them for food.
\q1 \v 10 Many shepherds have ruined my vineyard; they have tread down my portion;
\q1 they have made my pleasant portion a wilderness of desolation.
\q1 \v 11 He has made it a desolation; it mourns to me;
\q1 desolate, it is made desolate, all the land, because no man lays {it} to heart.
\q1 \v 12 Upon all the bare heights in the wilderness have come the destroyers,
\q1 for the sword of Yahweh {is} consuming from {one} end of the land and to the {other} end of the land;
\q2 there is no peace for any flesh.
\q1 \v 13 They have sown wheat but harvested thorns. They have exhausted themselves, {but} they did not profit.
\q1 Be ashamed of your harvests, because of the burning of the nose of Yahweh.”
\p
\v 14 Thus says Yahweh concerning all my evil neighbors who strike the inheritance that I that I have caused my people Israel to inherit, “Behold me plucking them up from upon their land, and I will pluck up the house of Judah from their midst.
\v 15 And it will happen after I pluck them up, I will turn and have compassion on them and bring them back, a man to his inheritance and a man to his land.
\v 16 And it will happen, if learning, they will learn the ways of my people, to swear by my name ‘As Yahweh lives’ just as they have taught my people to swear by Baal, then they will be built in the midst of my people.
\v 17 But if they do not listen, then I will pluck up that nation, to pluck up and destroy”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\c 13
\p
\v 1 Thus says Yahweh to me, “Go and buy a linen loincloth for yourself and put it around your loins, but do not put it in water.”
\v 2 So I bought a loincloth according to the word of Yahweh, and I put it around my loins.
\v 3 And the word of Yahweh was to me a second time, saying,
\v 4 “Take the loincloth that you bought, that {is} around your loins, and arise, go to Perath\f + \ft Some translate \fqa Perath\fqa* as \fqa the Euphrates\fqa*.\f* and hide it there in a cleft of the rock.”
\v 5 So I went and hid it by Perath, just as Yahweh had commanded me.
\v 6 And it happened, from the end of many days, that Yahweh said to me, “Arise, go to Perath, and take from there the loincloth that I commanded you to hide there.”
\v 7 Then I went to Perath \f + \ft Some versions translate \fqa Perath\fqa* as \fqa the Euphrates\fqa*.\f* and dug, and I took the loincloth from the place that I had hidden it, and behold, the loincloth was ruined; it was not good for anything.
\p
\v 8 And the word of Yahweh was to me, saying,
\v 9 “Thus says Yahweh, ‘Thus I will ruin the majesty of Judah and the great majesty of Jerusalem.
\v 10 This evil people, the ones who refuse to listen to my words, the ones walking in the stubbornness of their heart, and they have walked after other gods to serve them and to bow down to them—let him be like this loincloth that is not good for anything.
\v 11 For as a loincloth clings to the loins of a man, so I have caused the whole house of Israel and the whole house of Judah to cling to me”—the declaration of Yahweh—“to be for me {my} people, and {be} for name, and {be} for praise, and {be} for glory. But they would not listen.
\p
\v 12 Therefore, you shall speak this word to them, ‘Thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel: “Every jar shall be filled with wine.”’ And they will say to you, ‘Knowing, do we not know that every jar will be filled with wine?’
\v 13 Then you shall say to them, ‘Thus says Yahweh: “Behold me filling with drunkenness all of the ones inhabiting this land, and the kings, the ones sitting on David’s throne, and the priests, and the prophets, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem.
\v 14 And I will smash a man against his brother, and the fathers and the sons together”—the declaration of Yahweh—“I will not pity and I will not spare and I will not have compassion so as not to destroy them.”’”
\q1 \v 15 Listen and give ear; do not be proud, for Yahweh has spoken.
\q1 \v 16 Give glory to Yahweh your God before he brings darkness,
\q1 and before he causes your feet to stumble on the mountains at twilight,
\q1 and you wait for light, but he turns it to deep darkness, he turns it to gloom.
\q1 \v 17 But if you will not listen, my soul will weep in secret places because of {your} pride;
\q1 and weeping, she will weep, and my eyes will flow with tears because Yahweh’s flock has been taken captive.
\q1 \v 18 “Say to the king and to the queen mother, ‘Make low, sit,
\q1 for has come down from at your heads the crown of your glory.
\q1 \v 19 The cities of the Negev are shut up, and there is not one opening. All of Judah has been exiled, she has been wholly exiled.’”
\q1 \v 20 Lift up your eyes and see the ones coming from the north.
\q1 Where {is} the flock given to you, your flock of beauty?
\q1 \v 21 What will you say when he appoints over you, and you yourself have taught them, friends over you, to {be} head?
\q1 Will not labor pains seize you like a woman in labor?
\q1 \v 22 And if you say in your heart, “Why have these things come upon me?”
\q1 For the multitude of your iniquity your skirts have been uncovered {and} your heels have been exposed.
\q1 \v 23 Can the Cushite change his skin, or the leopard its spots?
\q1 {Then} also you yourself are able to do good, ones accustomed to do evil.
\q1 \v 24 Therefore, I will scatter them like stubble passing away by the wind of the wilderness.
\q1 \v 25 This {is} your lot, the portion of your measures from me”—the declaration of Yahweh—
\q1 “because you have forgotten me and trusted in a lie.
\q1 \v 26 So I myself shall strip off your skirts over your face, that your shame may be visible.
\q1 \v 27 Your adulteries and your neighings,
\q1 the lewdness of your harlotry on the hills in the fields.
\q1 I have seen your abominations.
\q1 Woe to you, Jerusalem! How long will you not be clean?”
\c 14
\p
\v 1 The word of Yahweh that came to Jeremiah concerning the drought:
\q1 \v 2 “Judah mourns, and her gates languish;
\q1 they mourn for the land,
\q1 and the cry of Jerusalem goes up.
\q1 \v 3 And their noble ones send out their servants for water;
\q1 they go to cisterns; they find no water;
\q1 they return with their vessels empty;
\q1 they are ashamed and confounded,
\q1 and they cover their head.
\q1 \v 4 Because the ground is dismayed since there is not rain in the land,
\q1 the farmers are ashamed; they cover their head.
\q1 \v 5 For even the doe in the field gives birth and abandons, because there is no grass.
\q1 \v 6 And the wild donkeys stand on bare heights, and they gasp for air like jackals.
\q1 Their eyes fail because {there is} no herbage.”
\q2 \v 7 Even though our iniquities testify against us, Yahweh, act for the sake of your name.
\q2 For our apostasies are many; we have sinned against you.
\q2 \v 8 Hope of Israel, the one saving him in the time of trouble,
\q2 Why should you be like a stranger in the land, and like one traveling who turns aside to lodge?
\q2 \v 9 Why should you be like a man being confused, like a mighty one who is not able to save?
\q2 Yet you {are} in our midst, Yahweh, and your name is called over us; do not leave us!
\q1 \v 10 Thus says Yahweh to this people: “Thus they have loved to wander; they have not restrained their feet.
\q1 Therefore, Yahweh does not accept them.
\q2 Now he will remember their iniquity and will punish their sins.”
\v 11 So Yahweh said to me, “Do not pray for this people for good.
\v 12 When they fast, I will not listen to their cry, and when they offer burnt offering and grain offering, I will not accept them. But by the sword and by the famine and by the plague, I will consume them.”
\p
\v 13 Then I said, “Ah, Lord Yahweh! Behold, the prophets are saying to them, ‘You shall not see sword and you shall not have famine, but peace of stability I will give to you in this place.’”
\v 14 And Yahweh said to me, “The prophets are prophesying a lie in my name. I have not sent them and I have not commanded them and I have not spoken to them. They are prophesying to you a vision of a lie and divination and worthlessness and the deceitfulness of their heart.
\v 15 Therefore, thus says Yahweh concerning the prophets, the ones prophesying in my name when I did not send them, and they are saying, ‘Sword and famine there shall not be in this land.’ Those prophets shall perish by sword and by famine.
\v 16 And the people to whom they are prophesying, they will be the ones being cast out in the streets of Jerusalem because of the famine and the sword, and there will be no one burying for them—they, their wives nor their sons nor their daughters. And I will pour out upon them their evil.
\q1 \v 17 And you shall speak this word to them: ‘Let my eyes flow with tears night and day,
\q1 and let them not cease, for with a great breaking she has been broken, the virgin daughter of my people,
\q1 with a very severe blow.
\q1 \v 18 If I go out to the field, then behold, pierced ones of the sword,
\q1 and if I enter the city, then behold, the diseases of famine,
\q1 yes, both prophet and priest go about in a land, and they know not.’”
\q1 \v 19 Have, rejecting, you rejected Judah? Or has your soul loathed Zion?
\q1 Why have you stricken us so that there is no healing for us? We hoped for peace, but there was no good,
\q1 and for a time of healing, but behold, terror!
\q1 \v 20 We know, Yahweh, our wickedness, the iniquity of our fathers, for we have sinned against you.
\q1 \v 21 For the sake of your name, do not spurn us; do not disgrace the throne of your glory.
\q1 Remember, do not break your covenant with us.
\q1 \v 22 Are there any among the idols of the nations who can bring rain or can the heavens give showers?
\q1 {Are} you not he, Yahweh our God? Therefore, we will wait for you, since you have done all these.
\c 15
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh said to me, “If Moses and Samuel stood to my face, my soul would not {be} to this people. Send {them} away from by my face, and let them go out.
\v 2 And it shall be, if they say to you, ‘Where should we go?’ Then you shall tell them, ‘Thus says Yahweh:
\q1 “{Those} who {are} for death to death and {those} who {are} for the sword to the sword
\q1 and {those} who {are} for the famine to the famine and {those} who {are} for the captivity to the captivity.”’
\p
\v 3 And I will appoint over them four kinds”—the declaration of Yahweh—“The sword to kill, and the dogs to drag away, and the bird of the skies and the beast of the earth to devour and to destroy.
\v 4 And I will make them a terror for all kingdoms of the earth, because of Manasseh son of Hezekiah, king of Judah, for what he did in Jerusalem.
\q1 \v 5 For who will have compassion on you, Jerusalem, or who will grieve for you, or who will turn aside to ask about your well-being?
\q1 \v 6 You have forsaken me”—the declaration of Yahweh—“you have gone backward.
\q1 Therefore, I will stretch out my hand against you and destroy you; I am weary of relenting.
\q1 \v 7 And I will winnow them with a winnowing fork in the gates of the land.
\q1 I will bereave {them} of children; I will destroy my people {since} they do not turn back from their ways.
\q1 \v 8 Their widows will be numerous to me, more than the sand of the seas. I will bring to them, against the mother of the young men,
\q1 one who destroys at the noonday; I will cause anguish and terror to fall on her suddenly.
\q1 \v 9 She who has borne seven will grow faint. She will breathe out her life. Her sun will go down while {it is} yet day.
\q1 She will be ashamed and disgraced, and the remnant of them I will deliver to the sword before the face of their enemies,”
\q1 the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 10 Woe to me, my mother, that you bore me, a man of strife and a man of contention to all the land.
\q1 I have not lent, nor have they lent to me, {but} every one of them is cursing me.
\q1 \v 11 Yahweh said: “Surely I have loosened you for good;
\q1 surely I will make the enemy plead with you in the time of disaster and in the time of distress!
\q1 \v 12 Can one break iron, iron from the north, or bronze?
\q1 \v 13 Your wealth and your treasures I will give for plunder, with no price,
\q1 because of all your sins, and throughout your territories.
\q1 \v 14 Then I will cause your enemies to bring you into a land that you do not know,
\q1 for a fire is kindled in my nose {which} shall burn against you.”
\f + \ft Instead of \fqa Then I will make you serve your enemies\fqa*, some ancient copies have \fqa Then I will make you serve your enemies \fqa*.\f*
\q1 \v 15 Yahweh, you yourself know! Remember me and visit me and take vengeance for me on the ones persecuting me.
\q1 May you not take me away in the length of your nose;
\q2 know that I suffer reproach for you.
\q1 \v 16 Your words were found, and I ate them, and your words were a joy to me
\q1 and the rejoicing of my heart,
\q1 for by your name I am called, Yahweh, God of Armies.
\q1 \v 17 I did not sit in the company of the ones who revel nor did I rejoice;
\q1 I sat alone because of your hand, for you have filled me with indignation.
\q1 \v 18 Why is my pain endless and my wound incurable, refusing to be healed?
\q1 Will you be to me like a deceptive {brook}, {like} waters {that are not} reliable?
\q1 \v 19 Therefore, thus says Yahweh, “If you return, then I will restore you, you shall stand to my face.
\q1 And if you take out the precious from the one who is worthless, you will be as my mouth.
\q1 They themselves shall turn to you, but you yourself shall not turn to them.
\q1 \v 20 I will make you to this people a wall of fortified bronze, and they will fight against you,
\q1 but they shall not prevail against you, for I {am} with you to save you and to deliver you,” —the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 21 “And I will deliver you from the hand of the evil and I will redeem you from the palm of the ruthless.”
\c 16
\p
\v 1 And the word of Yahweh came to me, saying,
\v 2 “You shall not take to you a wife, and there shall not be to you sons or daughters in this place.”
\v 3 For thus says Yahweh about the sons and the daughters, the ones born in this place, and about their mothers who bear them, and about their fathers, the ones fathering them in this land,
\v 4 “They will die deaths of diseases. They shall not be lamented, and they shall not be buried; they shall be as dung on the face of the ground, and by the sword and by famine they shall be consumed, and their corpses shall be food for the bird of the skies and for the beast of the earth.”
\p
\v 5 For thus says Yahweh, “Do not enter the house of a funeral meal nor go to lament nor grieve for them, for I have taken away my peace from this people,”—the declaration of Yahweh—“covenant faithfulness and mercies.
\v 6 And great and small shall die in this land. They shall not be buried, and they shall not lament for them and he shall not cut himself and he shall not be bald for them.
\v 7 And not they shall break bread for them in mourning to comfort him for the one who is dead, and they shall not give them a cup of consolation to drink for his father or for his mother.
\v 8 And you shall not go to the house of a feast to sit with them, to eat and drink.”
\v 9 For thus says Yahweh of Armies, God of Israel, “Behold me causing to cease from this place, before your eyes and in your days, the voice of joy and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride.
\p
\v 10 And it will happen that when you tell this people all of these words, and they say to you, ‘Why has Yahweh spoken all this great disaster concerning us,’ and ‘What {is} our iniquity?’ and ‘What {is} our sin that we have sinned against Yahweh our God?’
\v 11 Then you shall say to them, ‘Because your fathers have abandoned me’—the declaration of Yahweh—‘and they have walked after other gods and they have served them and they have bowed down to them and they have abandoned me and they have not kept my law.
\v 12 And you yourselves have done evil, to do more than your fathers, and behold, each one is walking after the stubbornness of his evil heart; to not listen to me.
\v 13 So I will throw you far out of this land into a land that you have not known, you nor your fathers, and you shall serve other gods there by day and night, for I will not show favor to you.’
\p
\v 14 “Therefore, behold, the days are coming,”—the declaration of Yahweh—“when it shall no longer be said, ‘The life of Yahweh, who brought up the sons of Israel from the land of Egypt,’
\v 15 but rather, ‘The life of Yahweh, who brought up the sons of Israel from the land of the north and from all of the lands where he had driven them there.’ For I will bring them back to their land that I gave to their fathers.
\p
\v 16 Behold me sending for many fishermen”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and they shall fish them out. And after that I will send for many hunters and they shall hunt them from on every mountain and from on every hill, and from out of the crevices of the rocks.
\v 17 For my eyes {are} on all their ways; they are not hidden from before my face, nor is their iniquity concealed from before my eyes.
\v 18 But I will first repay double of their iniquity and their sin because they have defiled my land with the carcass of their detestable things, and they have filled my inheritance with their abominations.”
\q1 \v 19 Yahweh, my strength and my stronghold and my refuge in the day of trouble.
\q1 To you the nations shall come from the ends of the earth and say, “Surely our fathers have inherited a lie,
\q1 worthlessness, and there is no profit in them.
\q1 \v 20 Will a man make for himself gods? But they {are} not gods.”
\q1 \v 21 “Therefore, behold me making them to know, this time I will make them to know my hand and my might,
\q1 and they shall know that my name {is} Yahweh.
\c 17
\q1 \v 1 “The sin of Judah is being written with a stylus of iron with a point of diamond,
\q1 being engraved on the tablet of their heart and on the horns of your altars.
\q1 \v 2 as their children remember their altars and their Asherim
\q1 by the green tree on the high hills.
\q1 \v 3 My mountain in the field,
\q1 your wealth,
\q1 all your treasures as plunder I will give,
\q1 your high places for sin within all your borders.
\q1 \v 4 And you shall loosen, even yourself, from the inheritance that I gave to you,
\q1 and I will make you serve your enemies in the land that you do not know,
\q1 for you have kindled a fire in my nose, which shall burn unto eternity.”
\q1 \v 5 Thus says Yahweh, “Cursed is the man who trusts in mankind;
\q1 and {who} makes flesh his arm, and {who} turns away his heart from Yahweh.
\q1 \v 6 For he will be like a shrub in the desert, and he will not see when good comes,
\q1 but will dwell in the parched places in the wilderness, a land of salt, and not inhabited.
\q1 \v 7 Blessed is the man who trusts in Yahweh, and his confidence is {in} Yahweh.
\q1 \v 8 For he will be like a tree which is planted by waters, and by the river, which sends out its roots,
\q1 and he does not fear when heat comes, for its leaf will be green,
\q1 and in the year of drought he will not be anxious, nor will he cease from producing fruit.
\q1 \v 9 The heart {is} deceitful above all {things}, and it is incurable; who can know it?
\q1 \v 10 I, Yahweh, {am} the one searching {the} heart, testing the kidneys,
\q1 to give to each man according to his ways, according to the fruit of his deeds.
\q1 \v 11 {Like} a partridge that hatches but did not lay is the one getting riches but not by justice;
\q1 in the midst of his days, it will leave him, and at his end, he will be a fool.”
\q1 \v 12 A throne of glory, high from the beginning, {is} the place of our sanctuary.
\q1 \v 13 The hope of Israel, Yahweh, all of the ones abandoning you will be ashamed. The ones turning away from me will be written in the earth,
\q1 because they have forsaken Yahweh, the fountain of living waters.
\q1 \v 14 Heal me, Yahweh, and I shall be healed; save me, and I shall be saved, for {you} are my praise.
\q1 \v 15 Behold, they are saying to me, “Where {is} the word of Yahweh? Let it come now!”
\q1 \v 16 But I did not hasten from being one shepherding following you, and the disastrous day I have not desired.
\q1 You know what came out of my lips; it was before your face.
\q1 \v 17 Do not be a terror to me; you {are} my refuge in the day of disaster.
\q1 \v 18 Let the ones persecuting me be put to shame, but do not let me be put to shame. Let them be dismayed, but do not let me be dismayed.
\q1 Bring the day of disaster on them and destroy them with double destruction.
\p
\v 19 Thus said Yahweh to me: “Go and stand in the gate of the sons of the people which the kings of Judah enter by and which they go out by, and in all of the gates of Jerusalem.
\v 20 And you shall say to them, ‘Hear the word of Yahweh, kings of Judah and all of Judah, and all of the ones who inhabit Jerusalem, the ones who enter by these gates.
\v 21 Thus says Yahweh: “Take heed to yourselves and do not bear a burden on the day of the Sabbath, nor bring it in by the gates of Jerusalem.
\v 22 And do not carry a burden out of your houses on the day of the Sabbath. and you are not to do any work, but you shall make holy the day of the Sabbath, as I commanded your fathers.
\v 23 Yet they listened not nor inclined their ear, but stiffened their neck to not hear and to not receive instruction.
\p
\v 24 And it will happen that if listening, you listen to me”—the declaration of Yahweh—“to not bring a burden through the gates of this city on the day of the Sabbath, but make holy the day of the Sabbath to not do on it any work,
\v 25 then shall enter the gates of this city, kings and officials, sitting on the throne of David, riding in the chariot and on horses, they and their officials, men of Judah and the ones inhabiting Jerusalem, and they shall inhabit this city forever.
\v 26 And they shall come from the cities of Judah and from all around Jerusalem, and from the land of Benjamin, and from the foothills, and from the hill country, and from the Negev, bringing burnt offerings and sacrifices and grain offerings and frankincense and bringing thank offerings to the house of Yahweh.
\v 27 But if you do not listen to me, to make holy the day of the Sabbath and to not bear a burden nor enter by the gates of Jerusalem on the day of the Sabbath, then I will kindle a fire in her gates, and it shall consume the palaces of Jerusalem, and it shall not be quenched.”’”
\c 18
\p
\v 1 The word that came to Jeremiah from Yahweh, saying,
\v 2 “Arise and go down to the house of the potter, and there I will cause you to hear my words.”
\v 3 And I went down to the house of the potter, and behold, he was doing work on the wheels.
\p
\v 4 And the vessel that he was making with the clay was ruined in the hand of the potter, and he returned and made it {into} another vessel, as {it} was right in the eyes of the potter to make.
\p
\v 5 And the word of Yahweh came to me, saying,
\v 6 “Like this potter, am I not able to do to you, house of Israel?”—the declaration of Yahweh—“Behold, like the clay in the hand of the potter, so {are} you in my hand, house of Israel.
\v 7 {If at any} moment I speak concerning a nation or concerning a kingdom, to uproot and to tear down and to destroy {it},
\v 8 and {if} that nation turns from its evil, which I have spoken against it, then I will relent concerning the disaster that I planned to do to it.
\v 9 And {if at any} moment I speak concerning a nation or concerning a kingdom, to build and to plant {it},
\v 10 And {if} it does the evil in my eyes, not listening to my voice, then I will relent concerning the good that I said to do good to it.
\p
\v 11 And now, please say to the men of Judah and to the dwellers of Jerusalem, saying, ‘Thus says Yahweh: “Behold, I am forming evil against you and devising a plan against you. Please turn back, each man from his evil way, and make good your ways and your deeds.”’”
\v 12 But they will say, “{It is} hopeless, for we will walk after our own plans, and each man, the stubbornness of his evil heart, we will do.”
\p
\v 13 Therefore, thus says Yahweh: “Please ask among the nations. Who has heard {things} like these? The virgin of Israel has done a very horrible thing.
\q1 \v 14 Does the snow of Lebanon leave the rock of the field? Or are foreign, cold, flowing waters removed?
\q1 \v 15 Yet my people have forgotten me; they burn incense to worthlessness, and they have caused themselves to stumble in their ways, the paths of old, to walk {on} paths, a way not being built up,
\q1 \v 16 to make their land a desolation, a hissing of eternity. Everyone passing over it will be appalled and will shake his head.
\q1 \v 17 Like a wind of the east I will scatter them to the face of the enemy. The neck and not the face I will show them on the day of their calamity.”
\p
\v 18 And they said, “Come, and let us devise plans against Jeremiah, for the law will not perish from the priest, nor counsel from the wise, nor a word from the prophet. Come and let us strike him with the tongue, and let us not pay attention to any of his words.”
\q1 \v 19 Pay attention to me, Yahweh, and listen to the voice of my opponents.
\q1 \v 20 Should evil be repaid in place of good? For they have dug a pit for my life. Remember my standing to your face to speak good concerning them, to turn back your wrath from them.
\q1 \v 21 Therefore, give their sons to the famine, and deliver them over to the hands of the sword. And let their wives become bereaved of children, and widows, and let their men become those killed of death, {and} their young men struck of the sword in the battle.
\q1 \v 22 Let a cry be heard from their houses when you bring a raiding band against them suddenly, for they have dug a pit to capture me, and they have hidden traps for my feet.
\q1 \v 23 But you, Yahweh, you know all of their counsel against me for death. Do not atone for their iniquity, and do not blot out their sin from before your face; but let them be overthrown to your face. In the time of your nose, deal with them.
\c 19
\p
\v 1 Thus says Yahweh: “Go and buy a potter’s earthenware jar, and {take} from of the elders of the people and of from of the elders of the priests.
\v 2 And go out to the Valley of the Son of Hinnom, which {is} at the entrance of the Potsherd Gate, and proclaim there the words that I will speak to you.
\v 3 And you shall say, ‘Hear the word of Yahweh, kings of Judah and dwellers of Jerusalem. Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: Behold me, bringing disaster on this place, that everyone hearing it, his ears will tingle,
\v 4 on account that they have forsaken me and have made this place foreign, and they have burned incense in it to other gods that they did not know, they nor their fathers nor the kings of Judah, and they have filled this place {with} the blood of innocents.
\v 5 And they have built the high places of Baal to burn their sons in the fire {as} burnt offerings to Baal, which I did not command, and I did not speak, and it did not come up into my heart.
\p
\v 6 Therefore, behold, days are coming”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and this place will no longer be called Topheth or the Valley of the Son of Hinnom, but rather, the Valley of Slaughter.
\v 7 And I will empty the counsel of Judah and Jerusalem in this place, and I will cause them to fall by the sword to the face of their enemies, and by the hand of those who seek their life. And I will give their corpses as food to the birds of the heavens and to the beast of the earth.
\v 8 And I will make this city a desolation and a hissing. Everyone passing by it will be appalled and will hiss over all its wounds.
\v 9 And I will cause them to eat the flesh of their sons and the flesh of their daughters, and each man will eat the flesh of his neighbor in the siege and in the distress with which their enemies and the seekers of their life will impose on them.’
\p
\v 10 “And you shall break the jar before the eyes of the men who are going with you.
\v 11 And you shall say to them, ‘Thus says Yahweh of Armies: Thus I will break this people and this city, as one breaks a vessel of the potter that is not able to be made whole again. And in Topheth they will bury, until there is no place to bury.
\v 12 Thus I will do to this place’ —the declaration of Yahweh—‘and to its dwellers, and to make this city like Topheth.
\v 13 And the houses of Jerusalem and the houses of the kings of Judah shall be like the defiled place of Topheth, because of all of the houses where they have burned incense on their roofs to all of the host of the heavens and poured out drink offerings to other gods.’”
\p
\v 14 And Jeremiah came from Topheth, where Yahweh had sent him there to prophesy, and he stood in the courtyard of the house of Yahweh and said to all the people:
\v 15 “Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: ‘Behold me bringing to this city and to all its towns all the evil that I have spoken against it, because they have stiffened their neck so as not to hear my words.’”
\c 20
\p
\v 1 Now Pashhur, the son of Immer the priest—he {was} chief officer in the house of Yahweh—heard Jeremiah prophesying these words.
\v 2 And Pashhur struck Jeremiah the prophet and put him in the stocks that {were} at the Upper Gate of Benjamin, which {was} in the house of Yahweh.
\v 3 And it happened on the next day that Pashhur brought Jeremiah out from the stocks. And Jeremiah said to him, “Yahweh has not called your name Pashhur, but rather, Magor-Missabib.
\p
\v 4 For thus says Yahweh: ‘Behold me making you as a terror to yourself and to all your lovers, and they will fall by the sword of their enemies, and your eyes will be seeing. And I will give all of Judah into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he will exile them to Babylon, and he will strike them with the sword.
\v 5 And I will give all the wealth of this city and all its produce and all its precious things, and all the treasures of the kings of Judah I will give into the hand of their enemies, and they will plunder them and take them and bring them to Babylon.
\v 6 And you, Pashhur, and all the dwellers of your house will go into captivity. And to Babylon you will go, and there you will die, and there you will be buried, you and all your lovers to whom you prophesied in falsehood.’”
\q1 \v 7 You persuaded me, Yahweh, and I was persuaded.
\q2 You overpowered me, and you prevailed.
\q1 I have become for a derision all the day;
\q2 everyone is mocking me.
\q1 \v 8 For as often as I speak, I cry out, I call out,
\q2 “Violence and destruction!”
\q1 For the word of Yahweh has become for me
\q2 a reproach and a derision all the day.
\q1 \v 9 And I said, “I will not remember him,
\q2 and I will not speak again in his name.”
\q1 But it was in my heart like a burning fire,
\q2 shut up in my bones;
\q1 and I was weary of containing {it,}
\q2 and I was not able.
\q1 \v 10 For I have heard the whispering of many:
\q2 “Terror on every side!
\q2 Report {him}! Let us report him!”
\q1 Every man of my peace,
\q2 the watchers of my stumbling:
\q2 “Perhaps he will be deceived;
\q2 then we will prevail against him,
\q2 and we will take our vengeance on him.”
\q1 \v 11 But Yahweh {is} with me like a ruthless, mighty warrior;
\q2 therefore, my pursuers will stumble and will not prevail.
\q1 They will be greatly ashamed, for they have not succeeded;
\q2 everlasting humiliation will not be forgotten.
\q1 \v 12 And Yahweh of Armies, the one testing the righteous,
\q2 seeing the kidneys and the heart—
\q1 let me see your vengeance on them,
\q2 for to you I have made known my cause.
\q1 \v 13 Sing to Yahweh!
\q2 Praise Yahweh!
\q1 For he has rescued the life of the needy
\q2 from the hand of evildoers.
\q1 \v 14 Cursed {be} the day on which I was born;
\q2 the day that my mother bore me,
\q2 let it not be blessed.
\q1 \v 15 Cursed {be} the man who brought news to my father, saying,
\q2 “A male child has been born to you,”
\q2 making glad, he made him glad.
\q1 \v 16 And let that man be like the cities
\q2 that Yahweh overthrew and did not relent.
\q1 And let him hear an outcry in the morning
\q2 and a shout at the time of noon,
\q1 \v 17 because he did not put me to death from the womb,
\q2 so that my mother would have been my grave
\q2 and her womb pregnant forever.
\q1 \v 18 Why {is} this, {that} from the womb I came out
\q2 to see trouble and sorrow,
\q2 and my days come to an end in shame.
\c 21
\p
\v 1 The word that was to Jeremiah from Yahweh when King Zedekiah sent to him Pashhur, the son of Malkijah, and Zephaniah, the son of Maaseiah the priest, saying,
\v 2 “Please inquire of Yahweh on our behalf, for Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon is fighting against us. Perhaps Yahweh will do with us according to all his wonders, and he will withdraw from upon us.”
\p
\v 3 And Jeremiah said to them, “Thus you shall say to Zedekiah:
\v 4 ‘Thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel: “Behold me turning back the weapons of war that {are} in your hand, which you are fighting with them against the king of Babylon and against the Chaldeans who are besieging against you from outside the wall, and I will gather them into the midst of this city.
\v 5 And I, I will fight against you with an outstretched hand and with a strong arm, and in nose and in heat and in great fury.
\v 6 And I will strike the dwellers of this city, both man and beast. They will die by a great plague.
\v 7 And afterward’—the declaration of Yahweh—‘I will give Zedekiah king of Judah and his servants and the people and the ones remaining in this city from the plague, from the sword, and from the famine into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon and into the hand of their enemies and into the hand of the seekers of their life. And he will strike them to the mouth of the sword. He will not pity them, and he will not spare, and he will not have compassion.’
\p
\v 8 And to this people you will say, ‘Thus says Yahweh: Behold me giving to your faces the way of life and the way of death.
\v 9 The one dwelling in this city will die by the sword and by the famine and by the plague. But the one going out and falling upon the Chaldeans who are besieging against you will live, and his life will be to him for spoil.
\v 10 For I have set my face against this city for evil and not for good’—the declaration of Yahweh—‘Into the hand of the king of Babylon it will be given, and he will burn it with fire.’”
\p
\v 11 “And to the house of the king of Judah, ‘Hear the word of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 12 House of David, thus says Yahweh, “Execute judgement {with} justice in the morning,
\q2 and rescue the one who is being robbed from the hand of the oppressor,
\q1 lest my wrath go out like fire and burn,
\q2 and there is no one quenching {it,}
\q2 from the face of the evil of their deeds.
\q1 \v 13 Behold me against you, dweller of the valley,
\q2 rock of the plateau”—the declaration of Yahweh—
\q1 “the ones saying, ‘Who will come down upon us,
\q2 and who will enter into our dwellings?’
\q1 \v 14 And I will visit upon you according to the fruit of your deeds”—the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 “and I will kindle a fire in her forest,
\q2 and it will consume all her surroundings.”
\c 22
\p
\v 1 Thus says Yahweh: “Go down to the house of the king of Judah and speak there this word.
\v 2 And you shall say, ‘Hear the word of Yahweh, king of Judah, the one sitting on the throne of David, you and your servants and your people who are entering in these gates.
\v 3 Thus says Yahweh: Do justice and righteousness, and rescue the one who has been robbed from the hand of the oppressor. And do not oppress the sojourner, the fatherless, or the widow. Do not do violence, and do not shed innocent blood in this place.
\v 4 For if doing you do this word, then kings sitting for David on his throne will enter through the gates of this house, riding in the chariot and on horses, he and his servants and his people.
\v 5 But if you will not hear these words, by myself I have sworn”—the declaration of Yahweh—“that this house will become a ruin.’”
\p
\v 6 For thus says Yahweh concerning the house of the king of Judah:
\q1 “You {are} Gilead to me,
\q2 the top of Lebanon.
\q1 {But} surely I will make you a wilderness,
\q2 cities not inhabited.
\q1 \v 7 And I will consecrate destroyers against you,
\q2 a man and his weapons,
\q1 and they will cut down the choicest of your cedars
\q2 and cast {them} on the fire.
\p
\v 8 And many nations will pass by this city, and they will say, a man to his neighbor, ‘On account of what has Yahweh done thus to great this city?’
\v 9 And they will say, ‘On account of the fact that they abandoned the covenant of Yahweh their God and bowed down to other gods and served them.’”
\p
\v 10 Do not weep for the dead one, and do not mourn for him. Weep to weep for the one going away, for he will not return again nor see his native land.
\p
\v 11 For thus says Yahweh concerning Shallum, the son of Josiah, king of Judah, the one reigning in the place of Josiah his father, who went out from this place: “He will not return there again.
\v 12 But in the place where they exiled him, there he shall die, and he will not see this land again.”
\q1 \v 13 Woe to the one building his house by unrighteousness
\q2 and his upper rooms by injustice,
\q1 {who} makes his neighbor work for nothing
\q2 and does not give him his wages,
\q1 \v 14 the one saying, “I will build for myself a house of great measurements
\q2 and spacious upper rooms,”
\q1 and he cuts out windows for himself
\q2 and panels {it} with cedar
\q2 and paints {it} with vermilion.
\p
\v 15 Will you reign because you are competing in cedar? Did not your father eat and drink and do justice and righteousness? Then {it} was good for him.
\v 16 He judged the cause of the poor and the needy. Then {it} was good. Is not this the knowledge of me?”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\v 17 “But there is nothing in your eyes and your heart except upon your unjust gain, and upon the blood of the innocent to pour {it} out, and upon oppression and upon extortion to do {them.}
\p
\v 18 Therefore, thus says Yahweh concerning Jehoiakim, the son of Josiah, king of Judah:
\q1 “They will not mourn for him, {saying,}
\q2 ‘Woe, my brother!’ and ‘Woe, sister!’
\q1 They will not mourn for him, {saying,}
\q2 ‘Woe, master!’ and ‘Woe, his majesty!’
\q1 \v 19 He will be buried with the burial of a donkey,
\q2 dragged and thrown out
\q2 beyond the gates of Jerusalem.”
\q1 \v 20 Go up to Lebanon and cry out,
\q2 and give your voice in Bashan,
\q1 and cry out from Abarim,
\q2 for all your lovers are broken.
\q1 \v 21 I spoke to you in your prosperity;
\q2 you said, ‘I will not hear.’
\q1 This {has} been your way from your youth,
\q2 for you have not heard my voice.
\q1 \v 22 The wind shall shepherd all your shepherds,
\q2 and your lovers shall go into captivity.
\q1 Surely then you will be ashamed and humiliated
\q2 because of all your evil.
\q1 \v 23 You who are dwelling in Lebanon,
\q2 you who are nesting in the cedars,
\q1 how you will be pitied when pangs come to you,
\q2 pain like the one giving birth!
\p
\v 24 {As} I live”—the declaration of Yahweh—“even if Coniah, the son of Jehoiakim, king of Judah, were a signet on my right hand, surely from there I would tear you off.
\v 25 And I will give you into the hand of the ones seeking your life and into the hand of those whom you fear from their face, and into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon and into the hand of the Chaldeans.
\v 26 And I will hurl you and your mother who bore you into another land where you were not born, and there you will die.
\v 27 And to the land where they are lifting up their soul to return there, there they will not return!”
\q1 \v 28 “{Is} this man Coniah a despised, shattered vessel?
\q2 Or {is} he a vessel in which there is no delight?
\q1 Why have they been hurled, he and his seed,
\q2 and thrown into a land that they did not know?”
\q1 \v 29 Land, land, land,
\q2 hear the word of Yahweh!
\q1 \v 30 Thus says Yahweh:
\q1 “Write down this man childless,
\q2 a man {who} will not prosper in his days,
\q1 for no man from his seed shall prosper,
\q2 sitting on the throne of David
\q2 and ruling again in Judah.”
\c 23
\p
\v 1 “Woe to the shepherds who are destroying and scattering the sheep of my pasture!”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\v 2 Therefore, thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel, concerning the shepherds who are shepherding my people, “You yourselves have scattered my flock and have driven them away, and you have not attended to them. Behold, I am attending to you for the evil of your deeds”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\v 3 “And I, I will gather the remnant of my flock from all the lands where I have driven them, and I will bring them back to their pasture, and they will be fruitful and multiply.
\v 4 And I will raise up shepherds over them, and they will shepherd them. And they will no longer fear, and they will not be dismayed, and none will be missing”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 5 “Behold, days are coming”—the declaration of Yahweh—“when I will raise up for David a righteous branch,
\q2 and he shall reign {as} king and act wisely, and he shall do justice and righteousness in the land.
\q1 \v 6 In his days Judah will be saved,
\q2 and Israel will dwell in safety.
\q1 And this {is} his name by which he will be called:
\q2 ‘Yahweh {is} our righteousness.’”
\p
\v 7 “Therefore, behold, days are coming”—the declaration of Yahweh—“when they will no longer say, ‘As Yahweh lives, who brought the sons of Israel up from the land of Egypt,’
\v 8 but rather, ‘As Yahweh lives, who brought up and who led the offspring of the house of Israel from the land of the north and from all the lands where I had driven them.’ And they will dwell in their own land.”
\p
\v 9 Concerning the prophets:
\q1 My heart is broken within me;
\q2 all my bones tremble.
\q1 I have become like a drunken man,
\q2 and like a man whom wine has overcome,
\q1 because of the face of Yahweh
\q2 and because of the face of his holy words.
\q1 \v 10 For the land is full of adulterers,
\q2 for because of the face of a curse, the land mourns,
\q1 the pastures of the wilderness have dried up.
\q2 Their course is evil,
\q2 and their might {is} not right.
\q1 \v 11 “For both prophet and priest are polluted;
\q2 even in my house I have found their evil”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 12 “Therefore, their way shall be to them like slippery places in the darkness;
\q2 they shall be pushed in and shall fall in it,
\q1 for I will bring disaster upon them,
\q2 {in} the year of their visitation”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 13 “And among the prophets of Samaria, I saw an impropriety:
\q2 they prophesied by Baal
\q2 and led my people Israel astray.
\q1 \v 14 And among the prophets of Jerusalem, I have seen a horrible thing:
\q2 committing adultery and walking in falsehood.
\q1 And they have strengthened the hands of evildoers,
\q2 so that no man turns back from his evil.
\q1 All of them have become to me like Sodom,
\q2 and her inhabitants, like Gomorrah.”
\p
\v 15 Therefore, thus says Yahweh of Armies concerning the prophets:
\q1 “Behold me making them eat wormwood
\q2 and making them drink water of poison,
\q1 for from the prophets of Jerusalem
\q2 pollution has gone out to all the land.”
\p
\v 16 Thus says Yahweh of Armies:
\q1 “Do not listen to the words of the prophets
\q2 who are prophesying to you.
\q1 They are deluding you;
\q2 they are speaking a vision of their {own} heart,
\q2 not from the mouth of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 17 Saying, they say to the ones despising me,
\q2 ‘Yahweh has spoken: Peace will be for you.’
\q1 And to everyone walking in the stubbornness of his heart they have said,
\q2 ‘Disaster will not come upon you.’”
\q1 \v 18 For who has stood in the counsel of Yahweh
\q2 and seen and heard his word?
\q1 Who has paid attention to his word and listened?
\q1 \v 19 Behold, a storm of Yahweh! Wrath has gone out,
\q2 and a whirling tempest—
\q1 upon the head of the wicked it will whirl.
\q1 \v 20 The nose of Yahweh will not turn back
\q2 until he has done and until he has established
\q2 the intentions of his heart.
\q1 In the latter days
\q2 you will understand it with understanding.
\q1 \v 21 “I did not send the prophets,
\q2 yet they, they ran.
\q1 I did not speak to them,
\q2 yet they, they prophesied.
\q1 \v 22 But if they had stood in my counsel,
\q2 then they would have caused my people to hear my words,
\q1 and they would have turned them back from their evil way
\q2 and from the evil of their deeds.”
\q1 \v 23 “Am I a God from near”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and not a God from far?
\q1 \v 24 Can a man hide himself in secret places so I will not see him?”—the declaration of Yahweh—
\q1 “Do I not fill the heavens and the earth?”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\p
\v 25 “I have heard what the prophets have said, the ones prophesying falsehood in my name, saying, ‘I have dreamed! I have dreamed!’
\v 26 Until when will this be in the heart of the prophets, the ones prophesying falsehood and the prophets of the deceit of their heart,
\v 27 the ones planning to make my people forget my name by their dreams which they recount, a man to his neighbor, just as their fathers forgot my name on account of Baal?
\p
\v 28 The prophet who has a dream with him, let him recount {his} dream, but the one who has my word with him, let him speak my word faithfully. What to the straw with the grain?”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\p
\v 29 “Is not my word like fire?”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and like a hammer that shatters a rock?”
\p
\v 30 “Therefore, behold me against the prophets”—the declaration of Yahweh—“the ones stealing my words, a man from his neighbor.
\v 31 Behold me against the prophets”—the declaration of Yahweh—“the ones taking their tongue and declaring a declaration.
\v 32 Behold me against the ones prophesying dreams of falsehood”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and they have recounted them and lead my people astray by their falsehoods and by their recklessness, yet I, I did not send them, and I did not command them. So benefiting, they will not benefit this people”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\p
\v 33 “And when this people, or a prophet, or a priest asks you, saying, ‘What {is} the burden of Yahweh?’—then you shall say to them, ‘What burden?’ And I will abandon you,” the declaration of Yahweh.
\v 34 “And the prophet and the priest and the people who say, ‘The burden of Yahweh,’ I will punish that man and his house.
\v 35 Thus you will say, a man to his neighbor and a man to his brother, ‘What has Yahweh answered?’ or ‘What has Yahweh spoken?’
\v 36 But ‘the burden of Yahweh’ you shall not mention again, for the burden will be each man’s {own} word, for you have perverted the words of the living God, Yahweh of Armies, our God.
\v 37 Thus you say to the prophet, ‘What has Yahweh answered you?’ or ‘What has Yahweh spoken?’
\v 38 But if you say, ‘The burden of Yahweh,’ therefore thus says Yahweh: ‘Because you say this word, “The burden of Yahweh,” when I sent to you, saying, “You shall not say, ‘The burden of Yahweh,’
\v 39 therefore, behold me, I will surely forget you and will abandon you and the city that I gave to you and to your fathers, from at my face.
\v 40 And I will put upon you everlasting reproach and everlasting shame that will not be forgotten.”’”’
\c 24
\p
\v 1 Yahweh showed me, and behold, two baskets of figs {were} set to the face of the temple of Yahweh, after Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, exiled Jeconiah, the son of Jehoiakim, king of Judah, and the officials of Judah, and the craftsmen and the smiths from Jerusalem, and brought them {to} Babylon.
\v 2 One basket {had} very good figs, like the first ripe figs, and one basket {had} very bad figs that could not be eaten from badness.
\v 3 And said Yahweh to me, “What are you seeing, Jeremiah?” And I said, “Figs. The good figs {are} very good, and the bad {ones} are very bad, that they cannot be eaten from badness.”
\p
\v 4 And the word of Yahweh was to me, saying,
\v 5 “Thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel: Like these good figs, so I will regard the exile of Judah whom I sent away from this place {to} the land of the Chaldeans, for good.
\v 6 And I will set my eyes on them for good, and I will bring them back to this land. And I will build them up and not tear {them} down, and I will plant them and not uproot {them}.
\v 7 And I will give to them a heart to know me, that I {am} Yahweh. And they will be to me for a people, and I, I will be to them for God, for they will return to me with all of their heart.
\p
\v 8 And like the bad figs that cannot be eaten from badness”—for thus says Yahweh—“so I will give Zedekiah, king of Judah, and his officials, and the remnant of Jerusalem, the ones remaining in this land, and the ones dwelling in the land of Egypt.
\v 9 And I will give them to horror, for evil, to all of the kingdoms of the earth, for a reproach and for a proverb, for a taunt and for a curse, in all of the places where I will drive them there.
\v 10 And I will send among them the sword, the famine, and the plague, until they are consumed from upon the land that I gave to them and to their fathers.”
\c 25
\p
\v 1 The word that came to Jeremiah concerning all of the people of Judah in the fourth year of Jehoiakim, the son of Josiah, king of Judah—that {was} the first year of Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon—
\v 2 which Jeremiah the prophet spoke to all of the people of Judah and to all of the dwellers of Jerusalem, saying,
\v 3 “From the thirteenth year of Josiah, son of Amon, king of Judah, and until this day—this {is} twenty-three years—the word of Yahweh has come to me, and I have spoken to you, rising early and speaking, but you have not listened.
\v 4 And Yahweh has sent to you all of his servants the prophets, rising early and sending {them}, but you have not listened, and you have not extended your ear to hear,
\v 5 saying, ‘Turn back, please, each man from his evil way and from the evil of your deeds, and dwell on the land that Yahweh gave to you and to your fathers from everlasting to everlasting.
\v 6 And do not go after other gods to serve them and to bow down to them, and do not provoke me to anger with the work of your hands, and I will not do harm to you.’
\v 7 Yet you have not listened to me”—the declaration of Yahweh—“so as to provoke me to anger with the work of your hands, to {your} harm.
\p
\v 8 Therefore, thus says Yahweh of Armies, ‘Because you have not listened to my words,
\v 9 behold me sending, and I will take all of the families of the north”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and to Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, my servant, and I will bring them against this land and against its dwellers and against all of these nations all around, and I will devote them to destruction and will make them a horror and a hissing and to an eternal desolation.
\v 10 And I will cause to perish from them the sound of joy and the sound of gladness, the sound of the bridegroom and the sound of the bride, the sound of the millstones and the light of the lamp.
\p
\v 11 And all of this land will become a ruin, a desolation, and these nations will serve the king of Babylon seventy years.
\p
\v 12 And it will happen, when seventy years are fulfilled, I will punish the king of Babylon and that nation”—the declaration of Yahweh—“for their iniquity, and the land of the Chaldeans, and I will make it a perpetual desolation.
\v 13 And I will bring upon that land all of my words that I have spoken against it, all of that which is written in this book, which Jeremiah has prophesied against all of the nations.
\v 14 For many nations and great kings will also make them serve, even them, and I will repay them according to their deed and according to the work of their hands.’”
\p
\v 15 For thus says Yahweh, God of Israel, to me, “Take this cup of the wine of the wrath from my hand, and you will make all of the nations to which I am sending you drink it.
\v 16 And they will drink and will stagger and will act like madmen from the face of the sword that I am sending among them.”
\v 17 And I took the cup from the hand of Yahweh, and I made all of the nations drink, {those} to which Yahweh had sent me:
\v 18 Jerusalem and the cities of Judah and its kings {and} its officials, to make them a ruin, a desolation, a hissing, and a curse, as {on} this day;
\v 19 Pharaoh, king of Egypt, and his servants and his officials and all of his people,
\v 20 and all of the mixed people, and all of the kings of the land of Uz, and all of the kings of the land of the Philistines—Ashkelon and Gaza and Ekron and the remnant of Ashdod;
\v 21 Edom and Moab and the sons of Ammon;
\v 22 and all of the kings of Tyre and all of the kings of Sidon and the kings of the coastland that {is} across the sea;
\v 23 and Dedan and Tema and Buz and all of the ones cut {at} the corner {of} their hair;
\v 24 and all of the kings of Arabia and all of the kings of the mixed people who dwell in the wilderness;
\v 25 and all of the kings of Zimri and all of the kings of Elam and all of the kings of Media;
\v 26 and all of the kings of the north, the near and the far, a man to his brother, and all of the kingdoms of the world that {are} on the face of the earth. And the king of Sheshach shall drink after them.
\p
\v 27 “Then you shall say to them, ‘Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: Drink and be drunk and vomit and fall, and do not rise, from the face of the sword that I am sending among you.’
\v 28 And it will happen, if they refuse to take the cup from your hand to drink, then you shall say to them, ‘Thus says Yahweh of Armies: Drinking, you shall drink.’
\v 29 For behold, in the city over which my name is called I am beginning to bring harm, and you, being acquitted, will you be acquitted? You will not be acquitted, for a sword I am calling against all of the dwellers of the earth”—the declaration of Yahweh of Armies.
\p
\v 30 “And you, you will prophesy to them all of these words and say to them,
\q1 ‘Yahweh will roar from the height,
\q2 and from the habitation of his holiness he will give his voice.
\q1 Roaring, he will roar against his pasture;
\q2 a shout like {those} who are treading, he will sing
\q2 to all of the dwellers of the earth.
\q1 \v 31 A tumult will come to the end of the earth,
\q2 for Yahweh has a dispute with the nations.
\q1 He is entering into judgment with all flesh;
\q2 the wicked—he will give them to the sword’”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 32 Thus says Yahweh of Armies,
\q2 “Behold, disaster is going out from nation to nation,
\q1 and a great storm will be stirred up
\q2 from the farthest parts of the earth.
\q1 \v 33 And the slain of Yahweh shall be on that day
\q2 from {one} end of the earth to the {other} end of the earth.
\q1 They shall not be mourned,
\q2 and they shall not be gathered, and they shall not be buried.
\q2 They will be as dung on the face of the ground.
\q1 \v 34 Wail, you shepherds, and cry out,
\q2 and roll in {the} dust, you nobles of the flock,
\q1 for your days for slaughter and your dispersions have been fulfilled,
\q2 and you shall fall like a vessel of delight.
\q1 \v 35 And refuge shall vanish from the shepherds,
\q2 and escape, from the noble ones of the flock.
\q1 \v 36 {There is} the sound of the cry of the shepherds
\q2 and the wailing of the nobles of the flock,
\q1 for Yahweh is destroying their pasture.
\q1 \v 37 And the pastures of peace are devastated
\q2 from the face of the burning of the nose of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 38 He has left his lair like the young lion,
\q2 for their land has become a desolation
\q1 from the face of the burning of the oppressor
\q2 and from the face of the burning of his nose.”
\c 26
\p
\v 1 In the beginning of the reign of Jehoiakim, the son of Josiah, king of Judah, this word was from Yahweh, saying,
\v 2 “Thus says Yahweh: Stand in the courtyard of the house of Yahweh and speak to all the cities of Judah that are coming to bow down {in} the house of Yahweh all the words that I have commanded you to speak to them. Do not take away a word.
\v 3 Perhaps they will listen and turn back, a man from his evil way. Then I will relent concerning the disaster that I am planning to do to them from the face of the evil of their deeds.”
\p
\v 4 And you shall say to them, “Thus says Yahweh: ‘If you do not listen to me, to walk in my law that I have given to the face of you,
\v 5 to listen to the words of my servants the prophets whom I am sending to you, rising early and sending, but you have not listened,
\v 6 then I will make this house like Shiloh, and this city I will make as a curse for all the nations of the earth.’”
\p
\v 7 And the priests and the prophets and all the people heard Jeremiah speaking these words in the house of Yahweh.
\v 8 And it happened, when Jeremiah had finished speaking all that Yahweh had commanded {him} to speak to all the people, that the priests and the prophets and all the people seized him, saying, “Dying, you must die!
\v 9 Why have you prophesied in the name of Yahweh, saying, ‘Like Shiloh this house will be, and this city will be desolate, without a dweller’?” And all the people gathered against Jeremiah in the house of Yahweh.
\p
\v 10 And the officials of Judah heard these things, and they went up from the house of the king {to} the house of Yahweh, and they sat down in the entrance of the New Gate of Yahweh.
\v 11 And the priests and the prophets said to the officials and to all the people, saying, “A judgment of death to this man, because he has prophesied against this city, just as you have heard with your ears.”
\p
\v 12 And Jeremiah said to all the officials and to all the people, saying, “Yahweh sent me to prophesy against this house and against this city all the words that you have heard.
\v 13 And now, make good your ways and your deeds, and listen to the voice of Yahweh your God, and Yahweh will relent concerning the disaster that he has spoken against you.
\v 14 And as for me, behold me in your hand. Do to me according to the good and according to the right in your eyes.
\v 15 Only, knowing, you will know that if you are putting me to death, then you are putting innocent blood upon yourselves and upon this city and upon its dwellers, for in truth Yahweh has sent me to you to speak in your ears all these words.”
\p
\v 16 And the officials and all the people said to the priests and to the prophets, “There is no judgment of death for this man, for in the name of Yahweh our God he has spoken to us.”
\v 17 And men from the elders of the land rose up and said to all the assembly of the people, saying,
\v 18 “Micah the Morashtite was prophesying in the days of Hezekiah king of Judah, and he said to all the people of Judah, saying, ‘Thus says Yahweh of Armies:
\q1 Zion, a field will be plowed,
\q1 and Jerusalem will become heaps,
\q1 and the mountain of the house to high places of a forest.’
\p
\v 19 Putting to death, did Hezekiah king of Judah and all Judah put him to death? Did he not fear Yahweh and entreat the face of Yahweh, and Yahweh relented concerning the evil that he had spoken against them? But we are doing great evil against ourselves.”
\p
\v 20 And also there was a man prophesying in the name of Yahweh, Uriah, the son of Shemaiah, from Kiriath Jearim, and he prophesied against this city and against this land according to all the words of Jeremiah.
\v 21 And King Jehoiakim and all his mighty men and all the officials heard his words, and the king sought to put him to death. And Uriah heard, and he was afraid, and he fled and went to Egypt.
\v 22 And King Jehoiakim sent men {to} Egypt -- Elnathan, the son of Akbor, and men with him -- to Egypt.
\v 23 And they brought Uriah out from Egypt, and they brought him to King Jehoiakim, and he struck him with the sword and threw his corpse into the graves of the sons of the people.
\v 24 However, the hand of Ahikam, the son of Shaphan, was with Jeremiah so as not to give him into the hand of the people to put him to death.
\c 27
\p
\v 1 In the beginning of the reign of Jehoiakim, the son of Josiah, king of Judah, this word was to Jeremiah from Yahweh, saying,
\f + \ft Although the Hebrew text reads “Jehoiakim,” the context of this chapter indicates that this prophecy occurred during the reign of Zedekiah.\f*
\v 2 “Thus says Yahweh to me, ‘Make for yourself bonds and yoke bars, and put them on your neck.
\v 3 And send them to the king of Edom, and to the king of Moab, and to the king of the sons of Ammon, and to the king of Tyre, and to the king of Sidon, by the hand of the messengers who are coming {to} Jerusalem to Zedekiah king of Judah.
\v 4 And command them to their masters, saying, “Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: Thus you shall say to your masters,
\v 5 ‘I myself made the earth, the man and the beast that {are} on the face of the earth, by my great strength and by my outstretched arm, and I have given it to whoever is right in my eyes.
\v 6 And now I myself have given all these lands into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, my servant. And also the living creatures of the field I have given to him to serve him.
\v 7 And all the nations will serve him and his son and the son of his son, until the time of his own land comes, even it. And many nations and great kings will make him serve them.
\p
\v 8 And it shall be {that} the nation and the kingdom that will not serve him, Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, and that will not put its neck in the yoke of the king of Babylon, with the sword and with the famine and with the plague, I will visit upon that nation”—the declaration of Yahweh—“until I have finished them by his hand.
\p
\v 9 And as for you, do not listen to your prophets, and to your diviners, and to your dreamers, and to your soothsayers, and to your sorcerers, they who are speaking to you, saying, ‘You shall not serve the king of Babylon.’
\v 10 For they are prophesying falsehood to you, so as to remove you far away from on your land, and I will drive you out, and you will perish.
\v 11 But the nation that brings its neck into the yoke of the king of Babylon and serves him, I will let it rest on its land”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and it will cultivate it and dwell in it.’”’”
\p
\v 12 And to Zedekiah, king of Judah, I spoke according to all these words, saying, “Bring your necks into the yoke of the king of Babylon and serve him and his people, and live.
\v 13 Why will you die, you and your people, by the sword, by the famine, and by the plague, as Yahweh has spoken concerning the nation that does not serve the king of Babylon?
\v 14 And do not listen to the words of the prophets who are speaking to you, saying, ‘You shall not serve the king of Babylon,’ for they are prophesying falsehood to you.
\v 15 For I have not sent them”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and they are prophesying in my name for falsehood, so that I will drive you out, and you will perish, you and the prophets who are prophesying to you.”
\p
\v 16 And to the priests and to all this people I spoke, saying, “Thus says Yahweh: Do not listen to the words of your prophets who are prophesying to you, saying, ‘Behold, the vessels of the house of Yahweh are being brought back from Babylon now, quickly,’ for they are prophesying falsehood to you.
\v 17 Do not listen to them. Serve the king of Babylon and live. Why should this city become a ruin?
\p
\v 18 And if they {are} prophets, and if the word of Yahweh is with them, let them please intercede with Yahweh of Armies, that the vessels remaining in the house of Yahweh and {in} the house of the king of Judah and in Jerusalem not go to Babylon.”
\p
\v 19 For thus says Yahweh of Armies concerning the pillars, and concerning the Sea, and concerning the stands, and concerning the rest of the vessels remaining in this city,
\v 20 which Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon did not take when he exiled Jeconiah, the son of Jehoiakim, king of Judah, from Jerusalem to Babylon, and all the nobles of Judah and Jerusalem—
\v 21 for thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel, concerning the vessels remaining {in} the house of Yahweh and the house of the king of Judah and Jerusalem:
\v 22 “They will be brought to Babylon, and there they will be until the day of my visiting them”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and I will bring them up and restore them to this place.”
\c 28
\p
\v 1 Now it happened in that year, at the beginning of the reign of Zedekiah king of Judah, in the fourth year, in the fifth month, Hananiah, the son of Azzur, the prophet who {was} from Gibeon, said to me in the house of Yahweh, to the eyes of the priests and all the people, saying,
\v 2 “Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel, saying, ‘I have broken the yoke of the king of Babylon.
\v 3 Within two years of days, I, myself, am bringing back to this place all the vessels of the house of Yahweh that Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon took from this place and brought them {to} Babylon.
\v 4 And I, myself, am bringing back to this place Jeconiah, the son of Jehoiakim, king of Judah, and all the exile of Judah, the ones going to Babylon’—the declaration of Yahweh—‘for I will break the yoke of the king of Babylon.’”
\p
\v 5 And Jeremiah the prophet said to Hananiah the prophet, to the eyes of the priests and to the eyes of all the people, the ones standing in the house of Yahweh.
\v 6 And Jeremiah the prophet said, “Amen! Thus may Yahweh do! May Yahweh establish your words that you prophesied, to bring back the vessels of the house of Yahweh and all the exiles from Babylon to this place.
\v 7 But please hear this word that I, myself, am speaking in your ears and in the ears of all the people:
\v 8 The prophets who were to the face of me and to the face of you from ancient time, they prophesied to many lands and against great kingdoms, regarding war and regarding disaster and regarding plague.
\v 9 The prophet who prophesies of peace, when the word of the prophet comes, the prophet will be known {as one} whom Yahweh has sent in truth.”
\p
\v 10 And Hananiah the prophet took the yoke bar from upon the neck of Jeremiah the prophet and broke it.
\v 11 And Hananiah said to the eyes of all the people, saying, “Thus says Yahweh: ‘Like this I will break the yoke of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon within two more years of days from upon the neck of all the nations.’” And Jeremiah the prophet went on his way.
\p
\v 12 And the word of Yahweh came to Jeremiah after Hananiah the prophet broke the yoke bar from upon the neck of Jeremiah the prophet, saying,
\v 13 “Go, and you shall speak to Hananiah, saying, ‘Thus says Yahweh: You have broken yoke bars of wood, but you will make in their place yoke bars of iron.’
\v 14 For thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: ‘I have put a yoke of iron on the neck of all these nations to serve Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, and they will serve him. And also the beast of the field I have given to him.’”
\v 15 And Jeremiah the prophet said to Hananiah the prophet, “Listen, please, Hananiah! Yahweh has not sent you, and you yourself have made this people trust in falsehood.
\v 16 Therefore, thus says Yahweh: ‘Behold, I am sending you away from upon the face of the earth. This year you {are} dying, for you have spoken rebellion against Yahweh.’”
\v 17 And Hananiah the prophet died in that year, in the seventh month.
\c 29
\p
\v 1 And these are the words of the scroll that Jeremiah the prophet sent from Jerusalem to the remainder of the elders of the exile, and to the priests, and to the prophets, and to all the people whom Nebuchadnezzar had exiled from Jerusalem to Babylon,
\v 2 after the going out of Jeconiah the king, and the queen mother, and the eunuchs, the princes of Judah and Jerusalem, and the craftsmen and the smiths, from Jerusalem.
\v 3 {He sent it} by the hand of Elasah, the son of Shaphan, and Gemariah, the son of Hilkiah, whom Zedekiah the king of Judah sent to Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon, to Babylon, saying,
\p
\v 4 “Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel, to all the exile whom I have exiled from Jerusalem to Babylon:
\v 5 ‘Build houses and dwell {in} them, and plant gardens and eat their fruit.
\v 6 Take wives, and father sons and daughters, and take wives for your sons, and give your daughters to men, that they may bear sons and daughters, and multiply there, and you not become few.
\v 7 And seek the peace of the city where I have exiled you there, and pray on behalf of it to Yahweh, for in its peace there shall be peace for you.’
\p
\v 8 For thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: ‘Let your prophets who are in your midst and your diviners not deceive you, and do not listen to your dreams that you are causing {yourselves} to dream.
\v 9 For in falsehood they are prophesying to you in my name. I did not send them”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\p
\v 10 “For thus says Yahweh: ‘When is completed seventy years for Babylon, I will visit you and I will establish my good word concerning you, to bring you back to this place.
\v 11 For I myself know the plans that I am planning concerning you”—the declaration of Yahweh—“plans of peace and not for evil, to give to you a future and a hope.
\v 12 And you will call to me and will go and pray to me, and I will listen to you.
\v 13 And you will seek me and will find {me}, when you seek me with all your heart.
\v 14 And I will be found by you”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and I will restore your captivity and I will gather you from all the nations and from all the places where I have driven you there”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and I will bring you back to the place from where I exiled you.’
\p
\v 15 Because you have said, ‘Yahweh has raised up prophets for us in Babylon,’
\v 16 for thus says Yahweh concerning the king who is sitting on the throne of David and concerning all the people who are dwelling in this city, your brothers who did not go out with you into the exile\ p —
\v 17 thus says Yahweh of Armies: ‘Behold, I am sending against them the sword, the famine, and the plague, and I will make them like the horrible figs that cannot be eaten from badness.
\v 18 And I will pursue after them with the sword, with the famine, and with the plague, and I will make them to {be} a horror to all the kingdoms of the earth, a curse and a desolation and a hissing and a reproach among all the nations where I have driven them,
\v 19 because they did not listen to my words“—the declaration of Yahweh—“that I sent to them {by} my servants the prophets, rising early and sending, but you did not listen“—the declaration of Yahweh.
\p
\v 20 “But you, hear the word of Yahweh, all the exile whom I have sent away from Jerusalem to Babylon.
\v 21 Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel, concerning Ahab, the son of Kolaiah, and concerning Zedekiah, the son of Maaseiah, who are prophesying to you in my name falsehood: ‘Behold, I am giving them into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon, and he will strike them before your eyes.
\v 22 And because of them a curse shall be taken up by all the exile of Judah who are in Babylon, saying, “May Yahweh make you like Zedekiah and like Ahab, whom the king of Babylon roasted in the fire,”
\v 23 because they have done a shameful thing in Israel and they have committed adultery with the wives of their neighbors, and they spoke a word in my name falsely that I did not command them. And I am the one knowing and {am} a witness’— the declaration of Yahweh.”
\p
\v 24 And to Shemaiah the Nehelamite you shall speak, saying,
\v 25 “Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel, saying: ‘Because you sent letters in your name to all the people who are in Jerusalem, and to Zephaniah, the son of Maaseiah the priest, and to all the priests, saying,
\v 26 “Yahweh has made you priest in place of Jehoiada the priest, to be overseers {in} the house of Yahweh over every man who is raving and making himself a prophet, and you shall put him in the stocks and in the neck iron.
\v 27 And now, why have you not rebuked Jeremiah the Anathothite, the one making himself a prophet to you?
\v 28 For on account of this he has sent to us in Babylon, saying, ‘It {will} be long. Build houses and dwell {in} them, and plant gardens and eat their fruit.’”’”
\v 29 And Zephaniah the priest read this letter in the ears of Jeremiah the prophet.
\p
\v 30 And the word of Yahweh came to Jeremiah, saying,
\v 31 “Send to all the exile, saying, ‘Thus says Yahweh concerning Shemaiah the Nehelamite: Because Shemaiah has prophesied to you, and I did not send him, and he made you trust upon falsehood,
\v 32 therefore thus says Yahweh: Behold, I am visiting {punishment} upon Shemaiah the Nehelamite and upon his seed. There will not be for him a man dwelling in the midst of this people, and he will not see the good that I am doing for my people’—the declaration of Yahweh—‘for he has spoken rebellion against Yahweh.’”
\c 30
\p
\v 1 The word that came to Jeremiah from Yahweh, saying,
\v 2 “Thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel, saying, ‘Write for yourself in a scroll all of the words that I have spoken to you.
\v 3 For behold, days are coming“—the declaration of Yahweh—“when I will restore the captivity of my people Israel and Judah,” says Yahweh, “and I will bring them back to the land that I gave to their fathers, and they will possess it.’”
\p
\v 4 And these are the words that Yahweh spoke concerning Israel and to Judah.
\v 5 For thus says Yahweh,
\q1 “We have heard a voice of trembling,
\q2 dread, and there is no peace.
\q1 \v 6 Ask, please, and see
\q2 can a male be giving birth?
\q1 Why do I see every strong man
\q2 {with} his hands on his loins
\q2 like a woman giving birth?
\q1 And all of the faces have turned
\q2 to paleness.
\q1 \v 7 Woe! For great is that day,
\q2 there is none like it.
\q1 And it is a time of distress for Jacob,
\q2 but he will be saved from it.
\q1 \v 8 And it will be on that day“—the declaration of Yahweh of Armies—“I will break his yoke from upon your neck, and I will tear apart your bonds, and foreigners will no longer enslave him.
\q1 \v 9 And they will serve Yahweh their God and David their king, whom I will raise up for them.
\q1 \v 10 And you, my servant Jacob, do not fear,”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and do not be dismayed, Israel, for behold, I am saving you from afar, and your offspring from the land of their captivity. And Jacob shall return and shall have quiet and shall be at ease, and there shall be no one making him afraid.
\q1 \v 11 For I am with you”—the declaration of Yahweh—“to save you. For I will make a complete end of all of the nations where I have scattered you, but you I will not make a complete end of. And I will discipline you according to justice, but acquitting, I will not acquit you.”
\q1 \v 12 For thus says Yahweh, “Incurable is your breaking;
\q2 grievous is your wound.
\q1 \v 13 There is no one pleading your cause,
\q2 {nor} for the wound medicines,
\q2 there is no healing for you.
\q1 \v 14 All of your lovers have forgotten you;
\q2 they do not seek you.
\q1 For I have struck you {with} the striking of an enemy,
\q2 {with} the discipline of a cruel one,
\q1 because of the abundance of your iniquity;
\q2 your sins are numerous.
\q1 \v 15 Why do you cry out over your breaking,
\q2 your pain {that is} incurable?
\q1 Because of the abundance of your iniquity,
\q2 your sins are numerous;
\q2 I have done these {things} to you.
\q1 \v 16 Therefore, all of the ones devouring you will be devoured,
\q2 and all of your adversaries, every one of them,
\q2 shall go into captivity.
\q1 And the ones plundering you shall be as plunder,
\q2 and all of the ones preying upon you I will give as spoil.
\q1 \v 17 For I will bring up healing for you,
\q2 and I will heal you from your wounds”—
\q2 the declaration of Yahweh—
\q1 “for they have called you an outcast:
\q2 ‘She {is} Zion; no one is seeking her.’”
\q1 \v 18 Thus says Yahweh, “Behold, I am restoring the fortune of the tents of Jacob,
\q2 and I will have compassion on his dwelling places.
\q1 And the city shall be built on its mound,
\q2 and the palace shall sit on its proper place.
\q1 \v 19 And thanksgiving will go out from them,
\q2 and the voice of those laughing.
\q1 And I will multiply them, and they will not become few;
\q2 and I will make them honored, and they will not become insignificant.
\q1 \v 20 And his sons will be as {in} former times,
\q2 and his congregation will be established to my face,
\q2 and I will punish all of his oppressors.
\q1 \v 21 And his majestic one will be from among him,
\q2 and his ruler will come from his midst.
\q1 And I will bring him near, and he will approach me,
\q2 for who is this who has pledged his heart
\q2 to approach me?”—
\q2 the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 22 “And you will be to me for a people,
\q2 and I will be to you for God.”
\q1 \v 23 Behold, the storm of Yahweh,
\q2 wrath, has gone out,
\q1 a whirling storm;
\q2 upon the head of the wicked it will whirl.
\q1 \v 24 The burning of the nose of Yahweh will not turn back
\q2 until his doing and until his establishing
\q2 the plans of his heart.
\q1 In the latter days,
\q2 you will understand it.
\c 31
\p
\v 1 “At that time”—the declaration of Yahweh—“I will be for God to all of the clans of Israel, and they will be my people.”
\v 2 Thus says Yahweh, “A people, the survivors of the sword, found favor in the wilderness, Israel, going to find his rest.”
\q1 \v 3 From far off Yahweh appeared to me, “And with everlasting love I have loved you.
\q2 Therefore I have drawn you out with covenant faithfulness.
\q1 \v 4 Again I will build you, and you will be built, virgin of Israel.
\q2 Again you will adorn yourself with your tambourines, and you will go out in the dance of the ones laughing.
\q1 \v 5 Again you will plant vineyards on the mountains of Samaria;
\q2 the planters will plant and will treat them as common.
\q1 \v 6 For there will be a day when the watchers on the mountain of Ephraim will call out,
\q2 ‘Arise, and let us go up to Zion, to Yahweh our God.’”
\p
\v 7 For thus says Yahweh, “Sing for Jacob with gladness, and shout for the chief of the nations. Cause it to be heard, praise, and say, ‘Yahweh, save your people, the remnant of Israel.’
\q1 \v 8 Behold, I am bringing them from the land of the north, and I will gather them from the remote parts of the earth. Among them are the blind and the lame, the pregnant woman and the one giving birth, together; a great assembly will return here.
\q1 \v 9 With weeping they will come, and with pleas for mercy I will lead them. I will cause them to walk to streams of water on a straight road; they will not stumble on it. For I have become a father for Israel, and Ephraim, he is my firstborn.
\q1 \v 10 Hear the word of Yahweh, nations, and declare it in the coastlands from far off, and say, ‘The one scattering Israel will gather him, and he will protect him like a shepherd of his flock.’
\q1 \v 11 For Yahweh has ransomed Jacob and redeemed him from the hand of one stronger than he.
\q1 \v 12 And they will come and will sing on the height of Zion, and they will be radiant to the goodness of Yahweh, over the grain, and over the new wine, and over the fresh oil, and over the young of the flock and the herd. And their soul will be like a watered garden, and they will not continue to languish again.
\q1 \v 13 Then the virgin will rejoice with dancing, and the young men and the old together. And I will turn their mourning to joy, and I will comfort them and I will cause them to rejoice rather than sorrow.
\q1 \v 14 And I will saturate the soul of the priests with fatness, and my people will be satisfied with my goodness— the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 15 Thus says Yahweh, “A voice is heard in Ramah, wailing, the weeping of bitterness. Rachel is weeping over her sons; she refuses to be comforted over her sons, for he is no more.”
\q1 \v 16 Thus says Yahweh, “Withhold your voice from weeping and your eyes from tears, for there is a reward for your work”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and they will return from the land of the enemy.
\q1 \v 17 And there is hope for your future”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and will return {your} sons to their border.
\q1 \v 18 Hearing I have heard Ephraim bemoaning himself, ‘You disciplined me, and I was disciplined like a calf not trained. Cause me to return, and I will return, for you are Yahweh my God.
\q1 \v 19 For after my turning away I repented, and after my being made to know I struck on my thigh. I was ashamed and also I was humiliated, for I bore the reproach of my youth.’
\q1 \v 20 Is Ephraim a precious son to me, or a child of delights? For as often as I speak against him, remembering I remember him. still Therefore my inward parts groan for him; having compassion I will have compassion on him”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 21 Set up for yourself signposts; place for yourself markers. Set your heart to the highway, the road you walked. Return, virgin of Israel; return to these your cities.
\q1 \v 22 Until when will you waver, apostate daughter? For Yahweh has created a new thing in the land: a female will surround a man protectively.”
\p
\v 23 Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel, “Again they will say this word in the land of Judah and in its cities, when I restore their fortune, ‘May bless you, Yahweh dwelling place of righteousness, mountain of holiness.’
\v 24 And in it Judah and all its cities together will dwell, farmers and those who set out with the flock.
\v 25 For I will saturate the weary soul, and every languishing soul I will fill.”
\v 26 At this I awoke and looked, and my sleep was pleasant to me.
\p
\v 27 “Behold, days are coming”—the declaration of Yahweh—“when I will sow the house of Israel and the house of Judah {with} the seed of man and the seed of beast.
\v 28 And it will be, just as I watched over them to uproot and to tear down, and to overthrow and to destroy and to bring harm, thus I will watch over them to build and to plant”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\v 29 In those days they will no longer say,
\q1 ‘Fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the teeth of the children will become blunt.’
\q1 \v 30 But a man will die in his own iniquity; every man who eats the sour grapes, his teeth will become blunt.
\q1 \v 31 Behold, days are coming”—the declaration of Yahweh—“when I will cut a covenant new with the house of Israel and with the house of Judah.
\q1 \v 32 It will not be like the covenant that I cut with their fathers on the day of my taking hold of their hand to bring them out from the land of Egypt, which they, they broke— my covenant— although I was a husband to them”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 33 For this is the covenant that I will cut with the house of Israel after those days— the declaration of Yahweh: I will put my law in their inward parts, and on their heart I will write it; and I will be God for them, and they will be a people for me.
\q1 \v 34 And no longer will a man teach his neighbor and a man his brother, saying, ‘Know Yahweh,’ for all of them will know me, from the smallest of them to the greatest of them”—the declaration of Yahweh—“for I will forgive their iniquity, and their sin I will no longer remember.”
\q1 \v 35 Thus says Yahweh, the one giving the sun for light by day, the statutes of the moon and the stars for light by night, the one stirring up the sea its waves so that roar, Yahweh of Armies is his name:
\q1 \v 36 “If these statutes depart from before me”—the declaration of Yahweh—“then also the seed of Israel will cease from being a nation before me all the days.”
\q1 \v 37 Thus says Yahweh, “If the heavens above can be measured and can be searched out, the foundations of the earth below then also I myself will reject all the seed of Israel for all that they have done”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\p
\v 38 “Behold, days {are coming}”—the declaration of Yahweh—“when the city will be built for Yahweh, from the Tower of Hananel to the Gate of the Corner.
\v 39 And will go out the measuring line again straight over the hill of Gareb, and then turn toward Goah.
\v 40 And all the valley of the corpses and the ashes, and all the fields as far as the brook of Kidron, to the corner of the Gate of the Horses toward the east, {will be} holiness to Yahweh. It will not be uprooted, and it will not be overthrown again, forever.”
\c 32
\p
\v 1 The word that came to Jeremiah from Yahweh in the tenth year of Zedekiah the king of Judah—that was the eighteenth year of Nebuchadnezzar.
\v 2 And at that time the army of the king of Babylon was laying siege to Jerusalem, and Jeremiah the prophet was shut up in the courtyard of the guard that {was in} the house of the king of Judah,
\v 3 where Zedekiah the king of Judah had shut him up, saying, “Why are you prophesying, saying, ‘Thus says Yahweh: Behold me, I am giving this city into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he will capture it.
\v 4 And Zedekiah the king of Judah will not escape from the hand of the Chaldeans, for being given he will be given into the hand of the king of Babylon, and his mouth will speak with his mouth, and his eyes will see his eyes.
\v 5 And he will make Zedekiah go to Babylon, and there he will be until I visit him”—the declaration of Yahweh.—“Though you fight against the Chaldeans, you will not succeed’?”
\p
\v 6 And Jeremiah said, “The word of Yahweh came to me, saying,
\v 7 ‘Behold, Hanamel the son of Shallum your uncle is coming to you, saying, “Buy for yourself my field that is in Anathoth, for to you belongs the right of redemption, to buy {it}.”’
\v 8 And Hanamel the son of my uncle came to me, according to the word of Yahweh, to the courtyard of the guard, and he said to me, ‘Buy, please, my field that is in Anathoth, which is in the land of Benjamin, for to you belongs the right of possession, and to you the redemption. Buy it for yourself.’ Then I knew that this was the word of Yahweh.
\v 9 So I bought the field from Hanamel the son of my uncle, which was in Anathoth, and I weighed out for him the silver, seven shekels and ten of the silver.
\v 10 And I wrote in the document and sealed it and I caused to testify witnesses, and I weighed the silver on the scales.
\v 11 Then I took the document of the purchase—the one being sealed the commandment and the terms, and the one being open—
\v 12 and I gave the document of the purchase to Baruch the son of Neriah, the son of Mahseiah, before the eyes of Hanamel of my uncle and before the eyes of the witnesses, the ones signing on the document of the purchase, before the eyes of all the Judeans who were sitting in the courtyard of the guard.
\v 13 And I commanded Baruch before their eyes, saying,
\v 14 ‘Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: Take these documents, this document of the purchase, {both} the sealed one and this open document, and put them in a vessel of earthenware, so that they may stand many days.
\v 15 For thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: Houses and fields and vineyards will again be bought in this land.’
\p
\v 16 And I prayed to Yahweh after I gave the document of the purchase to Baruch the son of Neriah, saying,
\v 17 Ah, Lord Yahweh! Behold, you yourself made the heavens and the earth by your great power and by your outstretched arm. Nothing is too difficult for you,
\v 18 doing covenant faithfulness to thousands and repaying the iniquity of fathers into the bosom of their sons after them, the great {and} mighty God—Yahweh of Armies is his name—
\v 19 great in counsel and abounding in deed, whose eyes are open to all of the ways of the sons of man, to give to each according to his ways and according to the fruit of his deeds,
\v 20 who have set signs and wonders in the land of Egypt, until this day, and in Israel and among mankind, and you made for yourself a name, as at this day.
\v 21 And you brought out your people Israel from the land of Egypt with signs and with wonders and with a strong hand and with an outstretched arm and with great terror.
\v 22 And you gave to them this land, which you swore to their fathers to give to them, a land flowing with milk and honey.
\v 23 And they entered and took possession of it, but they did not listen to your voice, and in your law they did not walk. All that you commanded them to do, they have not done, and so you caused all this calamity to happen to them.
\v 24 Behold, the siege mounds have come to the city to capture it, and the city has been given into the hand of the Chaldeans who are fighting against it, because of the face of the sword and the famine and the plague. And what you spoke has happened, and behold, you are seeing it.
\v 25 Yet you yourself said to me, Lord Yahweh, “Buy for yourself the field with silver and cause witnesses to testify,” though the city has been given into the hand of the Chaldeans.’”
\p
\v 26 And the word of Yahweh came to Jeremiah, saying,
\v 27 “Behold, I am Yahweh, the God of all flesh. Is anything too difficult for me?
\v 28 Therefore, thus says Yahweh: Behold, I am giving this city into the hand of the Chaldeans and into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon, and he will capture it.
\v 29 And the Chaldeans who are fighting against this city will come and they will set this city on fire and they will burn it, and the houses on whose roofs they made smoke to Baal and poured out drink offerings to other gods, so as to provoke me.
\v 30 For the sons of Israel and the sons of Judah have only been doing evil in my eyes from their youth, for the sons of Israel have surely been provoking me with the work of their hands”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\v 31 For this city has been to me to my nose and to my heat from the day that they built it and until this day, so that I should remove it from by my face,
\v 32 because of all the evil of the sons of Israel and the sons of Judah that they did to provoke me to anger—they, their kings, their officials, their priests, and their prophets, and the men of Judah and the dwellers of Jerusalem.
\v 33 And they turned to me the neck and not the face, and though teaching them, rising early and teaching, but they were not listening to receive instruction.
\v 34 And they set their detestable things in the house which is called by my name, to defile it.
\v 35 And they built the high places of Baal that are in the Valley of Ben Hinnom, to make their sons and their daughters pass through to Molech, which I did not command them, and it did not come up to my heart, to do this abomination, so as to cause Judah to sin.
\p
\v 36 And now, therefore, thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel, concerning this city, about which you are saying, ‘It has been given into the hand of the king of Babylon by the sword and by the famine and by the plague’:
\v 37 Behold me, gathering them from all the lands where I drove them there in my anger and in my heat and in great fury, and I will bring them back to this place and cause them to dwell in security.
\v 38 And they will be my people, and I myself will be their God.
\v 39 And I will give to them one heart and one way, to fear me all the days, for good to them and to their sons after them.
\v 40 And I will cut for them an everlasting covenant, that I will not turn back from after them, to do good to them, and I will put my fear in their heart, so that they will not turn aside from by me.
\v 41 And I will rejoice over them to do good to them, and I will plant them in this land in faithfulness, with all my heart and with all my being.
\p
\v 42 For thus says Yahweh: Just as I brought to this people all this great calamity, so I am bringing upon them all the good that I am speaking concerning them.
\v 43 And the field will be bought in this land, about which you are saying, “It is a desolation, without man and beast; it has been given into the hand of the Chaldeans.”
\v 44 They will buy fields with the silver, and writing in the document and sealing and making witnesses witness, in the land of Benjamin and in the surroundings of Jerusalem and in the cities of Judah and in the cities of the hill country and in the cities of the foothills and in the cities of the Negev, for I will bring back their captivity”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\c 33
\p
\v 1 And the word of Yahweh came to Jeremiah a second time, while he was still confined in the courtyard of the guard, saying,
\v 2 “Thus says Yahweh its maker, Yahweh forming it to establish it—Yahweh is his name:
\v 3 ‘Call to me, and I will answer you, and I will declare to you great things and inaccessible things that you have not known.’
\v 4 For thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel, concerning the houses of this city and concerning the houses of the kings of Judah that are torn down against the siege ramps and against the sword:
\v 5 ‘They are coming to fight with the Chaldeans and to fill them with the corpses of the man whom in my nose and in my heat I will strike, and whom I have hidden my face from this city on account of all their wickedness.
\v 6 Behold me bringing up for it healing and a cure, and I will heal them, and I will reveal to them an abundance of peace and truth.
\v 7 And I will restore the captivity of Judah and the captivity of Israel, and I will build them as at the first.
\v 8 And I will cleanse them from all their iniquity that they have sinned against me, and I will forgive all their iniquities that they have sinned against me and by which they transgressed against me.
\v 9 And it will be for me a name of joy, for praise, and for glory before all the nations of the earth who will hear of all the good that I am doing for them. And they will fear and tremble over all the good and over all the peace that I am doing for it.’
\p
\v 10 Thus says Yahweh: ‘Again there will be heard in this place—about which you are saying, “It is a waste, without man and without beast”—in the cities of Judah and in the streets of Jerusalem that are desolate, without man and without inhabitant and without beast,
\v 11 the voice of joy and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride, the voice of the ones saying, “Give thanks to Yahweh of Armies, for Yahweh is good, for his covenant faithfulness is forever,” the ones bringing thanksgiving {to} the house of Yahweh. For I will restore the captivity of the land as at the first,’ says Yahweh.
\p
\v 12 Thus says Yahweh of Armies: ‘Again there will be in this place, the desolate one— without man and without beast— and in all its cities a pasture of shepherds causing {their} flock to lie down.
\v 13 In the cities of the hill country, in the cities of the foothills, and in the cities of the Negev, and in the land of Benjamin, and in the surroundings of Jerusalem, and in the cities of Judah, again the flock will pass under the hands of the one counting {them},’ says Yahweh.
\p
\v 14 Behold, days are coming”—the declaration of Yahweh—“when I will establish the good word that I spoke to the house of Israel and to the house of Judah.
\v 15 In those days and in that time I will cause to sprout for David a branch of righteousness, and he will do justice and righteousness in the land.
\v 16 In those days Judah will be saved, and Jerusalem will dwell in security, and this {is} what {one} will call her: “Yahweh {is} our righteousness.”
\v 17 For thus says Yahweh: ‘A man will not be cut off for David sitting on the throne of the house of Israel,
\v 18 nor will the priests, the Levites, a man will not be cut off from before my face, raising a burnt offering, and burning a grain offering, and making a sacrifice all the days.’”
\p
\v 19 And the word of Yahweh came to Jeremiah, saying,
\v 20 “Thus says Yahweh: ‘If you break my covenant of the day and my covenant of the night, so that day and night do not come in their time,
\v 21 then also my covenant will be broken with David my servant, from there being for him a son reigning on his throne, and {with} the Levites, the priests, my ministers.
\v 22 As the host of the heavens cannot be counted, and the sand of the sea cannot be measured, so I will multiply the seed of David my servant and the Levites, the ones ministering to me.’”
\p
\v 23 And the word of Yahweh came to Jeremiah, saying,
\v 24 “Have you not seen what this people has spoken, saying, ‘The two families that Yahweh chose in them, now he has rejected them’? Thus my people they despise from being a nation anymore before their face.
\v 25 Thus says Yahweh: ‘If {I have} not established my covenant of day and night, {and if} I have not set the statutes of heaven and earth,
\v 26 then also the seed of Jacob and David my servant I will reject, from taking rulers from his seed over the seed of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. For I will restore their fortunes and I will have compassion on them.’”
\c 34
\p
\v 1 The word that came to Jeremiah from Yahweh, when Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon and all his army and all the kingdoms of the earth, the dominion of his hand, and all the peoples were fighting against Jerusalem and against all her cities, saying:
\v 2 “Thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel: Go and speak to Zedekiah king of Judah and say to him, ‘Thus says Yahweh: Behold me giving this city into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he will burn it with fire.
\v 3 And you, you will not escape from his hand, but capturing you will be captured, and into his hand you will be given. And your eyes will see the eyes of the king of Babylon, and his mouth will speak with your mouth, and to Babylon you will go.’
\v 4 Yet hear the word of Yahweh, Zedekiah king of Judah! Thus says Yahweh concerning you: ‘You will not die by the sword.
\v 5 In peace you will die, and like the burnings of your fathers, the former kings who were before you, so they will burn for you, “Alas, master!” they will mourn for you, for I myself have spoken a word—the declaration of Yahweh.’”
\p
\v 6 And Jeremiah the prophet spoke to Zedekiah king of Judah all these words in Jerusalem,
\v 7 while the army of the king of Babylon was fighting against Jerusalem and against all the remaining cities of Judah, against Lachish and against Azekah, for these remained among the cities of Judah as cities of fortification.
\p
\v 8 The word that came to Jeremiah from Yahweh after King Zedekiah cut a covenant with all the people who were in Jerusalem, to proclaim freedom to them,
\v 9 to send away, a man his male servant and a man his female servant, the Hebrew man and the Hebrew woman, free, to not serve by them—on a Jew, his brother man.
\v 10 And all the officials and all the people who had entered into the covenant obeyed—to send away, a man his male servant and a man his female servant, free, to not serve by them any longer—and they obeyed and sent them away.
\v 11 But afterward they turned back and brought back the male servants and the female servants whom they had sent away free, and they subjected them as male servants and as female servants.
\p
\v 12 And the word of Yahweh came to Jeremiah from Yahweh, saying:
\v 13 “Thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel: I myself cut a covenant with your fathers in the day of my bringing them out from the land of Egypt, from the house of slaves, saying,
\v 14 ‘At the end of seven years you shall send away a man his brother, the Hebrew who has been sold to you and has served you six years, then you shall send him away free from you.’ But your fathers did not listen to me, and they did not incline their ear.
\v 15 And you yourselves turned today and did what is right in my eyes, to proclaim freedom, a man to his neighbor, and you cut a covenant to my face, in the house over which my name is called.
\v 16 But you turned and profaned my name, and you brought back, a man his male servant and a man his female servant whom you had sent away free for themselves, and you subjected them to be for you for male servants and for female servants.
\p
\v 17 Therefore thus says Yahweh: You, yourselves have not listened to me to proclaim freedom, a man to his brother and a man to his neighbor. Behold me proclaiming freedom to you”—the declaration of Yahweh—“to the sword, to the plague, and to the famine, and I will make you a horror to all the kingdoms of the earth.
\v 18 And I will give the men transgressing my covenant, who did not establish the words of the covenant that they cut before my face—the calf that they cut in two and passed between its pieces—
\v 19 the officials of Judah and the officials of Jerusalem, the eunuchs and the priests, and all the people of the land, the ones passing between the pieces of the calf—
\v 20 and I will give them into the hand of their enemies and into the hand of the ones seeking their life, and their corpse will be as food for the birds of the heavens and for the beasts of the earth.
\v 21 And Zedekiah king of Judah and his officials I will give into the hand of their enemies and into the hand of the ones seeking their life, and into the hand of the army of the king of Babylon, the ones withdrawing from against you.
\v 22 Behold me, commanding,”—the declaration of Yahweh—“and I will bring them back to this city, and they will fight against it and capture it and burn it with fire. And the cities of Judah I will make a desolation, without inhabitant.”
\c 35
\p
\v 1 The word that came to Jeremiah from Yahweh in the days of Jehoiakim son of Josiah, king of Judah, saying,
\v 2 “Go to the house of the Rechabites and speak with them, and bring them to the house of Yahweh, into one of the chambers, and give them wine to drink.”
\v 3 So I took Jaazaniah son of Jeremiah son of Habazziniah, and his brothers, and all his sons, and all the house of the Rechabites,
\v 4 and I brought them to the house of Yahweh, into the chamber of the sons of Hanan son of Igdaliah, the man of God, which was beside the chamber of the officials, which was above the chamber of Maaseiah son of Shallum, the keeper of the threshold.
\v 5 Then I set before the sons of the house of the Rechabites bowls full of wine, and cups, and I said to them, “Drink wine.”
\v 6 But they said, “We will not drink wine, for Jonadab son of Rechab, our father, commanded us, saying, ‘You shall not drink wine, you nor your sons, forever.
\v 7 And you shall not build a house, and you shall not sow seed, and you shall not plant a vineyard, and it shall not be to you; but in tents you shall dwell all your days, so that you may live many days on the face of the ground where you are sojourning there.’
\v 8 And we have listened to the voice of Jonadab son of Rechab, our father, in all that he commanded us, to not drink wine all our days, we, our wives, our sons, and our daughters,
\v 9 and to not build houses for us to inhabit, and there will not be to us vineyard or field or seed.
\v 10 But we have dwelt in tents, and we have listened and done according to all that Jonadab our father commanded us.
\v 11 But it happened, when Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon came up against the land, that we said, ‘Come, and let us go to Jerusalem from before the face of the army of the Chaldeans and from before the face of the army of Aram.’ So we dwell in Jerusalem.”
\p
\v 12 Then the word of Yahweh came to Jeremiah, saying,
\v 13 “Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: ‘Go and say to the man of Judah and to the inhabitants of Jerusalem, “Will you not receive correction to listen to my words?”— the declaration of Yahweh—
\v 14 “The words of Jonadab son of Rechab that he commanded his sons, to not drink wine, have been carried out, and they have not drunk until this day, for they have listened to the commandment of their father. But I myself have spoken to you, rising early and speaking, but you have not listened to me.
\v 15 And I have sent to you all my servants the prophets, rising early and sending {them}, saying, ‘Turn now, a man from his evil way, and make your deeds good, and do not walk after other gods to serve them, and dwell in the land that I gave to you and to your fathers.’ But you did not incline your ear, and you did not listen to me.
\v 16 For the sons of Jonadab son of Rechab have carried out the commandment of their father that he commanded them, but this people has not listened to me.”’
\v 17 Therefore, thus says Yahweh, the God of Armies, the God of Israel: ‘Behold me bringing to Judah and to all the inhabitants of Jerusalem all the disaster that I have spoken against them, because I spoke to them but they did not listen, and I called to them but they did not answer.’”
\p
\v 18 And to the house of the Rechabites Jeremiah said, “Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: Because you have listened to the commandment of Jonadab your father, and have kept all his commandments, and have done according to all that he commanded you,
\v 19 therefore thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: ‘A man for Jonadab son of Rechab shall not be cut off to stand before my face all the days.’”
\c 36
\p
\v 1 Now it happened in the fourth year of Jehoiakim son of Josiah, king of Judah, that this word came to Jeremiah from Yahweh, saying,
\v 2 “Take for yourself a scroll of a book, and you shall write on it all the words that I have spoken to you concerning Israel and concerning Judah and concerning all the nations, from the day I spoke to you, from the days of Josiah and until this day.
\v 3 Perhaps the house of Judah will hear all the evil that I am planning to do to them, so that they may turn back, a man from his evil way, and I will forgive their iniquity and their sin.”
\p
\v 4 And Jeremiah called Baruch son of Neriah, and Baruch wrote from the mouth of Jeremiah all the words of Yahweh that he had spoken to him on a scroll of a book.
\v 5 And Jeremiah commanded Baruch, saying, “I am restrained; I am not able to enter the house of Yahweh.
\v 6 And you yourself shall go and shall call out from the scroll what you have written from my mouth, the words of Yahweh, in the ears of the people{in} the house of Yahweh on the day of fasting. And also in the ears of all Judah, the ones coming from their cities, you shall read them.
\v 7 Perhaps their supplication will fall to the face of Yahweh, and they will turn back, a man from his evil way; for great is the nose and the heat that Yahweh has spoken against this people.”
\v 8 And Baruch son of Neriah did according to all that Jeremiah the prophet had commanded him, to read in the book the words of Yahweh {in} the house of Yahweh.
\p
\v 9 Now it happened in the fifth year of Jehoiakim son of Josiah, king of Judah, in the ninth month, {that} all the people in Jerusalem and all the people, the ones coming from the cities of Judah into Jerusalem, proclaimed a fast to the face of Yahweh.
\v 10 And Baruch called out from the book of the words of Jeremiah {in} the house of Yahweh, in the chamber of Gemariah son of Shaphan the scribe, in the upper courtyard, {at} the opening of the New Gate of the house of Yahweh, in the ears of all the people.
\p
\v 11 And Micaiah son of Gemariah son of Shaphan heard all the words of Yahweh from upon the book.
\v 12 And he went down to the house of the king, into the chamber of the scribe, and behold, all the officials were sitting there: Elishama the scribe, and Delaiah son of Shemaiah, and Elnathan son of Akbor, and Gemariah son of Shaphan, and Zedekiah son of Hananiah, and all the officials.
\v 13 And Micaiah declared to them all the words that he had heard when Baruch read in the book in the ears of the people.
\v 14 Then all the officials sent Jehudi son of Nethaniah son of Shelemiah son of Cushi to Baruch, saying, “The scroll that you read in it in the ears of the people—take it in your hand and go.” So Baruch son of Neriah took the scroll in his hand and came to them.
\v 15 And they said to him, “Sit, please, and read it in our ears.” And Baruch read in their ears.
\v 16 And it happened, when they heard all the words, that they expressed dread, a man to his neighbor, and they said to Baruch, “Reporting we must report all these words to the king.”
\v 17 Then they asked Baruch, saying, “Declare, please, to us: How did you write all these words from his mouth?”
\v 18 And Baruch said to them, “From his mouth he called to me all these words, and I was writing upon the book with ink.”
\v 19 Then the officials said to Baruch, “Go, hide yourself, you and Jeremiah, and let a man not know where you {are}.”
\p
\v 20 And they went to the king, into the courtyard, and the scroll they deposited in the chamber of Elishama the scribe. And they reported in the ears of the king all of the words.
\v 21 Then the king sent Jehudi to take the scroll, and he took it from the chamber of Elishama the scribe. And Jehudi read it in the ears of the king and in the ears of all the officials, the ones standing beside the king.
\v 22 And the king was sitting in the house of the winter, in the ninth month, and the brazier was burning to his face.
\v 23 And it happened, as Jehudi read three or four columns, that he would cut it up in pieces with the knife of a scribe and throw {it} into the fire that was on the brazier, until all the scroll was finished on the fire that was on the brazier.
\v 24 And they were not afraid, and they did not tear their garments—the king and all his servants, the ones hearing all these words.
\v 25 And even Elnathan and Delaiah and Gemariah interceded with the king not to burn the scroll, but he did not listen to them.
\v 26 And the king commanded Jerahmeel son of the king, and Seraiah son of Azriel, and Shelemiah son of Abdeel, to seize Baruch the scribe and Jeremiah the prophet, but Yahweh hid them.
\p
\v 27 And the word of Yahweh came to Jeremiah after the king burned the scroll and the words that Baruch wrote from the mouth of Jeremiah, saying,
\v 28 “Return, take for yourself another scroll, and write on it all the former words that were on the first scroll, which Jehoiakim king of Judah burned.
\v 29 And concerning Jehoiakim king of Judah you shall say, ‘Thus says Yahweh: “You yourself have burned this scroll, saying, ‘Why did you write on it, saying, coming, the king of Babylon will come and destroy this land, and he will cause man and beast to cease from it?’”
\v 30 Therefore thus says Yahweh concerning Jehoiakim king of Judah: ‘There will not be to him one sitting on the throne of David, and his dead body will be thrown to the heat by the day and to the frost by the night.
\v 31 And I will bring punishment upon him and on his seed and on his servants {for} their iniquity, and I will bring upon them and upon the dwellers of Jerusalem and to a man of Judah all the evil that I have spoken to them, but they did not listen.’”
\v 32 Then Jeremiah took another scroll and gave it to Baruch son of Neriah the scribe, and he wrote on it from the mouth of Jeremiah all the words of the book that Jehoiakim king of Judah burned in the fire. And besides {these}, many words like them were added to them.
\c 37
\p
\v 1 And king Zedekiah son of Josiah reigned in place of Coniah son of Jehoiakim, whom Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon made king in the land of Judah.
\v 2 But he did not listen, he and his servants and the people of the land, to the words of Yahweh that he spoke by the hand of Jeremiah the prophet.
\v 3 And King Zedekiah sent Jehucal son of Shelemiah and Zephaniah son of Maaseiah the priest to Jeremiah the prophet, saying, “Pray, please, on behalf of us to Yahweh our God.”
\v 4 Now Jeremiah was coming and going in the midst of the people, and they had not put him {in} the house of imprisonment.
\v 5 And the army of Pharaoh had come out from Egypt, and the Chaldeans, the ones laying siege against Jerusalem, had heard their report and they withdrew from upon Jerusalem.
\p
\v 6 And the word of Yahweh came to Jeremiah the prophet, saying,
\v 7 “Thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel: Thus you shall say to the king of Judah, the one sending you to me to inquire of me: ‘Behold, the army of Pharaoh, the one coming out to you to help, will return to its own land, Egypt.
\v 8 And the Chaldeans will return and fight against this city, and they will capture it and burn it with fire.’
\v 9 Thus says Yahweh: ‘Do not deceive yourselves, saying, “Going, the Chaldeans will go from upon us,” for they will not go.
\v 10 For even if you had struck all the army of the Chaldeans, the ones fighting with you, and pierced men were left among them, a man in his tent, they would rise up and burn this city with fire.’”
\v 11 And it happened, when the army of the Chaldeans went up from upon Jerusalem from the face of the army of Pharaoh,
\p
\v 12 that Jeremiah went out from Jerusalem to go {to} the land of Benjamin, to divide {a share} from there in the midst of the people.
\v 13 And it happened, while he was at the Gate of Benjamin, that a captain of the guard {was} there, and his name was Irijah son of Shelemiah son of Hananiah, and he seized Jeremiah the prophet, saying, “You are deserting to the Chaldeans!”
\v 14 And Jeremiah said, “Falsehood! I am not deserting to the Chaldeans.” But he did not listen to him. And Irijah seized Jeremiah and brought him to the officials.
\v 15 And the officials were angry with Jeremiah, and they struck him and put him {in} the house of bonds, the house of Jonathan the scribe, for they had made it into a house of imprisonment.
\v 16 So Jeremiah had come to the house of the cistern and to the cells, and Jeremiah stayed there many days.
\p
\v 17 And King Zedekiah sent and took him out, and the king asked him in his house in secret, and said, “Is there a word from Yahweh?” And Jeremiah said, “There is.” And he said, “Into the hand of the king of Babylon you will be given.”
\v 18 And Jeremiah said to King Zedekiah, “What wrong have I done against you and against your servants and against this people, that you have put me into the house of imprisonment?
\v 19 And where are your prophets who prophesied to you, saying, ‘The king of Babylon will not come against you and against this land’?
\v 20 And now, listen, please, my lord the king. Please let my supplication fall to your face, and may you not make me return {to} the house of Jonathan the scribe, so I will not die there.
\v 21 So King Zedekiah commanded, and they committed Jeremiah to the courtyard of the guard, and to give to him a loaf of bread daily from the street of the bakers, until all the bread from the city was finished. And Jeremiah remained in the courtyard of the guard.
\c 38
\p
\v 1 And Shephatiah son of Mattan, and Gedaliah son of Pashhur, and Jucal son of Shelemiah, and Pashhur son of Malkijah heard the words that Jeremiah was speaking to all the people, saying,
\v 2 “Thus says Yahweh: The one dwelling in this city will die by the sword, by the famine and by the plague. But the one going out to the Chaldeans will live, and his life will be to him for spoil, and he will live.
\v 3 Thus says Yahweh: Being given, this city will be given into the hand of the army of the king of Babylon, and he will capture it.”
\v 4 And the officials said to the king, “Please let this man be put to death, for on account of this he is weakening the hands of the men of war, the ones remaining in this city, and the hands of all the people by speaking to them according to these words. For this man is not seeking for peace for this people, but rather for evil.”
\v 5 And King Zedekiah said, “Behold, he is in your hand, for the king is not able {to do} a word {against} you.”
\v 6 And they took Jeremiah and threw him into the cistern of Malkijah son of the king, which was in the courtyard of the guard, and they let Jeremiah down with ropes. And in the cistern there was no water, but only mud, and Jeremiah sank in the mud.
\p
\v 7 When Ebed Melech the Cushite, a man, a eunuch, and he was in the house of the king, heard that they had put Jeremiah into the cistern—and the king was sitting in the Gate of Benjamin—
\v 8 And Ebed Melech went out from the house of the king and spoke to the king, saying,
\v 9 “My lord the king, these men have done evil {in} all that they have done to Jeremiah the prophet, whom they threw into the cistern, and he will die in his place from the face of the hunger, for there is no more bread in the city.”
\v 10 And the king commanded Ebed Melech the Cushite, saying, “Take in your hand from here 30 men and bring up Jeremiah the prophet from the cistern before he dies.”
\v 11 And Ebed Melech took the men in his hand and went {to} the house of the king, to under the storehouse, and he took from there worn-out rags and worn-out clothes and let them down to Jeremiah, into the cistern, with ropes.
\v 12 And Ebed Melech the Cushite said to Jeremiah, “Put, please, the worn-out rags and the clothes under your armpits, from under the ropes.” And Jeremiah did so.
\v 13 And they drew out Jeremiah with the ropes and brought him up from the cistern. And Jeremiah stayed in the courtyard of the guard.
\p
\v 14 And King Zedekiah sent and took Jeremiah the prophet to himself, at the third entrance that was in the house of Yahweh. And the king said to Jeremiah, “I am going to ask you a word. May you not hide from me a word.
\v 15 And Jeremiah said to Zedekiah, “If I declare {it} to you, will you not, putting to death, put me to death? And if I advise you, you will not listen to me.”
\v 16 Then King Zedekiah swore to Jeremiah in secret, saying, “{As} Yahweh lives, who made this life for us, I will not put you to death nor give you into the hand of these men who are seeking your life!”
\p
\v 17 And Jeremiah said to Zedekiah, “Thus says Yahweh, the God of Armies, the God of Israel: If, going out, you go out to the officials of the king of Babylon, then your soul will live, and this city will not be burned with fire, and you will live, you and your house.
\v 18 And if you do not go out to the officials of the king of Babylon, then this city will be given into the hand of the Chaldeans, and they will burn it with fire, and you yourself will not escape from their hand.”
\v 19 And King Zedekiah said to Jeremiah, “I am fearing the Judeans who have fallen away to the Chaldeans, lest they give me into their hand and they deal ruthlessly with me.”
\v 20 And Jeremiah said, “They will not give {you}. Listen, please, to the voice of Yahweh, to what I am speaking to you, that it may go well for you, and your soul may live.
\v 21 And if you are refusing to go out, this is the word that Yahweh has shown me:
\v 22 And behold, all the women who are left in the house of the king of Judah are being brought out to the officials of the king of Babylon, and they are saying,
\q1 ‘They have incited you and have prevailed over you, the men of your peace.
\q2 Your feet have been sunk in the mire; they have turned backward.’
\v 23 And all your wives and your sons they are bringing out to the Chaldeans, and you yourself will not escape from their hand, for by the hand of the king of Babylon you will be seized, and this city will burn with fire.”
\p
\v 24 And Zedekiah said to Jeremiah, “Let not a man know about these words, and you will not die.
\v 25 But if the officials hear that I have spoken with you, and they come to you and say to you, ‘Declare, please, to us what you spoke to the king. Do not hide it from us, and we will not put you to death. And what did the king speak to you?’
\v 26 Then you shall say to them, ‘I myself am causing my supplication to fall to the face of the king, so that {he} would not make me return {to} the house of Jonathan to die there.’”
\v 27 Then all the officials came to Jeremiah and asked him, and he declared to them according to all these words that the king had commanded. So they stopped speaking with him, for the matter had not been heard.
\v 28 And Jeremiah stayed in the courtyard of the guard until the day that Jerusalem was captured. And he was there when Jerusalem was captured.
\c 39
\p
\v 1 In the ninth year of Zedekiah king of Judah, in the tenth month, Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon and all his army came to Jerusalem, and they besieged against it.
\v 2 In the eleventh year of Zedekiah, in the fourth month, on the ninth day of the month, the city was broken through.
\v 3 And all the officials of the king of Babylon came and sat in the Middle Gate: Nergal Sarezer, Samgar Nebu, Sarsekim the Rabsaris, Nergal Sarezer the Rabmag, and all the remnant of the officials of the king of Babylon.
\p
\v 4 And it happened that when Zedekiah king of Judah and all the men of war saw them, then they fled and went out at night from the city, {by} the way of the garden of the king, through the gate between the two walls. And he went out the way of the Arabah.
\v 5 But the army of the Chaldeans pursued after them, and they overtook Zedekiah in the plains of Jericho. And they took him and brought him up to Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, to Riblah in the land of Hamath, and he pronounced judgments on him.
\v 6 And the king of Babylon slaughtered the sons of Zedekiah at Riblah before his eyes. And all the nobles of Judah the king of Babylon slaughtered.
\v 7 And he put out the eyes of Zedekiah, and he bound him with bronze fetters to bring him to Babylon.
\v 8 And the house of the king and the house of the people the Chaldeans burned with fire, and the walls of Jerusalem they tore down.
\v 9 And the rest of the people, the ones remaining in the city, and the ones having fallen away, who had fallen to him, and the rest of the people, the ones remaining, Nebuzaradan the captain of the guards exiled {to} Babylon.
\v 10 But from the people, the poor ones, who there was not to them anything, Nebuzaradan, the captain of the guards, left in the land of Judah, and he gave to them vineyards and fields on that day.
\p
\v 11 And Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon commanded concerning Jeremiah by the hand of Nebuzaradan the captain of the guards, saying,
\v 12 “Take him, and set your eyes on him, and do not do to him anything harmful, but rather, as he speaks to you, thus do with him.”
\v 13 So Nebuzaradan, the captain of the guards, sent Nebushazban the Rabsaris, and Nergal Sharezer the Rabmag, and all the chiefs of the king of Babylon
\v 14 and they sent and took Jeremiah from the courtyard of the guard, and they gave him to Gedaliah son of Ahikam son of Shaphan to bring him out to the house. And he dwelt in the midst of the people.
\p
\v 15 And the word of Yahweh had come to Jeremiah when he was shut up in the courtyard of the guard, saying,
\v 16 “Go, and you shall say to Ebed Melech the Cushite, saying, ‘Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: Behold me bringing my words against this city for harm and not for good, and they will be to your face on that day.
\v 17 But I will deliver you on that day’—the declaration of Yahweh—‘and you will not be given into the hand of the men whom you fear from their face.
\v 18 For rescuing I will rescue you, and by the sword you will not fall, and your life will be to you for spoil, because you have trusted in me’”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\c 40
\p
\v 1 The word that came to Jeremiah from Yahweh after Nebuzaradan, the captain of the guards, sent him away from Ramah, when he took him, and he was bound in chains in the midst of all of the exiles of Jerusalem and Judah, the ones being exiled to Babylon.
\v 2 And the captain of the guards took Jeremiah and said to him, “Yahweh your God spoke this disaster against this place.
\v 3 And Yahweh brought {it} and did just as he spoke, because you have sinned against Yahweh and you have not listened to his voice. So this thing has happened to you.
\v 4 And now, behold, I have loosed you today from the chains that {were} on your hand. If {it is} good in your eyes to come with me {to} Babylon, come, and I will set my eye on you. But if it is bad in your eyes to come with me {to} Babylon, refrain. See, all of the land {is} to your face. Unto the good and to the right in your eyes to go there, go.”
\v 5 And while he had not yet turned, {he said}, “So return to Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam, the son of Shaphan, whom the king of Babylon has appointed in the cities of Judah, and dwell with him in the midst of the people. Or to all the right in your eyes to go, go.” And the captain of the guards gave him an allowance and a gift and sent him away.
\v 6 Then Jeremiah went to Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam, to Mizpah, and he dwelt with him in the midst of the people, the ones having been left in the land.
\p
\v 7 And all of the captains of the forces who {were} in the field, they and their men, heard that the king of Babylon had appointed Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam, in the land, and that he had appointed to him men and women and little ones and from the poor of the land, from those who had not been exiled to Babylon.
\v 8 And they came to Gedaliah, at Mizpah: Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, and Johanan and Jonathan, the sons of Kareah, and Seraiah, the son of Tanhumeth, and the sons of Ephai the Netophathite, and Jezaniah, the son of the Maacathite, they and their men.
\v 9 And Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam, the son of Shaphan, swore to them and to their men, saying, “Do not be afraid to serve the Chaldeans. Dwell in the land and serve the king of Babylon, and it will go well with you.
\v 10 And I, behold me dwelling at Mizpah to stand to the face of the Chaldeans who will come to us. But you, gather wine and summer fruit and oil, and put {them} in your vessels, and dwell in your cities that you have seized.”
\v 11 And also all of the Jews who {were} in Moab and among the sons of Ammon and in Edom, and who {were} in all of the lands, heard that the king of Babylon had given a remnant to Judah, and that he had appointed over them Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam, the son of Shaphan.
\v 12 And all of the Judeans returned from all of the places where they had been driven there, and they came {to} the land of Judah, to Gedaliah, at Mizpah. And they gathered wine and summer fruit, very much.
\p
\v 13 And Johanan, the son of Kareah, and all of the captains of the forces who {were} in the field came to Gedaliah, at Mizpah.
\v 14 And they said to him, “Knowing, do you know that Baalis, the king of the sons of Ammon, has sent Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, to strike your life?” But Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam, did not believe them.
\v 15 And Johanan, the son of Kareah, said to Gedaliah in secret at Mizpah, saying, “Let me go, please, and I will strike Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, and a man will not know. Why should he strike your life, and all of Judah, the ones being gathered to you, be scattered, and the remnant of Judah perish?”
\v 16 And Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam, said to Johanan, the son of Kareah, “You shall not do this thing, for you are speaking a lie about Ishmael.”
\c 41
\p
\v 1 Now it happened in the seventh month, Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, the son of Elishama, from the seed of the kingdom and the officers of the king, and ten men with him, came to Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam, at Mizpah. And they ate bread there together in Mizpah.
\v 2 And Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, and the ten men who were with him rose up, and they struck Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam, the son of Shaphan, with the sword, and he put him to death, whom the king of Babylon had appointed in the land.
\v 3 And all of the Judeans who were with him, with Gedaliah, in Mizpah, and the Chaldeans who were found there, the men of war, Ishmael struck.
\v 4 And it happened on the second day after putting Gedaliah to death, and a man did not know it,
\v 5 and men came from Shechem, from Shiloh, and from Samaria, eighty men, ones being shaved of beard and torn of clothes and cutting themselves, and a grain offering and frankincense in their hand, to bring {them} to the house of Yahweh.
\v 6 And Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, went out to meet them from Mizpah, going to go and weeping. And it happened, as he encountered them, that he said to them, “Come to Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam.”
\p
\v 7 And it happened, when they came into the midst of the city, that Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, slaughtered them into the midst of the cistern, he and the men who were with him.
\v 8 But ten men were found among them, and they said to Ishmael, “May you not put us to death, for there is to us stores in the field: wheat and barley and oil and honey.” And he ceased, and he did not put them to death in the midst of their brothers.
\v 9 And the cistern which Ishmael had thrown there all of the corpses of the men whom he had struck down in the hand of Gedaliah, it that King Asa had made from the face of Baasha, the king of Israel. Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, filled it with the slain.
\v 10 And Ishmael took captive all of the remnant of the people who were in Mizpah, the daughters of the king and all of the people, the ones having been left in Mizpah, whom Nebuzaradan, the captain of the guards, had appointed {to} Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam. And Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, took them captive and went to cross over to the sons of Ammon.
\p
\v 11 And Johanan, the son of Kareah, and all of the captains of the forces who were with him heard all of the evil that Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, had done.
\v 12 And they took all of the men and they went to fight with Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah. And they found him at the great pool that is in Gibeon.
\v 13 And it happened, when all of the people who were with Ishmael saw Johanan, the son of Kareah, and all of the captains of the forces who were with him, that they rejoiced.
\v 14 And all of the people whom Ishmael had taken captive from Mizpah turned around, and they returned and they went to Johanan, the son of Kareah.
\v 15 But Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, escaped with eight men from the face of Johanan, and he went to the sons of Ammon.
\p
\v 16 And Johanan, the son of Kareah, and all of the captains of the forces who were with him took all of the remnant of the people whom he had brought back from Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, from Mizpah, after he had struck Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam—the mighty men of war, and women and little ones and eunuchs, whom he had brought back from Gibeon.
\v 17 And they went and stayed in Geruth Kimham, which is beside Bethlehem, to go to enter Egypt,
\v 18 from the face of the Chaldeans, for they were afraid from their face, because Ishmael, the son of Nethaniah, had struck Gedaliah, the son of Ahikam, whom the king of Babylon had appointed in the land.
\c 42
\p
\v 1 Then all the commanders of the forces, and Johanan son of Kareah, and Jezaniah son of Hoshaiah, and all the people from small and to great, approached.
\v 2 And they said to Jeremiah the prophet, “Please, let our supplication fall before your face, and pray for us to Yahweh your God, for all this remnant, because we are left few from many, just as your eyes are seeing us,
\v 3 that Yahweh your God may declare to us the way in which we should walk and the word that we should do.”
\v 4 And Jeremiah the prophet said to them, “I have heard. Behold me praying to Yahweh your God according to your words. And it will happen that every word that Yahweh answers you, I will declare to you. I will not withhold a word from you.”
\v 5 Then they themselves said to Jeremiah, “May Yahweh be against us as a witness of truth and being faithful, if not, according to every word {with} which Yahweh your God sends you to us, thus we do.
\v 6 Whether good or whether bad, we will listen to the voice of Yahweh our God, to whom we are sending you, in order that it may be good for us when we listen to the voice of Yahweh our God.”
\p
\v 7 Now it happened at the end of ten days that the word of Yahweh came to Jeremiah.
\v 8 And he called to Johanan son of Kareah and to all the commanders of the forces who were with him and to all the people, from small and to great.
\v 9 And he said to them, “Thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel, to whom you sent me to make your supplication fall to his face:
\v 10 ‘If turning you will dwell in this land, then I will build you and I will not tear down, and I will plant you and I will not uproot, for I am grieved concerning the disaster that I did to you.
\v 11 You shall not fear from the face of the king of Babylon, from whose face you {are} afraid. Do not be afraid of him’—the declaration of Yahweh—‘for I am with you, to save you and to rescue you from his hand.
\v 12 And I will give to you compassions, that he will have compassion on you and will cause you return to your land.’
\v 13 But if you are saying, ‘We will not dwell in this land,’ so as not to listen to the voice of Yahweh your God,
\v 14 saying, ‘No, for we will go to the land of Egypt, where we will not see war and we will not hear the sound of a shofar and we will not hunger for bread, and there we will dwell,’
\v 15 then now, therefore, hear the word of Yahweh, remnant of Judah. Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: ‘If setting you will set your faces to go to Egypt, and you go to sojourn there,
\v 16 then it will happen that the sword that you are afraid of, there it will overtake you in the land of Egypt, and the famine that you are anxious about, there it will cling after you {to} Egypt, and there you will die.
\v 17 And it will be that all the men who set their faces to go to Egypt to sojourn there will die by the sword, by the famine, and by the plague. And there will not be for them a survivor or a fugitive from the face of the disaster that I am bringing upon them.’
\p
\v 18 For thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: ‘Just as my nose and my heat were poured out on the dwellers of Jerusalem, so my heat will be poured out on you when you go to Egypt. And you will be to execration and to horror and to curse and to taunt, and you will not see this place again.’
\v 19 Yahweh has spoken concerning you, remnant of Judah: ‘Do not go to Egypt.’ Knowing, you will know that I have given a warning to you today
\v 20 that you have gone astray in your souls when you yourselves sent me to Yahweh your God, saying, ‘Pray on behalf of us to Yahweh our God, and according to all that Yahweh our God says, thus declare {it} to us, and we will do {it}.’
\v 21 And I have declared {it} to you today, but you have not listened to the voice of Yahweh your God, and in all that he sent me to you.
\v 22 And now, knowing you will know that by the sword, by the famine, and by the plague you will die in the place where you desire to go to sojourn there.”
\c 43
\p
\v 1 And it happened, when Jeremiah had finished speaking to all the people all the words of Yahweh their God, {with} which Yahweh their God had sent him to them, all these words,
\p
\v 2 that Azariah son of Hoshaiah and Johanan son of Kareah and all the arrogant men said, saying to Jeremiah, “Falsehood you are speaking! Yahweh our God did not send you, saying, ‘You shall not go to Egypt to sojourn there.’
\v 3 For Baruch son of Neriah is inciting you against us, in order to give us into the hand of the Chaldeans, to put us to death or to exile us {to} Babylon.”
\v 4 And Johanan son of Kareah and all the commanders of the forces and all the people did not listen to the voice of Yahweh, to dwell in the land of Judah.
\v 5 But Johanan son of Kareah and all the commanders of the forces took all the remnant of Judah who had returned from all the nations where they had been driven there, to sojourn in the land of Judah:
\v 6 the men and the women and the little ones and the daughters of the king and every soul whom Nebuzaradan the captain of the guards had left with Gedaliah son of Ahikam son of Shaphan, and Jeremiah the prophet and Baruch son of Neriah.
\v 7 And they came {to} the land of Egypt, for they did not listen to the voice of Yahweh. And they came as far as Tahpanhes.
\p
\v 8 And the word of Yahweh came to Jeremiah in Tahpanhes, saying,
\v 9 “Take in your hand large stones, and you shall hide them in the mortar in the brick pavement that is at the entrance of the house of Pharaoh in Tahpanhes, before the eyes of the Judean men.
\v 10 And you shall say to them, ‘Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: “Behold me, sending, and I will take Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, my servant, and I will set his throne above these stones that I have hidden, and he will spread out his canopy over them.
\v 11 And he will come and strike the land of Egypt: whoever {is} for death, to death; and whoever {is} for captivity, to captivity; and whoever {is} for the sword, to the sword.
\v 12 And I will kindle a fire in the houses of the gods of Egypt, and he will burn them and take them captive. And he will wrap himself {with} the land of Egypt just as the shepherd wraps himself {with} his garment, and he will go out from there in peace.
\v 13 And he will break the pillars of Beth Shemesh that are in the land of Egypt, and the houses of the gods of Egypt he will burn with fire.”’”
\c 44
\p
\v 1 The word that came to Jeremiah for all the Jews, the ones dwelling in the land of Egypt, the ones dwelling in Migdol and in Tahpanhes and in Memphis and in the land of Pathros, saying,
\p
\v 2 “Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: ‘You yourself have seen all the disaster that I have brought upon Jerusalem and upon all the cities of Judah. And behold them, a ruin this day, and there is no dweller in them,
\v 3 from the face of their evil that they did to provoke me to anger, by going to burn incense, to serve other gods that they did not know—they, you, nor your fathers.
\v 4 And I sent to you all my servants the prophets, rising early and sending, saying, “Please, do not do this thing of abomination that I hate.”
\v 5 But they did not listen and they did not incline their ear to turn back from their evil, so as not to burn incense to other gods.
\v 6 And my heat and my nose poured out, and it burned in the cities of Judah and in the streets of Jerusalem, and they became a ruin, a desolation, as at this day.’
\p
\v 7 And now, thus says Yahweh, the God of Armies, the God of Israel: ‘Why are you doing great evil against your souls, to cut off for yourselves man and woman, child and nursing one, from the midst of Judah, so as not to leave for yourselves a remnant,
\v 8 by provoking me to anger with the works of your hands, by burning incense to other gods in the land of Egypt, where you are going to sojourn there, in order to cut off for yourselves, and in order for you to become a curse and a reproach among all the nations of the earth?
\v 9 Have you forgotten the evils of your fathers and the evils of the kings of Judah and the evils of his wives and your evils and the evils of your wives that they did in the land of Judah and in the streets of Jerusalem?
\v 10 They have not been crushed to this day, and they have not feared, and they have not walked in my law and in my statutes that I gave to the face of you and to the face of your fathers.’
\p
\v 11 Therefore thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: ‘Behold me, setting my face against you for disaster, and to cut off all Judah.
\v 12 And I will take the remnant of Judah who set their faces to go {to} the land of Egypt to sojourn there, and they will all come to an end in the land of Egypt. They will fall by the sword; by the famine they will come to an end, from small and to great. By the sword and by the famine they will die, and they will be to execration, to horror, and to curse, and to taunt.
\v 13 And I will visit {punishment} upon the ones dwelling in the land of Egypt just as I visited {punishment} upon Jerusalem, by the sword, by the famine, and by the plague.
\v 14 So there will not be a fugitive or a survivor for the remnant of Judah, the ones going to sojourn there in the land of Egypt, or to return {to} the land of Judah, where they are lifting up their soul to return to dwell there. For they will not return, except fugitives.’”
\p
\v 15 And all the men who knew that their wives were burning incense to other gods, and all the women standing by, a great assembly, and all the people dwelling in the land of Egypt, in Pathros, answered Jeremiah, saying,
\v 16 “The word that you have spoken to us in the name of Yahweh—we are not listening to you.
\v 17 For doing we will do every word that has gone out from our mouth, to burn incense to the queen of the heavens and to pour out drink offerings to her, as we have done, we and our fathers, our kings and our officials, in the cities of Judah and in the streets of Jerusalem. And we were satisfied {with} bread and we had good, and disaster we did not see.
\v 18 But from when we ceased to burn incense to the queen of the heavens and to pour out drink offerings to her, we have lacked everything, and by the sword and by the famine we have come to an end.
\v 19 And when we were burning incense to the queen of the heavens and poured out drink offerings to her, was it without our husbands {that} we made for her cakes to copy her and pour out to her drink offerings?”
\p
\v 20 And Jeremiah said to all the people, to the men, and to the women, and to all the people answering him {that} word, saying,
\v 21 “{As for} the incense that you burned in the cities of Judah and in the streets of Jerusalem—you and your fathers, your kings and your officials and the people of the land—has Yahweh not remembered them, and it come to his heart?
\v 22 And Yahweh was no longer able to bear {it}, from the face of the evil of your deeds, from the face of the abominations that you did. And your land became a ruin and a horror and a curse, without a dweller, as at this day.
\v 23 From the face of {the fact} that you have burned incense and that you have sinned against Yahweh, and you have not listened to the voice of Yahweh, and in his law and in his statutes and in his testimonies you have not walked—therefore this disaster has met you, as at this day.”
\p
\v 24 And Jeremiah said to all the people and to all the women, “Hear the word of Yahweh, all Judah who {are} in the land of Egypt.
\v 25 Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel, saying: ‘You and your wives have spoken with your mouth and you have fulfilled {it} with your hands, saying, “Performing we will perform our vows that we vowed, to burn incense to the queen of the heavens and to pour out drink offerings to her.” Establishing you will establish your vows, and performing you will perform your vows!’
\p
\v 26 Therefore hear the word of Yahweh, all Judah, the ones dwelling in the land of Egypt: ‘Behold me, I have sworn by my great name,’ says Yahweh, ‘My name will no longer be called by the mouth of any man of Judah, saying, “{By} the life of Lord Yahweh,” in all the land of Egypt.
\v 27 Behold me, watching over them for harm and not for good. And all the men of Judah who {are} in the land of Egypt will come to an end by the sword and by the famine, until their finishing.
\v 28 And the fugitives of the sword will return from the land of Egypt {to} the land of Judah, men of number. And all the remnant of Judah, the ones coming to the land of Egypt to sojourn there, will know whose word will stand, from me or from them.
\v 29 And this {will be} for you a sign’—the declaration of Yahweh—‘that I am visiting {punishment} upon you in this place, so that you may know that standing my words will stand against you for harm.’
\p
\v 30 Thus says Yahweh: ‘Behold me, giving Pharaoh Hophra king of Egypt into the hand of his enemies and into the hand of the ones seeking his life, just as I gave Zedekiah king of Judah into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, his enemy and the one seeking his life.’”
\c 45
\p
\v 1 The word that Jeremiah the prophet spoke to Baruch son of Neriah, when he wrote these words on a book from the mouth of Jeremiah, in the fourth year of Jehoiakim son of Josiah, king of Judah, saying,
\p
\v 2 “Thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel, concerning you, Baruch:
\v 3 You have said, ‘Woe now to me, for Yahweh has added sorrow to my pain. I am weary with my groaning, and I have not rest found!’
\p
\v 4 Thus you shall say to him: ‘Thus says Yahweh: Behold, what I have built, I am tearing down, and what I have planted, I am plucking up—and this with regard to all of the earth.
\v 5 And you, do you seek great things for yourself? May you not seek {them}. For behold me, bringing disaster upon all flesh’—the declaration of Yahweh—‘but I will give to you your life for spoil in all the places where you will go there.’”
\c 46
\p
\v 1 The word of Yahweh that was to Jeremiah the prophet concerning the nations.
\v 2 For Egypt: concerning the army of Pharaoh Neco king of Egypt, which was at the river Euphrates in Carchemish, {and} which Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon struck in the fourth year of Jehoiakim son of Josiah, king of Judah:
\q1 \v 3 “Arrange small shield and large shield,
\q2 and draw near for the battle.
\q1 \v 4 Harness the horses, and go up, O horsemen,
\q2 and station yourselves with helmets.
\q2 Polish the spears, put on the body armor.
\q1 \v 5 Why have I seen {it}? They are dismayed, turning back backward,
\q2 and their mighty ones are crushed.
\q2 And in flight they have fled, and they have not turned.
\q2 Terror from all around”— the declaration of Yahweh—
\q1 \v 6 “The swift cannot flee, and the mighty one cannot escape.
\q2 In the north, by the side of the Euphrates River, they stumble and they fall.
\q1 \v 7 Who is this rising up like the Nile,
\q2 like the streams their surging back and forth its waters?
\q1 \v 8 Egypt rises up like the Nile, and like the streams {its} waters toss.
\q2 And he said, ‘Let me rise, let me cover {the} earth. Let me destroy city and the dwellers in it.’
\q1 \v 9 Go up, O horses, and drive madly O chariots, and let the mighty ones come out,
\q2 Cush and Put, the ones grasping the shield, and men of Lud, ones grasping, ones treading the bow.
\q1 \v 10 And that day {is} to Lord Yahweh of Armies, a day of vengeance, to avenge himself on his adversaries.
\q2 And the sword will devour and be satisfied and be quenched from their blood,
\q2 for {there is} a sacrifice for Lord Yahweh of Armies in the land of the north, by the Euphrates River.
\q1 \v 11 Go up {to} Gilead and take balm, {O} virgin daughter of Egypt.
\q2 For emptiness I have multiplied medicines;
\q2 there is no healing for you.
\q1 \v 12 The nations have heard your shame, and your cry has filled the earth,
\q2 for mighty one against mighty one has stumbled;
\q2 together the two of them have fallen.”
\p
\v 13 The word that Yahweh spoke to Jeremiah the prophet, regarding the coming of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon to strike the land of Egypt:
\q1 \v 14 “Declare {it} in Egypt, and cause {it} to be heard in Migdol, and cause {it} to be heard in Memphis and in Tahpanhes.
\q2 Say, ‘Station yourself and prepare yourself,
\q2 for a sword will devour those around you.’
\q1 \v 15 Why have your mighty ones been cut down? They did not stand,
\q2 because Yahweh has thrust them down.
\q1 \v 16 He multiplied the one stumbling; yes, a man has fallen upon his neighbor.
\q2 And they said, ‘Arise, and let us return to our people and to the land of our birth,
\q2 from the face of the sword of the oppressor.’
\q1 \v 17 There they call out,
\q2 ‘Pharaoh king of Egypt {is} a loud noise; he has let the appointed time to pass by.’
\q1 \v 18 As I live”—the declaration of the King, Yahweh of Armies is his name—“surely like Tabor among the mountains,
\q2 and like Carmel by the sea, he will come.
\q1 \v 19 Prepare baggage for yourself for exile, O dweller, daughter of Egypt,
\q2 for Memphis will become a desolation, and it will be burned, without a dweller.
\q1 \v 20 Egypt is a beautiful, beautiful heifer;
\q2 a gadfly from the north coming, coming.
\q1 \v 21 Also her hired ones among her {are} like calves of the stall,
\q2 for they themselves have also turned back, they have fled together; they have not stood,
\q2 for the day of their calamity has come upon them, the time of their visitation.
\q1 \v 22 Her sound {is} like a serpent {that} goes away,
\q2 for in force they go, and with axes they have come against her, like men cutting down trees.
\q1 \v 23 They have cut down her forest”—the declaration of Yahweh—“for it will not be searched out,
\q2 for they are more numerous than the locust, and there is no number to them.
\q1 \v 24 The daughter of Egypt will be put to shame;
\q2 she will be given into the hand of the people of the north.”
\p
\v 25 Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel, says, “Behold me, visiting {punishment} upon Amon from No, and upon Pharaoh, and upon Egypt, and upon her gods, and upon her kings, and upon Pharaoh, and upon the ones trusting in him.
\v 26 And I will give them into the hand of the ones seeking their life, and into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, and into the hand of his servants. And after that she will dwell as in the days of antiquity”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\p
\v 27 “But you, do not fear, my servant Jacob, nor be dismayed, Israel, for behold me, saving you from far off, and your seed from the land of their captivity. And Jacob will return and will have quiet and will be at ease, and there will be no one making him afraid.
\v 28 You, my servant Jacob, do not fear”— the declaration of Yahweh— “for I {am} with you. For I will make a complete end of all the nations where I have scattered you there, but you I will not make a complete end of. And I will discipline you according to justice, and leaving unpunished, I will not leave you unpunished.”
\c 47
\p
\v 1 The word of Yahweh that was to Jeremiah the prophet concerning the Philistines, before Pharaoh struck Gaza.
\q1 \v 2 Thus says Yahweh:
\q1 “Behold, waters are rising from the north,
\q2 and they will become as an overflowing torrent.
\q1 And they will overflow the land and its fullness,
\q2 the city and the dwellers in it.
\q1 And mankind will cry out,
\q2 and every dweller of the land will wail.
\q1 \v 3 At the sound of the stamping of the hooves of his mighty ones,
\q2 at the rattling of his chariot, the rumble of his wheels,
\q1 fathers do not turn to sons
\q2 from the slackness of hands,
\q1 \v 4 because of the day that is coming
\q2 to devastate all the Philistines,
\q1 to cut off from Tyre and from Sidon every remaining one helping.
\q1 For Yahweh is devastating the Philistines,
\q2 the remnant of the coastland of Caphtor.
\q1 \v 5 Baldness has come to Gaza;
\q2 Ashkelon has been destroyed,
\q2 O remnant of their valley,
\q1 until when will you cut yourself?
\q1 \v 6 ‘Ah, sword of Yahweh!
\q2 How long will you not be quiet?
\q1 Be gathered into your sheath;
\q2 be at rest and be still.’
\q1 \v 7 How can it be quiet,
\q2 when Yahweh has given a command to it?
\q1 Against Ashkelon and against the coast of the sea,
\q2 there he has appointed it.”
\c 48
\p
\v 1 Concerning Moab: Thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel:
\q1 “Woe to Nebo, for she is devastated!
\q2 Kiriathaim is ashamed; she is captured.
\q2 The stronghold is put to shame and is dismayed.
\q1 \v 2 There is no longer praise of Moab.
\q2 In Heshbon they have devised evil against her:
\q2 ‘Come, and let us cut her off from {being} a nation.’
\q1 Also, O Madmen, you will be silenced;
\q2 after you the sword will go.
\q1 \v 3 A sound of a cry from Horonaim:
\q2 destruction and great breaking!
\q1 \v 4 Moab is broken;
\q2 her little ones have a cry heard.
\q1 \v 5 For the ascent of Luhith—
\q2 with weeping he goes up weeping;
\q1 for at the descent of Horonaim
\q2 the adversaries have heard a cry of breaking.
\q1 \v 6 Flee! Save yourselves,
\q2 and you will be like a juniper bush in the wilderness.
\q1 \v 7 For because of your trusting in your works and in your treasures,
\q2 you also will be captured.
\q1 And Chemosh will go out into exile,
\q2 his priests and his officials together.
\q1 \v 8 And a destroyer will come to every city,
\q2 and no city will escape.
\q1 And the valley will perish and the plateau will be destroyed,
\q2 as Yahweh has said.
\q1 \v 9 Give wing to Moab, for to fly she will go away,
\q2 and her cities will become a desolation,
\q2 with no dweller in them.
\q1 \v 10 Cursed {is} the one doing the work of Yahweh {with} slackness,
\q2 and cursed {is} the one withholding his sword from blood.
\q1 \v 11 Moab has been at ease from his youth,
\q2 and he is undisturbed on his dregs.
\q1 And he has not been emptied from vessel to vessel,
\q2 and into exile he has not gone.
\q1 Therefore his taste stands in him,
\q2 and his scent has not changed.
\q1 \v 12 Therefore, behold, days are coming”—the declaration of Yahweh—“when I will send to him tippers, and they will tip him. And they will empty his vessels and they will shatter their jars.
\q1 \v 13 And Moab will be ashamed of Chemosh,
\q2 just as the house of Israel was ashamed of Bethel, their confidence.
\q1 \v 14 How can you say, ‘We {are} mighty,
\q2 and men of valor for battle’?
\q1 \v 15 Moab is devastated, and he has gone up {against} her cities;
\q2 and the choicest of his young men have gone down to the slaughter, the declaration of the King, Yahweh of Armies is his name.
\q1 \v 16 The calamity of Moab is near to coming,
\q2 and his disaster hastens exceedingly.
\q1 \v 17 Grieve for him, all of {you} around him,
\q2 and all the knowers of his name.
\q1 Say, ‘How the staff of strength is broken,
\q2 the rod of splendor!’
\q1 \v 18 Come down from glory and sit in thirst,
\q2 dweller, daughter of Dibon,
\q1 for the destroyer of Moab has come up against you;
\q2 he has ruined your fortifications.
\q1 \v 19 Stand by the road and watch,
\q2 dweller of Aroer.
\q1 Ask him fleeing and her escaping;
\q2 say, ‘What has happened?’
\q1 \v 20 Moab is put to shame, for she is dismayed.
\q2 Wail and cry out!
\q1 Declare {it} by Arnon
\q2 that Moab is devastated.
\q1 \v 21 And judgment has come to the land of the plateau,
\q2 to Holon and to Jahaz and upon Mephaath,
\q1 \v 22 and upon Dibon and upon Nebo and upon Beth Diblathaim,
\q1 \v 23 and upon Kiriathaim and upon Beth Gamul and upon Beth Meon,
\q1 \v 24 and upon Kerioth and upon Bozrah,
\q2 and upon all the cities of the land of Moab,
\q2 the far and the near.
\q1 \v 25 The horn of Moab has been cut off,
\q2 and his arm has been broken”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\p
\v 26 “Make him drunk, because against Yahweh he has made himself great. And Moab will splash in his vomit, and he will be for derision, also he.
\v 27 For did not Israel come to be a derision to you? Was he found among thieves, that as often as your words {were} about him you shook?
\q1 \v 28 Abandon the cities and dwell among the rock,
\q2 dwellers of Moab,
\q1 and be like a dove {that} nests
\q2 in the sides of the mouth of a pit.
\q1 \v 29 We have heard of the pride of Moab—exceedingly proud—
\q2 his loftiness and his pride and his arrogance and the height of his heart.
\q1 \v 30 I, I know”—the declaration of Yahweh—“his outburst, but his boasts {are} not trustworthy;
\q2 his deeds {are} not trustworthy.
\q1 \v 31 Therefore I will wail over Moab,
\q2 and for all of Moab I will cry out;
\q2 for the men of Kir Hareseth he will moan.
\q1 \v 32 More than the weeping of Jazer I will weep for you,
\q2 vine of Sibmah.
\q1 Your tendrils passed over to the sea;
\q2 as far as the sea of Jazer they reached.
\q1 Upon your summer fruit and upon your grape harvest
\q2 a destroyer has fallen.
\q1 \v 33 And gladness and rejoicing have been taken away
\q2 from the fertile land and from the land of Moab.
\q1 And I have caused wine to cease from the winepresses.
\q2 He will not tread {with} a shout;
\q2 the shout {will} not {be} a shout.
\p
\v 34 From the outcry of Heshbon as far as Elealeh, as far as Jahaz, they have given their voice, from Zoar as far as Horonaim, Eglath Shelishiyah, for even the waters of Nimrim will become desolations.
\v 35 And I will cause to cease for Moab”—the declaration of Yahweh—“the one causing {offerings} to ascend on a high place and the one burning incense to his gods.
\v 36 Therefore my heart moans for Moab like flutes, and my heart moans like flutes for the men of Kir Hareseth. Therefore the abundance he has aquired has perished.
\v 37 For every head {will be} baldness, and every beard will be clipped On all hands will be} gashes, and on the loins {will be} sackcloth.
\v 38 On all the housetops of Moab and in her open squares, all of it lamentation, for I have broken Moab like a vessel without delight in it”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 39 “How it is dismayed! They wail!
\q2 How Moab has turned {the} neck in shame!
\q1 Moab has come to be for a derision
\q2 and a terror to all around him.”
\p
\v 40 For thus says Yahweh:
\q1 “Behold, like an eagle he will swoop down,
\q2 and he will spread his wings against Moab.
\q1 \v 41 Kerioth has been captured,
\q2 and the strongholds have been seized.
\q1 And the heart of the warriors of Moab in that day
\q2 will be like the heart of a woman in labor.
\q1 \v 42 And Moab will be destroyed from {being} a people,
\q2 for against Yahweh he has made himself great.
\q1 \v 43 Terror and pit and snare {are} upon you, dweller of Moab”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 44 “The one fleeing from the face of the terror will fall into the pit,
\q2 and the one coming up from the pit will be caught in the snare,
\q1 for I will bring to her, to Moab,
\q2 the year of their visitation”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 45 “In the shadow of Heshbon the ones fleeing stand without strength,
\q2 for a fire has gone forth from Heshbon,
\q2 and a flame from the midst of Sihon,
\q1 and it has devoured the forehead of Moab
\q2 and the crown of the head of the sons of tumult.
\q1 \v 46 Woe to you, Moab!
\q2 The people of Chemosh have perished,
\q1 for your sons have been taken into captivity,
\q2 and your daughters into captivity.
\q1 \v 47 Yet I will restore the restoration of Moab in the end of the days”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 As far as here {is} the judgment of Moab.
\c 49
\q1 \v 1 Concerning the sons of Ammon, thus says Yahweh:
\q1 “Are there no sons for Israel?
\q2 Or is there no heir for him?
\q1 Why {then} has their king taken possession of Gad,
\q2 and his people dwelt in his cities?
\q1 \v 2 Therefore, behold, days are coming”— the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 “when I will make the shout of war heard against Rabbah of the sons of Ammon,
\q1 and she will become a mound of desolation,
\q2 and her daughters will be kindled with fire.
\q1 And Israel will take possession of his possessors,”
\q2 says Yahweh.
\q1 \v 3 “Wail, Heshbon, for Ai has been devastated!
\q2 Cry out, daughters of Rabbah!
\q1 Put on sackcloth; mourn,
\q2 and run to and fro among the walls,
\q1 for their king will go into exile,
\q2 his priests and his officials together.
\q1 \v 4 Why do you boast in the valleys,
\q2 your valley flowing, apostate daughter,
\q1 the one trusting in her treasures,
\q2 ‘Who will come against me?’
\q1 \v 5 Behold me, bringing terror upon you”— the declaration of the Lord Yahweh of Armies—
\q2 “from all of those around you.
\q1 And you will be scattered, a man to his face,
\q2 and there will be no one gathering the one fleeing.
\q1 \v 6 But after that I will restore the restoration of the sons of Ammon”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\p
\v 7 Concerning Edom, thus says Yahweh of Armies:
\q1 “Is there no longer wisdom in Teman?
\q2 Counsel has perished from the discerning ones;
\q2 their wisdom has rotted away.
\q1 \v 8 Flee! Turn back! Make deep to dwell,
\q2 dwellers of Dedan!
\q1 For I will bring the calamity of Esau upon him,
\q2 the time I visit him.
\q1 \v 9 If grape gatherers came to you,
\q2 would they not leave gleanings?
\q1 If thieves {came} in the night,
\q2 they would destroy their sufficiency.
\q1 \v 10 But I, I will strip Esau bare;
\q2 I will uncover his hiding places,
\q2 and he will not be able to hide himself.
\q1 His seed is devastated, and his brothers and his neighbors,
\q2 and he is no more.
\q1 \v 11 Leave your fatherless ones; I, I will keep {them} alive,
\q2 and your widows, may they trust in me.”
\p
\v 12 For thus says Yahweh: “Behold, those whose judgment {was} not to drink the cup, drinking they will drink. And you, {are} you the one {who}, acquitting, will be acquitted? You will not be acquitted, for drinking you will drink.
\v 13 For by myself I have sworn”— the declaration of Yahweh— “that Bozrah will become a horror, a reproach, a devastation, and a cursing, and all of her cities will become ruins of perpetuity.”
\q1 \v 14 I have heard a report from Yahweh,
\q2 and an envoy has been sent among the nations:
\q1 “Gather yourselves and come against her,
\q2 and rise up for the battle!”
\q1 \v 15 “For behold, I have made you small among the nations,
\q2 despised among mankind.
\q1 \v 16 Your terror has deceived you,
\q2 the pride of your heart,
\q1 one dwelling in the clefts of the rock,
\q2 grasping the height of the hill.
\q1 Though you make your nest high like the eagle,
\q2 from there I will bring you down”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 17 And Edom will become a horror.
\q2 Everyone passing by her will be appalled
\q2 and will hiss over all of her wounds.
\q1 \v 18 “Like the overthrow of Sodom and Gomorrah and her neighbors,” says Yahweh,
\q2 “no man will dwell there,
\q2 and a son of man will not sojourn in her.”
\q1 \v 19 “Behold, like a lion he will come up from the pride of the Jordan
\q2 to a permanent pasture,
\q1 for in a moment I will make him run from her.
\q2 And who is the chosen one {whom} I will appoint over her?
\q1 For who is like me, and who will summon me?
\q2 And who is this shepherd who will stand before my face?”
\q1 \v 20 Therefore hear the counsel of Yahweh
\q2 that he has counseled against Edom,
\q1 and his plans that he has planned
\q2 against the dwellers of Teman:
\q1 Surely they will drag them away, the little ones of the flock!
\q2 Surely he will make their pasture desolate over them!
\q1 \v 21 From the sound of their falling the earth has quaked. A cry— in the Sea of Reeds her voice was heard.
\q1 \v 22 Behold, like an eagle he will come up and swoop,
\q2 and he will spread his wings over Bozrah.
\q1 And the heart of the warriors of Edom in that day
\q2 will be like the heart of a woman in labor.
\p
\v 23 For Damascus:
\q1 Hamath and Arpad are ashamed,
\q2 for they have heard a bad report.
\q1 They have melted; in the sea {there is} anxiety—
\q2 it is not able to be quiet.
\q1 \v 24 Damascus has become feeble;
\q2 she has turned to flee, and trembling has seized her.
\q1 Distress and pangs have seized her
\q2 like a woman giving birth.
\q1 \v 25 How
\q2 the city of praise has not been forsaken, the city of my joy!
\q1 \v 26 “Therefore her young men will fall in her open squares,
\q2 and all the men of war will be silenced in that day”— the declaration of Yahweh of Armies.
\q1 \v 27 “And I will kindle a fire in the wall of Damascus,
\q2 and it will devour the citadels of Ben Hadad.”
\p
\v 28 For Kedar and for the kingdoms of Hazor, which Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon struck, thus says Yahweh:
\q1 “Rise up, go up against Kedar,
\q2 and devastate the sons of the east!
\q1 \v 29 They will take their tents and their flocks;
\q2 they will carry off for themselves their curtains and all of their vessels and their camels.
\q1 And they will call out over them,
\q2 ‘Terror from all around!’
\q1 \v 30 Flee! Wander far away! Make deep to dwell, dwellers of Hazor”— the declaration of Yahweh—
\q1 “for Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon has counseled a counsel against you,
\q2 and he has planned a plan against them.
\q1 \v 31 Rise up, go up against a nation at ease, dwelling in security”— the declaration of Yahweh—
\q1 “there are no doors and no bar for it;
\q2 they dwell alone.
\q1 \v 32 And their camels will become plunder,
\q2 and the multitude of their livestock spoil.
\q1 And I will scatter them to every wind,
\q2 the ones cut {at the} corner {of their hair},
\q1 and from all of his sides I will bring their calamity”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 33 And Hazor will become a habitation of jackals,
\q2 a desolation forever.
\q1 No man will dwell there,
\q2 and a son of man will not sojourn in her.
\p
\v 34 That which was the word of Yahweh to Jeremiah the prophet concerning Elam, in the beginning of the reign of Zedekiah king of Judah, saying,
\p
\v 35 Thus says Yahweh of Armies:
\q1 “Behold me, breaking the bow of Elam,
\q2 the beginning of their power.
\q1 \v 36 And I will bring against Elam four winds
\q2 from the four ends of the heavens,
\q1 and I will scatter them to all of these winds,
\q2 and there will not be a nation
\q2 where the banished ones of Elam will not come there.
\q1 \v 37 And I will make Elam dismayed before the face of their enemies
\q2 and before the face of the ones seeking their life.
\q1 And I will bring evil upon them, the burning of my nose”— the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 “and I will send the sword after them until I have finished them.
\q1 \v 38 And I will set my throne in Elam,
\q2 and I will destroy from there king and officials”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 39 “Yet it will happen in the end of the days {that} I will return the restoration of Elam”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\c 50
\p
\v 1 The word that Yahweh spoke concerning Babylon, concerning the land of the Chaldeans, by the hand of Jeremiah the prophet:
\q1 \v 2 “Declare among the nations and cause {it} to be heard,
\q2 and lift up a banner; cause {it} to be heard!
\q2 May you not conceal {it}!
\q1 Say, ‘Babylon has been captured;
\q2 Bel has come to shame; Marduk is dismayed.
\q1 Her idols have come to shame;
\q2 her images are dismayed.’
\q1 \v 3 For a nation will come up against her from the north.
\q2 It, it will set her land as a horror,
\q1 and there will not be a dweller in her.
\q2 From man even to beast will wander; they will go out.
\q1 \v 4 In those days and in that time”— the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 “the sons of Israel will come, they and the sons of Judah together.
\q1 Going and weeping they will go,
\q2 and they will seek Yahweh their God.
\q1 \v 5 They will ask the way {to} Zion; their faces are facing this way.
\q2 ‘Come!’ And they will join themselves to Yahweh
\q2 {in} an everlasting covenant {that} will not be forgotten.
\q1 \v 6 My people have been a lost flock;
\q2 their shepherds have caused them to go astray {on} turning-away mountains.
\q1 They went from mountain to hill;
\q2 they forgot their resting place.
\q1 \v 7 All of their finders have devoured them,
\q2 and their adversaries have said, ‘We are not guilty,
\q1 because they have sinned against Yahweh, the pasture of righteousness
\q2 and the hope of their fathers— Yahweh.’
\q1 \v 8 Wander away from the midst of Babylon,
\q2 and go out from the land of the Chaldeans;
\q1 and be like male goats before the face of the flock.
\q1 \v 9 For behold, I am stirring up and bringing up against Babylon
\q2 an assembly of great nations from the land of the north.
\q1 And they will array themselves against her;
\q2 from there she will be captured.
\q1 His arrows are like a warrior acting wisely;
\q2 he does not return empty-handed.
\q1 \v 10 And Chaldea will become plunder;
\q2 all of her plunderers will be satisfied”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 11 “Though you rejoice, though you exult,
\q2 plunderers of my inheritance;
\q1 though you leap about like a heifer threshing,
\q2 and you neigh like mighty ones.
\q1 \v 12 Your mother is greatly ashamed;
\q2 the one bearing you is humiliated.
\q1 Behold, the last of nations
\q2 is a wilderness, a dry land, and a desert.
\q1 \v 13 From the wrath of Yahweh she will not be inhabited,
\q2 and she will become a desolation, all of her.
\q1 Everyone passing by Babylon will be appalled
\q2 and will hiss over all of her wounds.
\q1 \v 14 Array yourselves against Babylon all around,
\q2 all of the ones bending a bow!
\q1 Shoot at her; may you not spare an arrow,
\q2 for she has sinned against Yahweh.
\q1 \v 15 Shout against her all around!
\q2 She has given her hand;
\q1 her buttresses have fallen;
\q2 her walls have been torn down.
\q1 For it is the vengeance of Yahweh— take vengeance on her!
\q2 Just as she has done, do to her!
\q1 \v 16 Cut off the sower from Babylon,
\q2 and the one grasping the sickle at the time of harvest.
\q1 From the face of the oppressing sword
\q2 each man will turn to his people, and each man will flee to his land.
\q1 \v 17 Israel is a scattered sheep; lions have driven {him} away.
\q2 The first one, the king of Assyria, devoured him,
\q1 and this last one, Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon,
\q2 has broken his bones.”
\p
\v 18 Therefore thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel: “Behold me, visiting {punishment} upon the king of Babylon and upon his land, just as I visited {punishment} upon the king of Assyria.
\q1 \v 19 And I will bring Israel back to his pasture,
\q2 and he will graze on Carmel and Bashan;
\q1 and in the hill country of Ephraim and Gilead
\q2 his soul will be satisfied.
\q1 \v 20 In those days and in that time”— the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 “the iniquity of Israel will be sought, and there will be none,
\q1 and the sins of Judah, and they will not be found;
\q2 for I will forgive the ones whom I leave remaining.
\q1 \v 21 Against the land of Merathaim, go up against her,
\q2 and against the dwellers of Pekod.
\q1 Destroy and devote {them} to destruction after them”— the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 “and do according to all that I have commanded you.
\q1 \v 22 A sound of battle is in the land,
\q2 and a great breaking.
\q1 \v 23 How the hammer of all the earth
\q2 has been cut down and broken!
\q1 How Babylon has become a horror
\q2 among the nations!
\q1 \v 24 I laid a snare for you, and you were also captured, Babylon,
\q2 and you, you did not know.
\q1 You were found and also seized,
\q2 for you have contended against Yahweh.
\q1 \v 25 Yahweh has opened his storehouse
\q2 and has brought out the weapons of his indignation,
\q1 for it is a work for my Lord Yahweh of Armies
\q2 in the land of the Chaldeans.
\q1 \v 26 Come to her from the end; open her granaries;
\q2 pile her up like heaps, and devote her to destruction.
\q1 May there not be a remnant for her.
\q1 \v 27 Destroy all of her bulls; they will go down to the slaughter.
\q2 Woe to them, for their day has come,
\q1 the time of their visitation.
\q1 \v 28 A sound of the ones fleeing and of survivors from the land of Babylon,
\q2 to declare in Zion
\q1 the vengeance of Yahweh our God, the vengeance of his temple.
\q1 \v 29 Summon archers against Babylon!
\q2 All of the ones bending a bow, encamp against her all around.
\q1 May there not be an escape for her.
\q2 Repay her according to her deed;
\q1 according to all that she has done, do to her.
\q2 For she has acted presumptuously against Yahweh,
\q2 against the Holy One of Israel.
\q1 \v 30 Therefore her young men will fall in her open squares,
\q2 and all of the men of her war will be silenced in that day”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 31 “Behold me against you, presumption”— the declaration of the Lord Yahweh of Armies—
\q2 “for your day has come,
\q1 the time {when} I visit {punishment} upon you.
\q1 \v 32 And presumption will stumble and fall,
\q2 and there will be no one raising him up.
\q1 And I will kindle a fire in his cities,
\q2 and it will devour all of those around him.”
\q1 \v 33 Thus says Yahweh of Armies:
\q1 “The sons of Israel are oppressed, and the sons of Judah together,
\q2 and all of their captors have held them fast;
\q2 they have refused to send them away.
\q1 \v 34 Their Redeemer is strong; Yahweh of Armies is his name.
\q2 Contending he will contend their dispute,
\q1 in order to make the land be at rest
\q2 and to make the dwellers of Babylon tremble.
\q1 \v 35 A sword is against the Chaldeans”— the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 “and against the dwellers of Babylon,
\q2 and against her officials and against her wise ones.
\q1 \v 36 A sword is against the liars, and they will become fools.
\q2 A sword is against her warriors, and they will be dismayed.
\q1 \v 37 A sword is against his horses and against his chariot,
\q2 and against all of the mixed company that is in her midst,
\q1 and they will become like women.
\q2 A sword is against her storehouses, and they will be plundered.
\q1 \v 38 A drought is against her waters, and they will dry up.
\q2 For it is a land of carved images,
\q1 and by the terrors they act madly.
\q1 \v 39 Therefore desert beasts will dwell with hyenas,
\q2 and daughters of ostriches will dwell in her.
\q1 And she will not be inhabited again forever,
\q2 and she will not be dwelt in unto generation and generation.
\q1 \v 40 Like God overthrowing Sodom and Gomorrah and her neighbors”— the declaration of Yahweh—
\q2 “no man will dwell there,
\q2 and a son of man will not sojourn in her.
\q1 \v 41 Behold, a people is coming from the north,
\q2 and a great nation and many kings
\q2 are being stirred up from the remote parts of the earth.
\q1 \v 42 They grasp bow and curved sword;
\q2 they are cruel and will not have compassion.
\q1 Their voice roars like the sea,
\q2 and they ride on horses,
\q1 arrayed like a man for the battle
\q2 against you, daughter of Babylon.
\q1 \v 43 The king of Babylon has heard their report,
\q2 and his hands have become feeble.
\q1 Distress has seized him,
\q2 anguish like a woman giving birth.
\q1 \v 44 Behold, like a lion he will come up from the pride of the Jordan
\q2 to a pasture of a permanent stream,
\q1 so in a moment I will make them run from her.
\q2 And who is the chosen one {whom} I will appoint over her?
\q1 For who is like me, and who will summon me?
\q2 And who is this shepherd who will stand before my face?”
\q1 \v 45 Therefore hear the counsel of Yahweh
\q2 that he has counseled against Babylon,
\q1 and his plans that he has planned
\q2 against the land of the Chaldeans:
\q1 Surely they will drag them away, the little ones of the flock!
\q2 Surely he will make their pasture desolate over them!
\q1 \v 46 From the sound {of} Babylon {being} seized the earth has quaked,
\q2 and an outcry among the nations has been heard.
\c 51
\q1 \v 1 Thus says Yahweh:
\q1 “Behold me, arousing against Babylon
\q2 and against the dwellers of Leb Kamai
\q2 a destroying wind.
\q1 \v 2 And I will send strangers to Babylon,
\q2 and they will winnow her and will empty her land,
\q1 for they will be against her from all around
\q2 in the day of evil.
\q1 \v 3 Against let him tread, let him tread, the one treading his bow,
\q2 and against let him rise up in his body armor,
\q1 but do not have pity on her young men;
\q2 devote all of her army to destruction.
\q1 \v 4 And the slain will fall in the land of the Chaldeans,
\q2 and the pierced ones in her streets.”
\q1 \v 5 For Israel is not widowed, nor Judah, from his God,
\q2 from Yahweh of Armies,
\q1 though their land is full of guilt
\q2 from the Holy One of Israel.
\q1 \v 6 Flee from the midst of Babylon,
\q2 and each of you save his life!
\q1 Do not be destroyed in her iniquity,
\q2 for the time of vengeance it {is} for Yahweh;
\q2 it {is}a recompense he is repaying to her.
\q1 \v 7 Babylon {was} a cup of gold in the hand of Yahweh,
\q2 making all the earth drunk.
\q1 From her wine the nations drank;
\q2 therefore the nations are acting madly.
\q1 \v 8 Suddenly Babylon has fallen and has been broken.
\q2 Wail over her!
\q1 Take balm for her pain;
\q2 perhaps she will be healed.
\q1 \v 9 “We healed Babylon, but she was not healed.
\q2 Abandon her, and let us go, each man to his land,
\q1 for her judgment has reached to the heavens
\q2 and has been lifted up to the clouds.
\q1 \v 10 Yahweh has brought out our righteousnesses.
\q2 Come, and let us recount in Zion the work of Yahweh our God.”
\q1 \v 11 Sharpen the arrows! Fill the quivers!
\q2 Yahweh has aroused the spirit of the kings of Media,
\q1 for his plan against Babylon {is} to destroy her,
\q2 for it {is}
\q2 the vengeance of Yahweh, the vengeance of his temple.
\q1 \v 12 Against the walls of Babylon lift up a banner!
\q2 Strengthen the watch! Raise up watchers!
\q2 Prepare the ambushers!
\q1 For Yahweh has both planned and also done
\q2 what he spoke against the dwellers of Babylon.
\q1 \v 13 Dweller upon many waters, abundant in treasures,
\q2 your end has come, the cubit of your severing.
\q1 \v 14 Yahweh of Armies has sworn by his life:
\q1 “Surely I will fill you {with} mankind like the locust,
\q2 and they will raise a shout over you.”
\q1 \v 15 {The one} making the earth by his power,
\q2 establishing the world by his wisdom—
\q2 and by his understanding he stretched out the heavens.
\q1 \v 16 At the sound of his giving, there is a tumult of waters in the heavens,
\q2 and he makes clouds go up from the end of the earth.
\q1 He makes lightnings for the rain,
\q2 and he brings out wind from his storehouses.
\q1 \v 17 Every man is brutish, without knowledge.
\q2 Every goldsmith has come to shame because of his carved image,
\q1 for his cast image {is} a lie,
\q2 and {there is} no breath in them.
\q1 \v 18 They {are} vapor, a work of mockeries.
\q2 In the time of their visitation they will perish.
\q1 \v 19 The portion of Jacob is not like these,
\q2 for he {is} the one forming all things,
\q2 and the tribe of his inheritance.
\q1 Yahweh of Armies is his name.
\q1 \v 20 “You {are} a shatterer for me, weapons of war,
\q2 and with you I have shattered nations,
\q2 and with you I have destroyed kingdoms.
\q1 \v 21 And with you I have shattered horse and his rider,
\q2 and with you I have shattered chariot and his rider.
\q1 \v 22 And with you I have shattered man and woman,
\q2 and with you I have shattered old man and youth,
\q2 and with you I have shattered young man and virgin.
\q1 \v 23 And with you I have shattered shepherd and his flock,
\q2 and with you I have shattered plowman and his team,
\q2 and with you I have shattered governors and prefects.
\q1 \v 24 And I will repay to Babylon and to all the dwellers of Chaldea all their evil that they did in Zion to your eyes”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 25 “Behold me, against you, mountain of destruction”—the declaration of Yahweh—“the one destroying all the earth.
\q2 And I will stretch out my hand against you,
\q2 and I will roll you from the cliffs,
\q2 and I will make you into a mountain of burning.
\q1 \v 26 And they will not take from you a stone for a corner
\q2 or a stone for foundations,
\q1 for you will be desolations of perpetuity”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 27 Lift up a banner in the land!
\q2 Blow a shofar among the nations!
\q1 Consecrate nations against her!
\q2 Make the kingdoms of Ararat, Minni, and Ashkenaz hear {the call} against her!
\q1 Appoint a marshal against her!
\q2 Bring up horses like the bristling locust!
\q1 \v 28 Consecrate nations against her:
\q2 the kings of Media, her governors and all her prefects,
\q2 and all the land of his dominion.
\q1 \v 29 And the land quaked and writhed,
\q2 for the plans of Yahweh have stood against Babylon,
\q1 to make the land of Babylon a desolation,
\q2 without a dweller.
\q1 \v 30 The warriors of Babylon have ceased to fight;
\q2 they have remained in the strongholds.
\q1 Their might has dried up; they have become as women.
\q2 They have set her dwellings on fire; her bars have been broken.
\q1 \v 31 A runner runs to meet a runner,
\q2 and an announcer to meet an announcer,
\q1 to announce to the king of Babylon
\q2 that his city has been captured from end {to end},
\q1 \v 32 and the fords have been seized,
\q2 and the marshes they have burned with fire,
\q2 and the men of war are terrified.
\q1 \v 33 For thus says Yahweh of Armies, the God of Israel:
\q1 “Daughter Babylon is like a threshing floor
\q2 at the time of treading her.
\q1 Yet a little while,
\q2 and the time of the harvest will come for her.”
\q1 \v 34 “Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon has devoured us; he has sucked us dry;
\q2 he has set us down—an empty vessel.
\q1 He has swallowed us like the sea monster;
\q2 he has filled his belly with my delicacies;
\q2 he has rinsed us out.”
\q1 \v 35 “My violence and my flesh {be} upon Babylon,”
\q2 the dweller of Zion says.
\q1 “And my blood {be} upon the dwellers of Chaldea,”
\q2 says Jerusalem.
\q1 \v 36 Therefore thus says Yahweh:
\q1 “Behold me, contending your contention,
\q2 and I will avenge your vengeance,
\q1 and I will dry up her sea,
\q2 and I will make her fountain dry.
\q1 \v 37 And Babylon will become heaps,
\q2 a habitation of jackals,
\q2 a desolation and a hissing, without a dweller.
\q1 \v 38 Together like the young lions they roar;
\q2 they growl like the cubs of lions.
\q1 \v 39 When they grow hot, I will set out their feast,
\q2 and I will make them drunk, so that they may exult
\q1 and sleep a sleep of perpetuity
\q2 and not wake up”—the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 40 “I will bring them down like lambs to the slaughter,
\q2 like rams with male goats.”
\q1 \v 41 How Sheshach has been captured,
\q2 and the praise of all the earth has been seized!
\q1 How Babylon has become a desolation
\q2 among the nations!
\q1 \v 42 The sea has come up over Babylon;
\q2 she has been covered with the tumult of its waves.
\q1 \v 43 Her cities have become a desolation,
\q2 a land of dryness and a desert plain,
\q1 a land in which no man dwells,
\q2 and a son of man does not pass through them.
\q1 \v 44 “And I will visit upon Bel in Babylon,
\q2 and I will bring out from his mouth what he swallowed,
\q1 and the nations will not stream to him anymore.
\q2 Even the wall of Babylon has fallen.
\q1 \v 45 Go out from her midst, my people,
\q2 and let each man save his life
\q2 from the burning of the nose of Yahweh!
\q1 \v 46 And lest your heart grow soft
\q2 and you fear at the report that is heard in the land—
\q1 and the report will come in the {one} year,
\q2 and after it in the {next} year the report,
\q1 and violence in the land,
\q2 and ruler against ruler.
\q1 \v 47 Therefore, behold, days are coming
\q2 when I will visit upon the carved images of Babylon,
\q1 and all her land will have shame,
\q2 and all her slain will fall in her midst.
\q1 \v 48 And the heavens and the earth and all that is in them
\q2 will shout for joy over Babylon,
\q1 for from the north the destroyers will come to her”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 49 Also Babylon {is} to fall for the slain of Israel,
\q2 as also for Babylon the slain of all the earth have fallen.
\q1 \v 50 Fugitives from the sword, go! May you not stand still!
\q2 Remember Yahweh from afar,
\q2 and may Jerusalem come up on your heart.
\q1 \v 51 “We have shame, for we have heard reproach;
\q2 disgrace has covered our faces,
\q1 for strangers have come
\q2 against the holy places of the house of Yahweh.”
\q1 \v 52 “Therefore, behold, days are coming”— the declaration of Yahweh— “when I will visit upon her carved images,
\q2 and in all her land the slain will groan.
\q1 \v 53 Though Babylon should go up to the heavens,
\q2 and though she should fortify the height of her strength,
\q1 from me destroyers will come to her”— the declaration of Yahweh.
\q1 \v 54 A sound of an outcry from Babylon,
\q2 and a great breaking from the land of the Chaldeans!
\q1 \v 55 For Yahweh is devastating Babylon,
\q2 and he will destroy from her the great voice.
\q1 And their waves roar like many waters;
\q2 the roaring of their voice is given out.
\q1 \v 56 For a destroyer has come against her, against Babylon,
\q2 and her warriors have been captured;
\q2 their bows have shattered.
\q1 For Yahweh is a God of recompenses;
\q2 repaying he will repay.
\q1 \v 57 “And I will make her officials and her wise men,
\q2 her governors and her prefects and her warriors drunk,
\q1 and they will sleep a sleep of perpetuity
\q2 and not wake up”— the declaration of the King; Yahweh of Armies is his name.
\q1 \v 58 Thus says Yahweh of Armies:
\q1 “The broad wall of Babylon— stripping bare, it will be stripped bare,
\q2 and her high gates will burn with fire.
\q1 And the peoples will labor for emptiness,
\q2 and the nations for fire,
\q2 and they will grow weary.”
\p
\v 59 The word that Jeremiah the prophet commanded Seraiah son of Neriah, son of Mahseiah, when he went with Zedekiah king of Judah to Babylon in the fourth year of his reign. Now Seraiah was the officer of the resting place.
\v 60 And Jeremiah wrote all the evil that would come to Babylon in one scroll, all these words that were written concerning Babylon.
\p
\v 61 And Jeremiah said to Seraiah, “When you come to Babylon, then you will see and you will read all these words,
\v 62 and you will say, ‘Yahweh, you yourself have spoken concerning this place, to cut it off, so that there is no dweller in it, from man even to beast, for it will be desolations of perpetuity.’
\v 63 And it will happen, when you have finished reading this scroll, you will tie a stone to it and throw it into the midst of the Euphrates,
\v 64 and you will say, ‘Thus Babylon will sink and will not rise, from the face of the evil that I am bringing upon her, and they will grow weary.’” Until here {are} the words of Jeremiah.
\c 52
\p
\v 1 Zedekiah {was} a son of 21 years when he reigned, and he reigned 11 years in Jerusalem. And the name of his mother {was} Hamutal, the daughter of Jeremiah, from Libnah.
\v 2 And he did evil in the eyes of Yahweh, like all that Jehoiakim had done.
\v 3 For because of the nose of Yahweh it was against Jerusalem and Judah, until he cast them away from his face. And Zedekiah rebelled against the king of Babylon.
\v 4 And it happened in the ninth year of his reigning, in the tenth month, on ten of the month, Nebuchadnezzar, the king of Babylon, came, he and all of his army, against Jerusalem. And he encamped against it, and they built a siege mound against it all around.
\v 5 And the city entered into siege until the eleventh year of King Zedekiah.
\v 6 In the fourth month, on nine of the month, then famine seized the city, and {there} was no bread for the people of the land.
\v 7 And the city was breached, and all of the men of war fled and went out from the city at night the way of the gate between the two walls that {were} near the garden of the king (now the Chaldeans {were} against the city all around), and they went the way of the Arabah.
\v 8 But the army of the Chaldeans pursued after the king, and they overtook Zedekiah in the plains of Jericho. And all of his army were scattered away from him.
\v 9 And they captured the king, and they brought him up to the king of Babylon, {to} Riblah in the land of Hamath. And they pronounced judgment on him.
\v 10 And the king of Babylon slaughtered the sons of Zedekiah to his eyes, and he also slaughtered all of the leaders of Judah at Riblah.
\v 11 And he blinded the eyes of Zedekiah, and he bound him with two bronze chains, and the king of Babylon brought him to Babylon, and he put him in a house of oversight until the day of his death.
\p
\v 12 And in the fifth month, on seven of the month, it {was} the year of 19, the year to King Nebuchadnezzar, the king of Babylon, Nebuzaradan, the chief of the guards, he stood to the face of the king of Babylon, came to Jerusalem.
\v 13 And he burned the house of Yahweh and the house of the king. And all of the houses of Jerusalem and every house of the great, he burned with fire.
\v 14 And all of the walls of Jerusalem all around, all of the army of the Chaldeans who {were} with the chief of the guards tore down.
\v 15 And the poor of the people and the rest of the people left in the city and the ones having fallen, who had fallen to the king of Babylon, and the rest of the multitude, Nebuzaradan, the chief of the guards, exiled.
\v 16 But Nebuzaradan, the chief of the guards, let some of the poor of the land remain as vinedressers and as farmers.
\p
\v 17 And the Chaldeans broke up the pillars of bronze that {were} to the house of Yahweh and the stands and the sea of bronze that {were} in the house of Yahweh, and they carried all of their bronze to Babylon.
\v 18 And they took away the pots and the shovels and the trimmers and the bowls and the palms and all of the articles of bronze with which they served.
\v 19 And the basins and the firepans and the bowls and the pots and the lampstands and the palms and the cups that {were} gold gold and that {were} silver silver, the chief of the guards took away.
\v 20 The two pillars, the one sea, and the twelve bronze bulls that {were} under {it} {and} the stands, which King Solomon had made for the house of Yahweh, a weight was not for the bronze of all of these vessels.
\v 21 And the pillars, 18 cubits {was} the height of one pillar, and a line of 12 cubits went around {it}. And its thickness {was} four fingers. {It was} hollow.
\v 22 And the capital upon it {was} bronze, and the height of one capital {was} five cubits. And a network and pomegranates {were} upon the capital all around. The whole {was} bronze. And like these for the second pillar and the pomegranates.
\v 23 And 96 pomegranates were to the wind. All of the pomegranates {were} 100 upon the network all around.
\p
\v 24 And the chief of the guards took Seraiah, the head priest, and Zephaniah, the priest of the second rank, and the three guards of the threshold.
\v 25 And from the city he took a eunuch because he {was} the overseer over the men of war and seven men from the seers of the face of the king who were found in the city and the scribe, the commander of the army, the one mustering the people of the land, and 60 men from the people of the land found in the midst of the city.
\v 26 And Nebuzaradan, the chief of the guards, took them and made them go to the king of Babylon, to Riblah.
\v 27 And the king of Babylon struck them and killed them in Riblah in the land of Hamath. And Judah went into exile away from its land.
\p
\v 28 These {are} the people whom Nebuchadnezzar exiled. In the year of seven, 3,023 Judeans.
\v 29 In the year of 18 to Nebuchadnezzar, 832 soul from Jerusalem.
\v 30 In the year of 23 to Nebuchadnezzar, Nebuzaradan, the captain of the guards, exiled 745 soul, Judeans. All of the soul {was} 4,600.
\p
\v 31 And it happened in year 37 of the exile of Jehoiachin, the king of Judah, in month 12, on 25 of the month, Evil Merodach, the king of Babylon, in the year of his reigning, lifted the head of Jehoiachin, the king of Judah, and he brought him out from the house of imprisonment.
\v 32 And he spoke good {things} to him, and he put his seat from above to the seat of the kings who {were} with him in Babylon.
\v 33 And he changed the clothes of his imprisonment, and he ate bread regularly to his face all of the days of his life.
\v 34 And his allowance, a continual allowance, was given to him from the king of Babylon, the matter of a day in its day until the day of his death, all of the days of his life. 